In article <6utt7m$f7v$1@hecate.umd.edu> bleau@umdsp.umd.edu writes:
> The SCSI adapters are Qlogic 1040B UltraSCSI (whatever *that* means: 
> marketing hype or new standard?).  

  It's a standard referrent to the speed of the bus.  SCSI busses
come in two flavors of width (Narrow and Wide) and at least three
flavors of bus speed (Slow, Fast, Ultrafast).

Peak data throughput for the different combinations I've seen are:
Slow/Narrow  -  5MB/s
Fast/Narrow  - 10MB/s
Fast/Wide    - 20MB/s
Ultra/Narrow - 20MB/s
Ultra/Wide   - 40MB/s

  There's a new faster speed that comes after "Ultra", but I'm not
sure what it's being called.

  For more detailed information on SCSI, you can find the SCSI FAQ
at http://www.cis.ohio-state.edu/hypertext/faq/usenet/scsi-faq/top.html

  -- TTK




