There are a couple of free projects kicking around to untangle and display
Word docs on *ix systems, mostly they try and convert them into HTML. 
Announcements pop up every once in a while in comp.os.linux.announce.  I
can't point you at them, I didn't save the links.

If this is really important to you (enough for you to spend money on)
then your best bet is to go look at some office packages.  For example,
Applixware.  Their wordprocessor app can handle the following formats,
and they sell a Digital UNIX version:

(snipped from their web pages)

Import                             Export
======                             ======
  Applixware 3.0/3.11              Applix Words 3.x 
  ASCII                            ASCII 
  DCA                              Frame MIF 2.0, 4.0 and 5.0*
  Frame MIF 4.0 and 5.0*           Interleaf ASCII 4.0*
  Interleaf ASCII 4.0 and          MS Word for DOS* 
     5.0*                          MS Word for Windows (2.0,6.0)*
  MS Word for DOS*                 RTF* 
  MS Word for Windows              Word Perfect 4.2,5.0,5.1,6.0*
     (2.0, 6.0)*                   HTML
  MS Word for Macintosh
     5.1* 
  OfficeWriter* 
  RTF* 
  Word Perfect 4.2, 5.0,
     5.1, 6.0* 
  HTML 


Alternatively, you could try Corel WordPerfect for UNIX 7.  I guess
that might have a smaller memory footprint (could be cheeper?) and
they also have a DU version.  There may be others too.

