In article <907151002.10076.0.nnrp-01.9e98695e@news.demon.co.uk>, "Damian Draper" <DDraper AT caluk DOT com> writes:
:Here I go again... I need to pacify a customer asking 'Are DEC 2000 AXP
:systems Y2K compliant?'
:I would say yes, with correct firmware/software upgrades, but I was
:searching on the DEC sites for any Y2K info I could show them and couldn't
:find *anything* on this system regarding Y2K at all...
:
:could someome please point the way to the light...

  As a rule, I try not to answer Y2K questions, because these questions
  quickly breed into torrents of Y2K questions...

  I do point folks at the Y2K website, which is where the answers are, 
  and where questions on products that are not listed or that are not 
  obviously located in the database can be posed.

    http://ww1.digital.com/year2000/e_home.html

  To quote some text from the above webpage:

    "If you cannot find your product in the database or spreadsheet, use 
    the feedback form to send your request electronically to Y2K Help."

  The feedback form embedded in the above text will get you to the folks
  specifically handling the torrents of Y2K questions.

  And in general, I would recommend Y2K testing.  You can have an entirely
  Y2K-ready environment based solely on Y2K non-compliant products, and you
  can also have a Y2K non-compliant environment that is based entirely on 
  products that are Y2K-ready.  In other words, the only way you can tell
  for sure what Y2K problems you might have at your site is to test...

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
