In article <3611503F.79009976@montagar.com>, 
"David L. Cathey" <davidc@montagar.com> writes:

> With VMS, I can access the system with DECnet, TCP/IP,
> LAT, SMI, or the serial console.  I've used my PalmPilot (VT100
> emulator) as a console to configure and boot one of my Alpha's
> in a pinch.  You simply just can't do that on WNT.

If you have the KCRCM option installed on your AlphaServer, you
can do a lot more.   With it, you can dial in and do anything
(except change media) that you could do at the machine:

  Halt the CPU
  Reset the CPU
  Turn power off
  Turn power back on
  Re-boot the system
  Communicate with the serial console port
  Check the internal temperature and status of each power supply, fan, etc.

It will even dial your pager if there is a power failure.   Of
course, all this is password protected.   For $350, it is a cheap
way to avoid trips to a remote site.   I'm surprised that this
option hasn't been more heavily promoted.

