I'm looking closely at the new Compaq Digital Personal Workstations (-au series
running OpenVMS 7.1-1H2).  The SCSI adapters are Qlogic 1040B UltraSCSI
(whatever *that* means: marketing hype or new standard?).  We have a lot of
disks on another system that we are thinking of moving to the newer system when
we get it.  The older system runs only normal speed (dare I say "old"?) SCSI-2. 
The disks are modern enough to know about fast SCSI, though.  As I recall, fast
vs. slow is negotiated between the two devices.

The rub is the max SCSI cable length.  On the older system it was 3 meters, and
we could push the envelope somewhat.  We have a lot of external disks with
1.5ft cables between them.  Here's my concern: If I move these to the new
system the disks will automatically handshake with the adapter and go into fast
SCSI mode.  How badly will this impact me if I keep the same cable lengths?

Someone at DEC said the max cable length for UltraSCSI is 1 meter, but he
wasn't sure.  Can anyone confirm that?  How "hard" is the limit?  (I know, that
sounds like a dumb question, but my boss is convinced that "these standards
folks" always build in some slack and therefore, unless documented otherwise,
we can always use longer lengths, maybe 10-20% longer.)

Is there a way to modify the Qlogic board's behavior to allow us to use longer
cable lengths reliably?

Thanks, all.

Lawrence Bleau
University of Maryland
Physics Dept., Space Physics Group
