You must have a base license for Alpha VMS or DU.  I don't
believe there is a full NT ASAP program, they recomment you
go the MS Solution Provider or MS Direct Access route, but
ASAP does discount the DEQ NT tools to go along with the MS
program (can't really comment, we haven't tried the DEQ NT
stuff yet).  To the best of my knowledge there is no ASAP
for VAX VMS but I might be wrong.  Also, I believe you must
be a software developer to qualify for any ASAP program.

A side benefit is ASAP partners also get hardware discounts
for development or demo purposes.  I think we got a 500au
for a pretty good price through ASAP (not entirely sure, I
don't do the purchase orders), but I wouldn't recommend
their specials, usually overpriced and underpowered old DEC
PC servers (anyone really want a Pentium Pro 150 server?).


One caveat....the ASAP Layered Product license PAKs are only
good for one year.  You have to renew each year, and
obsolete products are not carried forward.  If DEQ stops
selling a product you don't get it anymore, and your
existing PAK expires.
