I'm always a bit skeptical  that Cutler had much
influence on NT as a whole,  but ...

Mark Russinovich, http://www.sysinternals.com used to have
on his Web site, a  program that would exercise native NT
system calls, calling  them w/ random  bogus parameters,  
bad addresses, bad handles, and the like.

He found about 200+ BlueScreen holes this way
(since then,  fixed  in one of the various service packs)

He wrote a later version, that foucused on various
virtual address boundary cases, rather than randomly,
that found still more.

The interesting bit, though,  was that MR tried to
repeat the exercise, against kernel routines, but had no luck.

Supposedly, the inner kernel  was much more robust than
the outer-lying  layers.

That the outer layers of NT, the native API, Win32, MFC, 
came to be so bad,  i think is  typical MSFT, 
good intentions,  flawed execution ...




