"Guillaume SCHAER" <gschaer@usa.net> wrote:
>Hi,
>a friend of mine gave me his old DECpc 325SL who's not working anymore (CPU
>and motherboard dead..) and I'd like to use the old screen (Toshiba
>TLX-5152S-C3M1) on my own computer.
>    So I opened the notebook to take the screen away, but I have a few
>problem when trying to connecting the new screen to my video card (Matrox
>Millenium II). I'd like to know exactly how to do this.

It's not going to work.  The notebook has a video chip designed to
drive the digital inputs of the laptop screen.  The Matrox card is
only designed to drive the analog inputs of a regular monitor.

You're only possible hope is to find a video card that's designed
specifically to drive a raw laptop screen.

There are some new video cards coming out that are designed to
support the upcoming digital video standard, but even these will
likely not hook up directly to a raw laptop screen without some
support circuitry.




