Um, there's MVS, VMS, the (ugh) excuses for operating systems from Redmond,
BEos, OS/390, QNX, RT11 (yes they STILL are selling copies!) and so
on and so forth. 


>It's really a shame that the last pure OS has to decided to go UNIX.
>When MacOS X is released there will be literally no mainstream OS
>which is not UNIX.  ;-(   
>
>I'm beginning to think this atrocity is NEVER going to die, but
>hopefully 2038 will provide some fuel to transition to something else
>(assuming somebody builds an OS which is not UNIX, by then...but 
>considering that all college OS textbooks seem to think "UNIX"
>is synonymous with "OS", I'm beginning to doubt this is possible).


so i suppose you'd prefr the world runs some sick micro$oft product?
Peronsally there if very little i'dd ever change from 4.3BSD. Its pretty
close to ideal in my opinion. Also, there is so much diversity in the UNIX
thatit is hardly correct to consider them all the same thing or even
cover them under the blanket of 'UNIX' without knowing the differences. 
Take the NEXT system fr example- it has a lot of UNIXish things in it, 
but can you really call it UNIX? how about OS/2? it has a lot of SysV internals
but again, i ont think too many people would even think of calling it
UNIX. (because of the sysV derived internals, i left OS2 off of my
list above) If you look at, say, Seventh Edition, and then, say, look at 
DG/UX, you cant seriously call them the same OS. Theres a world of ddifference,
but youll still somehow be able to trace a lineage of one to the other. 
The very fact that the other 'mainstream' OS's out there only exist in a couple
of revisions generally following a linear development and update stream, 
and that UNIX on the other hand has spread and diversified to the point
where youve got somethign like Digital UNIX on one hand and Irix on the
oher and True Berkeley on the other and AIX on the other, none of which
share too much in common, all of which are currently being developed
and currently ship, the very fact that UNIX could diversify like that, 
is an incredidble tribute to the work by the likes of DMR and BWK and KT and
the other early UNIX gods. All the other OS's you coudld probably htink of 
might run on one, or two, or _maybe_ three platforms. UNIX runs on just 
about any machine that's been commercially produced in the past twenty years, 
and no small number of ones that werent. Yes, they all trace their lineage
to the Fifth Research Edition, but tey are incredibly diverse. You can hardly
claim that theyre the same thing anymore. So to see a new UNIX-derived
system is hardly a single expanding Borg, and much more yet a new colour in
the already extremely varied and colorful UNIX world. It's a lot healthier
and a lot more interesting than the ddull grey of, say, the wintel world. 

Yes, there are single flavors of UNIX which span vast number of platforms
(NetBSD almost takes this to an extreme, not at all a bad thing!), and
flavors such as Linux are very popular in new installations these days, 
as are all of the free UNIXen, but if you look at the sheer breadth of
UNIX development, pushing in all directions, you will gain a new respect for
the simplicity of the original. 

happy hacking,
gregg



