Walter Roberson <roberson@ibd.nrc.ca> wrote:
>Twenty years is a bit of an exaggeration. The Motorola 68000
>wasn't introduced until 1979; the 68020 (first Motorola with built
>in MMU) was not until 1984. You can't have Unix without hardware
>memory protection,

You can too. The TRS-80 model 16, and one of the Altos PCs both ran real AT&T
derived UNIX on 68000s. The TRS-80 had a simple MMU with a base and bound
register pair. The Altos ran two 68000s one instruction apart so it could
back out of page faults. I am running Real System V UNIX on an AT&T UNIX-PC
with a 68010 CPU.

All the lack of an MMU in the 68000 means is that you have to cons one up
yourself.

>so in the years leading up to about '85, UNIX-like
>systems that lacked hardware memory protection tended to be *very*
>careful not to call themselves UNIX.

They didn't call themselves UNIX because AT&T didn't let anyone but USG call
their product UNIX, for trademark protection reasons. That's why you had Irix,
and AIX, and so on even though they were complete UNIXes with hardware MMUs.

>20 years ago was about the introduction
>of the Z80, the age of commercial CPM systems -- not quite advanced
>enough to port UNIX to.

Cromemco and Whitsemiths both did it. There was even a CP/M based UNIX
clone called Marc that died with its developer... if it'd survived then
the Linux phenomenon might have happened in the '80s. It almost did with
Minix, except Andy wasn't quite forward-looking enough to hold onto
distribution rights.

