In article <6un7lm$77h$1@canopus.cc.umanitoba.ca>, roberson@ibd.nrc.ca (Walter Roberson) writes:
|> In article <6un15n$7ta$1@usenet01.srv.cis.pitt.edu>,
|> Gregg E Economou <geest3+@pitt.edu> wrote:
|> :UNIX runs on just 
|> :about any machine that's been commercially produced in the past twenty years, 
|> :and no small number of ones that werent.
|> 
|> Twenty years is a bit of an exaggeration. The Motorola 68000
|> wasn't introduced until 1979; the 68020 (first Motorola with built
|> in MMU) was not until 1984.

Not quite.  The ' 020 didn't have an integral MMU; it supported an
external paged MMU (68851 PMMU).  The Mac II shipped with a null MMU
in the socket; adding the PMMU slowed down memory refs by ~1 cycle.
'010 also supported instruction-continuation after faults, and
supported a segmented MMU, and of course the earliest with a real PMMU
was the '030.  (1979 sounds awfully early for the 68k; is that right?
When was first shipment?)


|> You  can't have Unix without hardware
|> memory protection, 


Of course you can, its just not protected.  Even base/bounds lets you
add protection.  I forget excatly what the PDP-11/10 with Mini-Unix
did; it ceratinly didn't have relocation. User programs were swapped,
and all linked to start at the same address, and if you grew the
kernel too far, you had to relink _everything_.  Or the single-user
UNIX used on LSI-11s (in the BSTJ special issue, july/august 1978).


|> so in the years leading up to about '85, UNIX-like
|> systems that lacked hardware memory protection tended to be *very*
|> careful not to call themselves UNIX. 

They couldn't call themselves UNIX for legal reasons.  Hence names
like Minix, and PC/IX, and whatever Whitesmiths and MKS(?) called
their unix clones.  Heck, Minix might even have kept the Linux niche,
if Addison-Wesley hadn't been so sticky about redistribution and
updates.  Well, that and the darn ACK.


|> 20 years ago was about the introduction
|> of the Z80, the age of commercial CPM systems -- not quite advanced
|> enough to port UNIX to.

The add-on protection hardware for the 8086 and System 3/System
V/Xenix/whatever-it-was was discussed here last month.  ISTR a similar
frob for the Z80, but not the details.



