In article <6uo0gd$hn2@info.telenor.no>, "Robert Danielsen" <rid@ieee.org> writes:
|>I am having trouble with a backup made with TK70. A hard disk has crashed,
|>and I need to restore. The backup runs for over an hour, and seems to write
|>lots of files, before it stops with a tape error message. Then when I mount
|>the disk, it finds nothing. It seems that an image backup cannot be partly
|>written to the disk (which makes sense, since it is an "image" of the disk).

The OpenVMS developers went to a fair amount of trouble to ensure that
error messages could be cleanly, concisely and consistently reported with
only a small amount of effort on the part of the individual programmers.
Unfortunately they had no control over the last link in the chain, which -
as has happened now - often breaks ... It may well be that your backup
is irretrievable, but if you don't post the exact error message text we'll
never know ...

|>So I went back to a very old image backup and restored that one OK.

This suggests that your tape hardware is OK.

|>Is there any way of extracting individual files from an image backup?

Instead of
	$ BACKUP/IMAGE/VERIFY device:saveset  target:

Try
	$BACKUP/VERIFY/SELECT=(files-you-want) device:saveset where-you-want-em

