In article <1998Sep25.140237.1368@flying-disk.spamblock.com>, frisbie@flying-disk.spamblock.com (Alan Frisbie) writes:

>Is there such a thing as a diagnostic floppy, CDROM or tape
>for Alphas?   What does Field Service use, if anything?

Hopefully, someone closer to field service can give you a more
complete answer.

In my experience, I think I've heard of diagnostics, but I've never
seen any: and I've never seen the field service people who work on
our internal systems use any (at least not on CPUs or any of the
peripherals on any system I have: there may be some for the more
'high end' systems, or for some of the more mechanically complicated
peripherals such as Mag Tape with loaders or the optical disk
libraries, but I don't have any of these in my department).

I believe there are a number of reasons for this.

First, the consoles for the Alpha systems are much more sophisticated
than for earlier systems (RAM and EPROM are much less expensive than
they were in the past, you you can put more on a system), and the
method of updating the console from CD-ROM is much more versitile
than similar systems were in the past.  So there are many more
diagnostic tests built into the hardware now than there were before.
I've even seen evolution in this area, so that the newer Alphas seem
to have more tests built in than, say, the DEC 3000 did.

You can say "TEST" at the console prompt, and the system will run
a reasonable set of diagnostics.

There also seem to be more diagnostics built into devices than there
were in the past, again because CPU and memory are becomming much less
expensive.

I think another reason why there aren't as many diagnostics is because
there aren't as many replaceable units as there used to be, and it's
less likely that one component will fail in a way that will cause the
system to keep working and not know something is wrong.  Back when
a (for example) PDP-15 cpu was made up of 200 single-height single-
width logic modules (and that was JUST the CPU), you could have one
IC on one board fail and it would be important to be able to track
that one board down and replace it.  Later, you might have a PDP-11/70
with several boards for the CPU, several more for the memory controller,
more for the I/O bus controllers, etc., and you had to track down to
the board that failed.  This was especially true if you had a system
where a component in the Floating Point processor failed so that
the processor continued to 'work' but returned bad values.

Now you usually have the CPU (including all of the Floating Point)
and most of the Memory and some I/O control all in one integrated circuit.
If that circuit fails, the whole system stops.  And to replace it,
you might pop out the processor, but more likely you have to exchange
the entire mother board.  If your SCSI controller stops working, you
pull the entire controller (which is now one board) and replace it.
You generally don't try to fix it in the field, because it isn't
cost effective.  So there isn't the same need for 'diagnostics' that
there once was.

Although I sometimes feel nostalgic for some of those tests, I think
it's good that the industry, or at least Alpha systems, have progressed
to the point where there is no longer a need for that kind of diagnostic
testing anymore.

Now if someone would create the equivalent of XXDP+ for Intel-based
PC systems, that would be useful, because there are too many hardware
incompatibilities that one runs into in that area, and there aren't
as many good self-tests built into those kinds of systems (at least
as far as I've seen).  But that's also an entirely different subject.

(I'm sure Alan knows all of this, but I figure other people are going
to read this article, so I typed it all in.)

-- 
