>>	"NEVER need a reboot"... They all need to be rebooted or they
>>slowly grind to a halt over time.  "/tmp" starts to fill, you get a
[...]
>>	I'll never understand some people's aversion to something as simple
>>as a reboot.  Shit; if the user has gone home for the day and I need

>It's just a very ignorant way to do things. Acceptance of ignorant
>methods breeds acceptance of ignorance in general- acceptance of ignorance
>breeds mediocrity and ultimately hurts us all. 
>Please dont contibute to this, the folks in redmond and all their smiling
>ignorant customers are contributing to this enough!

I happen to agree with the latter comment, but the inescapable fact is
that most humans are lazy and complacent, and that this trend developed
in the UNIX community before Microsoft abandoned XENIX as their flagship
OS.  It developed simply because it became less difficult to reboot than
to diagnose what the problem was and fix it.  It also happened because
marketers started happily selling computers to people who had no idea
how to support them, all the while convincing them that they didn't need
to purchase supported products from their competitors at much greater
cost.

We live in a prepackaged, disposable, microwavable society in which most
people would rather die than to invest any time or effort in learning to
understand anything or to do something well, so long as they can make
money.

The sea change is evident in the wording of Tom West's oft-quoted saying
that "Not everything worth doing is worth doing well."  Now, also as an
experienced manager, I'd say that perhaps what he *meant* to say was:
"Everything worth doing is worth doing well.  Not everything, however,
is worth doing to perfection."  Unfortunately, the former version seems
to be the driving force behind current "thinking," or lack thereof.

Frankly, there are times when, under pressure of business requirements,
one may have to simply punt and reboot the system.

OTOH, if you have the experience and *understanding* (not just
"knowledge" or "training" -- what are we, dogs or people?) to fix the
problem without doing so, it is not only easier for you, but also easier
for your users.  Try rebooting a corporate business server in peak
working hours simply because some *&^%$ bonehead at ORACLE can't figure
how to write a proper signal handling routine or to somehow keep track
of processes dependent on a particular server instance and kill them
when it dies, thus leaving the SGA sitting in memory and preventing the
DBA from restarting the server because there isn't enough memory to
create two SGAs.  Suddenly being able to step through pregions becomes
very handy.

OR, suppose there's some infrequent race condition which is exercised by
your particular applications mix.  Want the OS or application maker to
fix it?  You'll have to tell them what the heck is wrong first.

Simply rebooting to "solve" problems is like lowering your visor to
block the headlights from oncoming cars when you're driving on the wrong
side of the road.

Sure, there are times when you have to reboot out of expedience, but if
you don't at least try to find out what the problem was and fix it,
you're just contributing to your own demise.

I don't know about everyone, but I don't like my paycheck to depend on
systems with problems which "just happen."

We have enough of those that we *can't* do anything about without
inviting more through complacency and indifference.

