>:>You are also going to have problems with any binaries that assume that
>:>time differences can be stored in 32 bits. Or even that time
>:>differences can be calculated at all (going to a 64 bit time_t does not
>:>imply that the system can do 64 bit arithmetic.) You might say that
>
>:Ibegyourscuseme?  If you're not an idiot, it does.  See "quad_t".  Making
>:programmers' lives hell in that fashion smacks of SCO, or NT, or something.
>
>I checked both my ANSI C and POSIX.1 manuals. They contain
>no reference at all to 'quad_t'. 

And did I say that they would?

>I have also checked the include files for IRIX 6.2 and IRIX 6.4
>with the complete SGI "7.2" compiler release installed. I find no
>definition and no references to quad_t.
>
>In my opinion, you should either post an exact section number
>reference to the ANSI C definition of quad_t, or you should post
>an apology.

Your opinion is silly.

>There is nothing in ANSI C or POSIX.1 that requires that 64 bit
>arithmetic be supported. ANSI C does require that time_t be an
>arithmetic type, so any compliant system that does not support 64
>bit arithmetic cannot have a 64 bit time_t. (The requirement that
>it be an arithmetic type eliminates structures or arrays
>as your time_t.)

Yes, I understand this.

The point is that your underlying hardware architecture doesn't have to
be able to do 64-bit arithmetic in single instructions in order for
your operating system to provide 64-bit arithmetic types.  Expanding
operating system data types to 64 bits without providing the facility
to do arithmetic on them... is idiocy.

In practical terms, you'd have to be looney -- or maybe work for SCO or
Microsoft or someone like that, which is about the same -- to go stretching
the contents of sys/types.h to 64 bits without at least compiler support
for 64 bit arithmetic.  Which is there in at least one compiler (GCC) for
just about every 32-bit architecture.

In 4.4BSD, the basic 64-bit data type happens to be called "quad_t", and if
you're compiling with GCC it's implemented with "long long".  Obviously, you
need such a data type to stretch things like time_t to 64 bits, but who
cares?  It's there, it's been there for a Long Time, and it works on 32-bit
architectures -- because off_t required it first.

"long long" or equivalent will be part of C9X, barring radical changes before
the standard's on paper.  At least that's my understanding.
