JF Mezei wrote:
> 
> Check out the unix home page at www.unix.digital.com:
> 
>         COMPAQ HAS IT NOW
>         Sun's project Cascade
> 
> It just sounds so weird since we're so used to thinking "Digital Had it before
> everyone else but killed it before eveyone else".

One thing that got my notice:  the claim that Digital Unix is "The ONLY UNIX
Platform that Included FREE Internet Software".

I've been wondering what they could mean by this. They clearly don't mean 
that you don't have to pay any money to get this "Internet Software" with
Digital Unix, because they aren't giving Digital Unix out for free. So it
is most likely the old marketing approach that "If you pay us for X, we'll
give you Y for `free'".  That is, the "Internet Software" is bundled with
Digital Unix, and you don't have to pay extra for it.

But this doesn't work, either, because this is true of every commercial
Unix, not to mention linux.  I did see some AT&T Unix machines maybe 10
years ago that didn't include any Internet software.  But these days, I
don't think you can buy a Unix system from anyone that doesn't pretty much
come with a complete library of nearly every known Internet tool (except
for IE ;-).  Every Unix system comes with IP, TCP, the socket library,
and tools like inetd, telnet, finger, whois, NFS, sendmail, nntpd, and
on and on.  Most of them include at least one web server and two or more 
browsers on the install CDs.

So how can they justify claiming that "FREE Internet Software" is only
included with Digital Unix?  

The most likely explanation I can think of is that, if you read the claim
very narrowly, it can be interpreted as a bit of disinformation to try
to tell non-unix people (presumably those in positions to make purchase
decisions) that Unix systems don't generally come with Internet software.
You'd think that such a claim would just get laughs, but I did read of 
a survey a month or so back in which one of the claimed results was that 
about 2/3 of the "top managers" said that the Internet was built and 
owned  by Microsoft.  (They also said that 80% of 6-year-olds gave the
correct answer "nobody" to "Who owns the Internet?" ;-)  So there's a 
good chance that management in general is susceptible to a claim that  
Unix systems don't come with Internet software, and the Compaq claim is 
aimed at convincing them that Compaq/Digital is the first Unix system 
that has Internet softtware.  (And it's FREE, unlike those linux guys
that charge you for it. ;-)



