In article <6up3fp$eqa$1@hecate.umd.edu>, 
bleau@umdsp.umd.edu (Lawrence Bleau) writes:

> Have there been problems reading TLZ09-written tapes on non-TLZ09 drives?  What
> about the other way around?  Does it make a difference if compression is turned
> on (I have it always on when writing)?

We used to have a TLZ07 on an AlphaServer 1000A.   VMS Backup tapes
written with the TLZ07 (with compression on) were easily readable 
on our old Archive Python 4322NP drives.

After a whole string of TLZ07 failures, DEC Field Service finally
replaced it with a TLZ09.   Now we can no longer read compressed
tapes on the Archive drives.   The tapes mount OK, but after the
first few records, Backup reports parity errors.   Uncompressed
tapes read OK.   We are still using the same 90 meter tapes we
have always used.   

I have had no problem reading tapes from the Archive drives on
the TLZ09.

Other than that, we are very happy with the TLZ09.   It is
very fast and has only glitched once in eight months.   Turning
the power off and on again cleared the problem.

