> : Alas, it's already been done. The new name is Micro$oft Windows NT.

        I almost posted a reply when Terry  S.  said that 'cause it just
    ain't  so  (no  matter what Billy Boy and the urban  legend  mongers
    would have you believe).  But I restrained myself.  Now we get this:

> 	Yah, I've heard that NT is a derivitive of VMS.

        So this Bright  Light  _heard_  something,  and having heard it,
    takes  it  as _fact_!  A real powerhouse of critical thinking,  this
    one...

        VMS proponents _know_ that,  even  with  Dave Cutler having been
    instrumental  in early internals development on WNT (that's the only
    connection, Cutler <==> VMS), and even though  many  of  the  API's,
    etc.,  closely resemble similar structures found in VMS (those areas
    of VMS that Cutler contributed to), that don't make NT no derivative
    of VMS.  Remember (1) Cutler left  VMS development shortly after VMS
    1.0  was released (almost 20 years ago) and made no contribution  to
    VMS after that,  in  particular,  to  some  of  the  most  important
    features  found  in  "modern" VMS (like VMSclusters, shadowing, DLM,
    etc.); (2) that WNT is  much  more  a  derivative of MS Windows than
    anything  else,  and as such, M$ prevented Cutler from  implementing
    various strong points of VMS in WNT, or implemented them  so  poorly
    that  it  doesn't matter; and (3) a major difference between VMS and
    anything with the M$  label  on  it  is  QUALITY CONTROL and UPWARDS
    COMPATIBILITY, e.g., those very qualities that result in RELIABILITY
    and  the protection of the customer's investment (this last one  has
    seemed a bit strained  for  the  last  decade  what  with  Digital's
    stealth marketing and death wish; nevertheless, upward compatibility
    remains a fact).

        Which brings me back to Terry's  remark.  Personally, I think if
    Compaq  were  to give all of VMS's secrets to Redmond,  it  wouldn't
    make WNT a pinch more reliable!   WNT  is  already  "feature  rich".
    More  features  aren't going to help it.  And more input from Tandem
    and VMS aren't going to help it.  Only _quality_ _engineering_ and a
    culture of getting it right before shipping, of getting the bugs out
    as soon as possible, not in  the "next release", of _protecting_ the
    user's  data  as  the  _primary_ criteria, is going to  give  you  a
    reliable operating system.  It hasn't mattered in the past  that  MS
    Windows  crashed  semi-regularly.   It  was  "only for the desktop",
    after all.  WNT wishes to be an  enterprise class OS.  Well, you can
    wish  all  you  want,  you can add a zillion features,  but  if  the
    quality control isn't there (and design review as a prerequisite  of
    that), you're NEVER going to achieve reliability.

        What I cannot for  the  life  of  me  understand is why _anyone_
    thinks  that WNT will be ready for the enterprise in 5 years, or 10,
    or 15 for that matter.  In some  cases,  you  might  get  a  general
    lowering  of  expectations  such  that the term, "enterprise ready",
    starts to be applied to something with  as poor quality as WNT.  But
    I  do  not  for a moment believe that anything coming out of  M$  is
    _ever_ going to be suitable for 365x24 operations.  Period.

>                                                        This makes
> 	me think that the VMS proponents must have never used any
> 	other OS.

        Quite the  opposite,  Prof.   Einstein.   _Most_  VMS  users and
    system  managers have a good deal of experience with other operating
    systems, both your standard desktop fare (from  M$  and  Apple)  and
    unix  of  various  flavors,  as well as "mainframe" systems like MVS
    and/or VM/CMS.  With the  possible  exception  of  MVS  and VM, none
    compares favorably to VMS _except_ in certain _restricted_ instances
    (use the right tool for the right job, eh?).

>                  They even site being stuck with teletype like non-
> 	cased terminal emulation as an advantage.

        Excuse me, what in the world  are you talking about???  Terminal
    _emulation_?   That  sounds  like a Windows thing.   Teletype?   Not
    since the PDP-11.  Have you heard of X-windows?  Thought not.

>                                                  Wonder if they use
> 	paper tape as a backup media? :^)

        You'd do well  to  educate  yourself  before making such idiotic
    statements.   With the exception of MVS and VM/CMS, I've never  seen
    an operating system with a reliable means of backup (and I mean save
    _and_ restore, not  the  usual  write-once,  read-never  "archiving"
    systems  found  on  unix (tar) and various DOS and Windows systems).
    Are you aware that the most modern  tape drives (DLT) being used for
    backup  as well as other uses, were invented by Digital and are  the
    preferred medium on VMS?  Naw, didn't think so.

        -Ken
