graywolf wrote:
>         Yah, I've heard that NT is a derivitive of VMS.  This makes
>         me think that the VMS proponents must have never used any
>         other OS.  They even site being stuck with teletype like non-
>         cased terminal emulation as an advantage.  Wonder if they use
>         paper tape as a backup media? :^)

	Derivitive is the key.  An airplane and a boat are derivitive
in the same way, since they are both propelled by an internal
combustion engine.

	With VMS and WNT, the engine is very similar, but outside
the "engine" there are many other different things attached. Many
of us have used other O/S's (I was a Unix user about the time 
VMS was released).  Us VMS bigots don't have so much of a problem
with the Kernel itself, as the required buggy baggage of WIN32
that's so tightly intertwined and blue-screens your system so much.

	Yes, we see being able to use a dumb teletype in an
emgergency (and still be able to monitor and manage the system)
as a definite advantage.  After all, some big multi-processor
box with hot-swap CPU's and power supply's and fan cages and
redundant everything does you no good when the SVGA card is the
single point of failure - lose it and you're sunk, along with
a good portion of your enterprize.

	With VMS, I can access the system with DECnet, TCP/IP,
LAT, SMI, or the serial console.  I've used my PalmPilot (VT100
emulator) as a console to configure and boot one of my Alpha's
in a pinch.  You simply just can't do that on WNT.

	Backups?  To tape or real-time volume shadowing... with
the shadow volume in another state (see Credit Lyonisse).  But in
a pinch, I could probably back up a few things to the paper tape
on my ASR33.  I'd use mylar, though, as it has a better shelf life.

