I don't have an HP box to hand, so this is guess work, but try just
reading the tape and throwing away the contents.

 #  dd if=/dev/rmt/0m of=/dev/null

if you get errors doing this, then you could have:

  a) a duff tape drive on your HP box.
  b) a duff tape drive on your DEC box.  Have you tried reading it back on
     the DEC system?
  c) If (a) and (b) check out and both drives can happily write and read
     their own tapes, then it could be a head alignment problem between
     the two tape drives.

But if that works, then try this:

 # dd if=/dev/rmt/0m  |  tar tf -

You may need to add and fiddle with the 'bs' option to dd(1) to get the
tape to stream properly. 

Another idea .. the HP device name "/dev/rmt/0m" .. does the "m" mean
medium density?  And did you write the tape on the DEC box using rmt0h,
where "h" is high density (ie: hardware compression).  If so, then it's
no wonder you can't read the tape.  Does HP-UX give you more choices of
device names?

Those are the only ideas I have ..

