In article <6u94ko$kp1$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, bio@909mail.dk writes:

>I would like to use the disks in a Classic II MAC, using system 7.1
>as operating system. (Not the best machine I know !)
>
>Do you not think it is possible ??
>I succeded in using RZ25.
>
>I can see on the disks that
>RZ23L-E = CP30100D
>RZ24E = CP3200D
>RZ23-E = CP3100D

I've used drives similar to these in a number of systems, including
Macintosh (Quadra 650 and G3 MT).

As was noted by some other people, some of these drives have jumpers
to enable spin-up, and some don't.  If your drive doesn't have a 
jumper, then in some cases the controller can issue the spin-up
command.  Unfortunately, most of the built-in Macintosh controllers
apparently don't issue a spin-up command.

If that is the case, you need to program the drive to spin up.
As was noted, there is an old program RZSPINUP that runs on
IBM-PC compatibles, and will program some of the older drives
to spin up.

On a Macintosh, you usually need a third-party device driver to
use a non-Apple drive: this is certainly true for most drives
on System 7.1.  If you get the FWB HDT kit, you will get the
drivers and a utility that examines the various mode pages
within the drive.  I believe that this can also program the
spin-up characteristic in the drive, though the setting may
not be very obvious to a beginner.  It's been a while since
I've done it, but I'm pretty sure this is how I set the spin-up
bit on at least one drive.

-- 
