In article <36004E73.DDB095FA@home.com>, Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:
>second floor (elapsed time ~10 minutes total).  Once I got it hooked
>back up, I powered it up and both disks (Quantum Fireballs) were
>DOA.  Apparently the heads had parked and got stuck there.  Gotta
>love those backups (and pile of spare disks)!

Quantum drives seem very susceptible to this kind of problem. In my 
experience it is usually from the spindle bearing lubricant leaking or settling
to one side fo the bearing. Carefully opening the drive in a still, not
very dusty room, and putting a drop of Zep-45 on the bearing, letting it soak
in for an hour or so, and then putting the drive back into service, has
worked every time for me. 
The drives work fine, its just that they cant get past the initial resistnce
to rotation in the bearing once they spin down, since the motors dont
have the power to get it moving. 
YMMV

happy hacking
gregg



