In article <6u6gie$fgk@web.nmti.com>,
Peter da Silva <peter@baileynm.com> wrote:
>Barry Margolin  <barmar@bbnplanet.com> wrote:
>>If your goal is to avoid race conditions that can be used as security
>>holes, you're probably right.  But if your goal is a fully correct /tmp
>>cleaner, possibilities like this will always exist unless special kernel
>>support is provided.
>
>There's a number of hacks you can use to get the same result, though they
>do require moderate exotica... they're not specifically kernel support for
>temp cleaning.

These techniques still don't identify which files are genuinely
finished with. (Every so often, users ask for files in /tmp to be
recovered from backups!) Nor do they delete structured groups of files
together - you can't do that under Unix without the cooperation of the
program that created them. Since a fully correct /tmp cleaning program
is impossible under Unix, it's not worth spending much effort trying
to make one.

>If you have variable symlinks you can pull a nasty trick:
>    ln -s '/usrtmp/$(LOGNAME)/tmp' /tmp    # modulo variable symlink syntax
>
>Now anyone accessing /tmp will find themselves in a private temp dir they own.

I'm not too keen on variable symlinks, especially for /tmp. 
Suppose an unprivileged user sets LOGNAME to "../net/nfs-server-name". 
If a set-id program were to use a temporary file, that would give the
user access to what the program wrote, and control over what the
program read back.

[Follow-ups trimmed.]

Peter



