in /tmp anyway.

To use a tmp cleaner to delete arbitrary files with symlinks, you need to
substitute a symlink for a DIRECTORY.  This cannot be done with tmpwatch as
far as I can see.

(Of course there are other ways to fool some tmp cleaners, even down to files
with wildcards or spaces in them.  I don't believe tmpwatch has any of these
other vulnerabilities, either, but the symlink substitution is the one which
takes substantial work to evade, once you're writing something from scratch
like tmpwatch (as opposed to using find).)

(Note also that some of the proposals to chown files, etc, will actually
INCREASE the vulnerabilities, because although unlink() does not follow
symlinks, chown() and chmod() DO on most systems, chmod() unavoidably (because
there is no lchmod()).  This is not an issue for linux though (yay!).)

>As I stated originally, unless you can be entirely sure that you won't swap
>between deciding to delete a file, and the actual system call to delete
>the file, there is an insecurity.

If by "swap" you mean "substitute something else", then I think you are wrong,
this does not in general lead to a vulnerability.  It depends on what you can
substitute for what.

If by "swap" you mean "yield time to another process so that it might do
something", this is unavoidable; some of those system calls will remove your
"ready to run" status.  And you might be on a multiprocessor machine anyway.
Avoiding swapping is a single-user-system-oriented method of avoiding other
activity; what you really want is atomic operations in the kernel.
(I've wanted a rename3(), move Y to Z and X to Y in a single op, with a
guarantee that even in error, ONE of them will end up being linked in as Y,
and there will be no Y-less window... this would be a very general mechanism.)
But I don't think that these are necessary for secure tmp cleaning.

I would be extremely interested to hear of any vulnerabilities in tmpwatch,
because I rely on it.  (Actually, I have some arguably useful modifications to
it which I have to get around to posting..... next term, too busy now...)



