Subject: Digital Unix routing table bug?
Date: 1998/09/24
Message-ID: <360A6B87.BD2E9C3@eddie.mit.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 394427624
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Organization: Digital Equipment Corp (soon to be Compaq)
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

This certainly seems like a bug, but perhaps someone can propose
an explanation.  What happens is that a routing-table entry doesn't
work (i.e., ping fails when it should succeed).  Deleting the entry
and adding an identical entry causes pings to start working.

An example in a lab machine - "netstat -rn" produces the output:

10.1.1/24        10.1.1.102         UGS         0        6  tu0
10.3.1/24        10.1.1.102         UGS         0      116  tu0
16.30.48/24      16.30.48.229       U          24    62780  tu1
127.0.0.1        127.0.0.1          UH          3   112301  lo0

The command "ping 10.3.1.102" fails totally, despite the route
to the 10.3.1 network.  I type the commands:

# route delete -net 10.3.1
# route add -net 10.3.1 10.1.1.102
# ping 10.3.1.102
PING 10.3.1.102 (10.3.1.102): 56 data bytes
64 bytes from 10.3.1.102: icmp_seq=0 ttl=64 time=1 ms
...

It works!  After the "route add", "netstat -rn" shows:

10.1.1/24        10.1.1.102         UGS         0        6  tu0
10.3.1/24        10.1.1.102         UGS         0        0  tu0
16.30.48/24      16.30.48.229       U          23    62780  tu1
127.0.0.1        127.0.0.1          UH          3   112301  lo0

Apart from the zero Use counter, the 10.3.1 entry is identical,
so this should have changed nothing at all, but in fact it makes
the route start working.

The system's /etc/motd says it has Digital UNIX V4.0D (Rev. 878)
installed.  It also has "routed -q" running, but this seems to
have no effect.  (Killing it doesn't change the behavior, and
neither does restarting it.)

Is this a bona-fide bug, or is there something I'm missing?



