Tim Streater <Tim.Streater@dante.org.uk> wrote:
>In article <6u664m$bn9@web.nmti.com>, peter@baileynm.com (Peter da Silva) wrote:
>>But don't cry for MacOS. There wasn't anything worth saving below the GUI.

>and the finder

Which is part of the GUI.

>and the actual plug&play networking and the lack of paths
>and the lack of drive letters and the actual device plug&play and the fact
>that what counts is the volume, not the device and a large number of
>seemingly small usability features and the lack of need of a CLI.

All nice features, but hardly unique to Apple. About the only MacOS
specific feature below the GUI I can think of is the dual-fork files.

Which is kinda nice, but I'm sure that MacOS X will retain that, and all
the other stuff you DID mention.

What you're losing is the cooperative multitasking, the '60s era memory
partitioning, the poorly integrated VM, and all the other problems that
are part and parcel of having an OS that had to be crammed into 128K
along with an application *and* enough RAM left over to do stuff... and
just growing it piecemeal as time went by.

I hate to say this... because I do truly despise Microsoft, but if you
look at where they started and where Apple started, they really have done
a better job of making a silk purse out of a sow's ear. Windows has some
truly awful features... I'll grant you more than MacOS does... but it's
got some things that were done a lot more right than Apple's managed. And
they didn't start from the same place: if MacOS is a sow's ear, MS-DOS
is one from a crotchety old wild pig that's been chewed off by a dingo
and left to rot in the sun for a week until even the buzzards don't want
it.

As for a CLI, a good one makes up for a plethora of evils. A bad one, well,
I wouldn't mind never seeing MCR, DCL, or COMMAND.COM ever again.

The Mac, of course, does have a command interpreter. I don't recall what
its current incarnation is called, but it's had one since the original
Hypercard. Some kind of batch or script based command processor is too
useful to give up, even if it has to be disguised...

