In article <6u6p4e$2u7$1@news.latrobe.edu.au>,
Huw Davies <cchd@lucifer.its.latrobe.edu.au> wrote:

>I seem to recall you could have vectors in all systems upto the 64xx series.
>After that, it was faster to do vector work on the VAX CPU rather than 
>hand it over to the vector processor....

My memory is that in the 6xxx series only 6400 and 6500 supported a
vector processor (the same one, I think).  It wasn't much faster than
the 6500 CPU; the design suffered from loose coupling to the main CPU,
and insufficient memory bus bandwidth.  The 6400 and 6500 CPUs were
similar to each other, probably allowing DEC to reuse a lot of the work
done on the 6400 to support vector processing on the 6500.  Earlier
models were CVAX.  Later models were NVAX; DEC didn't find it worth
their time to add a vector interface when Alpha would be the platform
of choice for serious floating point users.

-- 
    John Carr (jfc@mit.edu)



