The following is reprinted without permission:

INTERNETWEEK via NewsEdge Corporation : Compaq plans to migrate Tandem
Computers' MIPS-based Himalaya enterprise servers to the Digital Alpha chip
architecture in an attempt to rescue Tandem users from the stalled MIPS
architecture.

The systems giant last week said it expects the first Alpha-based Himalaya
servers to be available in early 2002. The new systems will be
backward-compatible with MIPS-based storage and communications systems, and
perhaps most important, legacy applications.

Compaq will write translation software and accelerators to run existing MIPS
binaries on Alpha-based machines and hopes to achieve better than 80 percent
performance for the same code running as native MIPS instructions.

The decision comes five months after Silicon Graphics Inc., which owns the
MIPS chip, said it plans to focus the technology on embedded and consumer
devices, leaving high-end systems such as Himalaya in the lurch.

The decision signals several things, all of which are good for Compaq and
its customers, analysts said. First, it gives Tandem users a secure future.
The decision also is one of the first concrete signs that Compaq is
following through with its promise to invest in the Alpha chip, which it
acquired when it bought Digital Equipment this year. Also, it's one of the
first signs that Compaq is intelligently merging the two giant companies it
bought in the past 15 months.

"It looks like they're taking the best parts of the companies and doing
something with them," said Rob Enderle, an analyst at Giga Information
Group.

"Alpha users are going to be jumping up and down over this one," said
Dataquest Inc. analyst Jerry Sheridan.

News of Compaq's plans for Alpha were first reported last Thursday on
InternetWeek's Web site.

Some users and analysts feared that Compaq might abandon or sell the Alpha
chip. In May, while the acquisition by Compaq was pending, Digital sold its
Alpha manufacturing facilities to Intel but retained the right to design
future Alpha architectures.

Likewise, Tandem users will be pleased, said Laurie McCabe, an analyst at
Summit Strategies.

"It gives Tandem people more of a sense of commitment and security," McCabe
said. "It mainstreams them a little into this Compaq/Digital behemoth."

An alternative considered by Compaq was to move the chip to Intel's 64-bit
architecture. The first IA-64 chip, Merced, is due out in the year 2000,
followed by the more powerful McKinley chip. But Compaq was looking for a
processor that would be mature in the next three to five years, which it
decided IA-64 chips would not be, said Pauline Nist, vice president for
products and technology at Tandem. In particular, Tandem believes that Intel
needs more experience writing compilers for its architecture, Nist said.

Digital, on the other hand, is experienced in writing compilers, Nist said.
In particular, it is experienced with the kind of translators that Tandem
will need in order to run MIPS code on Alpha. Digital makes an Alpha
translator that runs Windows NT applications that were written for
Intel-based PCs.

The new Himalayas will use the EV7 generation of Alpha processors, scheduled
to go into sampling late in the year 2000. Current Alpha processors are
unsuitable because the Himalaya requires processors to work in lockstep
pairs for error checking-something current Alpha processors are unable to
do, according to Nist.

Ultimately, Compaq will phase out MIPS processors altogether, though the
transition will occur over the course of years. For Tandem users with
significant investments in hardware and software, "Internet time" is a null
concept.

"Tandem users are a slow-moving bunch," Nist said. "You don't want to bully
them into anything.''

Indeed, Tandem users expressed concern that Compaq would not make the
transition painless, saying that Tandem needs to succeed in its goal of
maintaining software compatibility.

"If it's a seamless transition, just another upgrade, it's no big deal,"
said Mike Thyken, director of technical services at Dayton-Hudson Corp. "If
it requires a major upgrade, then it becomes an issue."

Dayton-Hudson owns large retail stores, including the Target and Mervyn's
chains, and uses Tandem systems for data warehousing.

Kenneth Yagelski, an IT manager at Illinova Illinois Power, said he's also
concerned about performance degradation between the old servers and the new
ones. Aside from that, a change of processors is somewhat irrelevant to
users, he said.

"There's a lot of parity when it comes to some of these levels of
technology," Yagelski said. "It's like the Intel/AMD comparison-is there
really that much difference to the user? Does it matter if it's Alpha inside
or MIPS inside?"

The shift to Alpha will be the second big architectural change for Tandem,
which shipped its first fault-tolerant system in 1974. In 1990, the company
shifted from its proprietary CISC architecture to the MIPS RISC processor,
writing translation and optimization software for its users' code as it
plans to for the MIPS-to-Alpha transition. Many users achieved greater than
80 percent performance of the CISC code running on MIPS systems, and many
are still running that code today, without recompiling, Tandem's Nist said.

Tandem achieves fault tolerance through massive redundancy. Processors are
linked by dual buses into lockstep pairs, and groups of servers are
clustered together using the ServerNet interconnect, which Compaq now uses
for Intel PC servers as well. The system can support approximately 4,000
processor pairs.

The operating system, called NonStop Kernel, uses a microkernel architecture
that runs in parallel across processors to boost performance, balance loads
and make the clusters appear to the software to be a single machine. Users
can layer "personalities" over the microkernel to run Unix or Windows NT
applications.

