  Before anyone will manufacture low-cost SMP motherboards, there 
needs to be an OS targetting the budget-minded customer which can 
take advantage of SMP and run on the Alpha.  Unfortunately WNT 
does not scale very well across multiple processors, and Linux 
users are not (yet) perceived as a significant force in the market.

  Perhaps the folks at Be could port their OS to the Alpha?  It's 
supposed to be very portable by design (it was developed on the 
Hobbit, and then ported to PowerPC and x86) and has the technology
to take good advantage of SMP (via an operating system implemented
as distributable threads, and an application model based on the 
same).

  I suspect, however, that Be wouldn't want to do it until it saw
low-cost SMP Alpha systems in the marketplace .. which puts us back
where we started.  :-/

  On the other hand, I've heard rumors that dual-processor 21264
systems will be here "real soon now".  Perhaps by the time the 
21364 shows its face the SMP 21264 systems will be cheap enough 
to attract attention from the low end of the market (ie, personal 
computing).  By then BeOS might be a stable, accepted operating 
system with a significant base of applications.

  That's a lot of ifs, though.

