> VMS may indeed be on the way out, for reasons more eloquently stated
> elsewhere by Terry Shannon (though I also agree wholeheartedly with his
> analysis of the continuing need for high-end, high-availability robust
> systems, in which market VMS is a MIGHTY competitor - long live VMS!).
> However, I think its death even in other markets is likely to be
> prolonged - I am still running, supporting, and have just finished
> upgrading RSX on my PDP's, and "everyone" told me they'd be gone LONG
> ago.  In fact, a co-worker was hired 11 years ago and told that his
> PDP-11 experience was nice but wouldn't be particularly useful, since
> they'd be gone soon.  He's retiring next week.  They're still there.
> 
> It's a strange world out here ...
> 
> Nora Narum

Indeed it is. It is nothing short of amazing what a single statement in
a trade press article can do to user perceptions of product viability.
VMS has been buried by the press before--as has God (Time Magazine,
circa 1965ish), the Mainframe (multiple trade rags), and Unix (ditto).

All of the above remain as viable as, well, MVS!

The one potential show-stopper for OVMS is ISV support. If this goes
away, the OS loses its raison d'etre.

Were I to offer advice to the CPQ folks responsible for OVMS, I'd tell
'em to take a Bill Clinton "War Room" approach to marketing and
messaging. Thanks to a hospital-full of spin doctors, El Jefe's
popularity ratings continue to defy gravity, bimbo eruptions, tax hikes,
Special Prosecutors, Generic Persecutors and sundry blemishes too
numerous to mention.

OVMS certainly suffers from far fewer negatives, most of which can
countered, minimized, or neutralized by effective messaging.

More proactivity would be a Good Thing, too. Why is it that OVMS is such
a juicy target. You don't find people beating up on MVS, which is a
similar--and equally high quality--product catering to a similar market.

It clearly will take WNT quite some time to achieve parity with OVMS,
even in light of the technology transfer agreement between CPQ and
u$oft. As long as OVMS is differentiated from WNT, it will continue to
attract demanding customers (you wanna see that Screen of Death in your
stock exchange or nuclear powerplant process monitoring system? I don't
think so...).

Following is the content of a slide I use in my Compaq Strategy
presentation (a PDF version of which is available at
http://www.acersoft.com ). It contrasts the current capabilities of OVMS
vis-a-vis WNT.

 

"I Know OpenVMS, and WNT, You�re No OpenVMS�"

Does Windows NT Have The Right Stuff? NOT YET!

Attribute 		OpenVMS		WNT

File system		TB		GB
Clusters		>96 nodes	2 nodes
Cluster interconnects	100MB/sec	1.2MB/sec
Memory addressing	64bit		32bit
Availability		99.9999%	97+

The bottom line is that CPQ will support (and enhance) OVMS for, in the
words of Eckhard Pfeiffer, "as long as customers want it." The number of
pure OVMS shops will decline to virtually nil, but that doesn't mean the
OS itself is dead.

Regards,

Terry Shannon
Consultant and Publisher, Shannon Knows Distributed Enterprise Computing
http://www.acersoft.com




