Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <35F71814.1E0B911C@iakom.com> <36069F96.E8127ED3@llnl.gov> <6u67ug$ccd@web.nmti.com> <36079B1B.2781@s054.aone.net.au> <6u88qm$t5u@web.nmti.com>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Hewlett Packard Engineering Services Group (Consultant)
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.admin,comp.sys.hp.hpux,comp.sys.sgi.admin,comp.sys.sun.admin,comp.sys.dec,comp.security.unix

OK, I guess I'll throw my $.02 into the hat...

As many people have said, we can safely ignore the problem of
links to ordinary files because the unlink will remove the link
and not the file.

Also, doing setuid's to the directory owners is nasty because
it's complicated and because (on some systems) you can create
something in /tmp and then chown it to the user of your
choice.

So...what we need is a way to make sure we cd at each point
and then check that the cd's went where we wanted them to go.

One way that was mentioned by Peter de Silva is to
check that pwd returns the path we expect after the cd.

This is pretty good but has two problems:
  o It's slow for deep directory trees
  o It's not guaranteed to work for pwd returning
    very long paths (like longer than PATH_MAX).

However, we don't actually have to check the whole path, just
the last component if we check device/inode of .. vs . instead of
names (yes, you could munge . and .. on some systems, but
it's not a security concern since you'd need write permissions
on the directory already to munge it).

One implementation (in pseudo-code in no particular language
with error handling missing) is:
  
  function descend(device, inode, name) {
    chdir(name)
    dot=opendir(".")
    dotdotstatus=stat("..")
    if dotdotstatus.device != device
       error
    if dotdotstatus.inode != inode
       error
    status=fstat(dot)
    for entry in dot {
      fentry=open(entry)
      status=fstat(fentry)
      if isadirectory(status)
        descend(dotstatus.device, dotstatus.inode, entry.name)
      if isaregularfile(status) and isoldenough(status)
        unlink(entry.name)
    }

One problem with this implementation is that it keeps
file descriptors open for each directory you've descended
into.  That problem can't be fixed because you specifically
don't want to rely on '.' and '..' not changing as you descend.

Now, if there were an funlink system call, you could even get
rid of the race condition of a regular file changing after
that stat and before the unlink.

-- 
Christopher J. Calabrese
ES Architect and DR/Audit/Security/y2k Coordinator (Consultant)
Hewlett Packard Engineering Services Group
cjc@fpk.hp.com



