In article <6u8qtk$qgj$2@nntp.Stanford.EDU>,
Jonathan Stone <jonathan@DSG.Stanford.EDU> wrote:
>In article <3607E656.6ADC9713@email.sps.mot.com>, Michael Sadd <ra8845@email.sps.mot.com> writes:
>|> 
>|> I have seen a number of post asking about running DEC executable under
>|> Linux, but
>|> I was wondering if the reverse is also possible?  Is there a way to run
>|> Linux executables
>|> under Digital Unix 4.0?
>
>AFAIK, no, there isn't. This would require kernel support from DU.
>(which may be implementable as a third-party kernel module, but would
>probably require access to dU source, makin git non-redistirbutable.)
>
>|> In general, is there a good way to install software that has been
>|> packaged into Linux
>|> RPM's for alpha, and install them onto a DEC Unix system.  There is so
>|> much out
>|> there, it would be nice to use it, without have to go through the
>|> tedious process of
>|> figuring out the proper build parameters, etc.
>|> 
>|> Any help is appreciated.
>
>It may not be what you're after, but if you installed *BSD instead of
>Linux, you can run both DU and Linux binaries under emulation mode, as
>well as getting many precompiled native software packages.

Well, not on the Alpha, you can't.

You *may* be able to run statically linked DU binaries.  This is very
infrequently tested, at best, and static DU binaries are few and far
between, at best.

You can definitely not yet run Linux binaries on the Alpha.  It's in the
pipeline, but it's certainly not there yet.  The lack of consistency
in the Linux system call interface on different architectures essentially
means that you have to write most of a whole new emulation for each
platform.

