Leigh Gardner wrote in message <3606145a.7623841@news.globalnet.co.uk>...

>I picked up a DEC MicroVax2 over the weekend and I have no idea on how
>to use it, or to get VMS installed on it.  Can anyone point me in the
>right direction to resources on the web which will introduce me to the
>wonders of the MicroVax2?


www.netbsd.org for info on NetBSD for Vax machines.
There is also a port-vax mailing list which I strongly recommend, subscribe
through the netbsd.org website.

http://anacin.nsc.vcu.edu/~jim/mvax/mvax_faq_text.html
is the FAQ on Microvax/Vaxstations.

The Microvax II is well supported by NetBSD.

VS3100's (I read your other post too) are only partly supported. They need
to be netbooted off another
U*ix box, as the SCSI support is not working well.  The RX23 Floppy is not
supported yet.
The graphics console is now supported, and X is being worked on but is not
supported yet.

You would be able to run the Hobbyist licensed version of VMS on both the
VS3100 and the MVII.
This is available through DECUS, though I'm not sure if Hobbyist VMS is
available in UK yet.
You must be a DECUS member to be eligible.
If it available, there is a CD you can get through DECUS with VMS install
files, 5.5-? with DECwindows, 6.1 without it IIRC.

Try a "B" at the >> prompt.  You might find it still has an o/s (probably
VMS 5.x) still on it.
If so, there is information in the VMS FAQ
ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms
on how to change the SYSTEM account password.
Connections for the console port on the back of the MVII can be found in the
VMS FAQ and in the Microvax FAQ.
I strongly recommend you read both, as there is some information that is not
duplicated.
There is also an extensive VMS/Digital resource at
http://multivac.jb.man.ac.uk:8000/helbig/BOOKMARKS/VMS.HTML
with many links to useful information.

Hope this helps.

