David L. Cathey (davidc@montagar.com) wrote:
: 	Given that it's a text file, what happens to the system if
: you do something silly in vi, and have a bogus /etc/system file?
: What does the system do on reboot?

Crashes, probably, but you can add the 'c' flag to the console boot
flags and boot without the sysconfigtab file and then fix it.  The
system has enough sensible defaults that the system will come up
without a sysconfigtab.  Adding a 's' will just bring it up in
single user mode if this doesn't work for some reason (i.e. if
"boot -flags c" fails, try "boot -flags cs"...

