les@MCS.COM (Leslie Mikesell) writes:
> 
> Huh?  Why would you want to install any default route if you don't have
> a router?  A closed network only needs the correct netmask.  The
> only time I've heard of using your own IP as the default route was
> a trick to let it discover routers proxy-arping for remote addresses
> and I didn't think anyone did that these days.

Proxy-arp is a great way to deal with big flat switched networks.

We run multiple, non-contiguous class C IP subnet on one broadcast
domain.  Setting the router to do proxy-arp and all the machines on
the net to use themselves as their default gateway has _greatly_
simplified all our routing problems.  We used to either run dynamic
route finders (ala gated) or have a list of static routes to 'often'
used local 'other' subnets.  When you end up having to put 10 static
routes on every box you configure and then change them again when you
loose one of these subnets is a major pain in the tush.  With
proxy-arp, only the router needs to be reconfigured, and presto,
everything still works.

Like another poster in this thread, we dont control the router that we
attach to.  Sometimes, despite the best efforts of those other people,
we loose routing service.  Our local network continues to run just
fine and people on subnet X can still get to machines on subnet Y
(both local) without any problems.  I initially learned about
proxy-arp/default-gateway-itself in response to our department head
not being able to check his email for 24 hours from his office machine
when the router was having troubles.

Check out 'Switched LANs' by John J. Roese (McGraw-Hill) for a full
discussion of this stuff.

Now, I wouldn't want to live, without proxy-arp.

(Oh, there are several reasons we have non-contiguous class C's on one
broadcast domain, mostly historical/political -- eventually we'll
probably consolidate down to a 'supernet' of 4 class Cs we recently
traded for 6 other subnets -- even then, DCHP is your friend.)


