Since my garage has become the repository for old computers and modems
(anyone need a 110 baud acoustic modem? :-) I just ventured out there
and examined a real DEC VT100 and a Datamedia DT-80 (a VT100 clone) and
their keyboards' home rows begin from the left:

[CTRL] [C.LOCK] [ A ] [ S ] ...

I also glanced at my old TTY ASR 33 (circa 1950s and 1960s) and my first
CRT terminal (a Datapoint 3300, circa 1970) and their home rows begin:

[CTRL] [ A ] [ S ] ...

and a KeyTronic keyboard circa 1980 mimics the VT100 and DT-80 keyboards

and a Tektronix keyboard on a graphics workstation begins:

[TAB] [CTRL] [ A ] [ S ] ...

and I'm looking right now at a recent HP terminal (700/96) whose keyboard
home row begins:

[CAPS] [CTRL] [ A ] [ S ] ...

so the Sun UNIX-style keyboard follows a long tradition predating PCs
whose keyboards exchange the CTRL and CAPS_LOCK keys and promote carpal
tunnel syndrome.

Thad



