Hello!

In article <6tur3l$m3b$1@sapa.inka.de>,  <ecki@lina.inka.de> wrote:
>[...]

>You can eighter use private TMP directories for important applications
>(setting TMPDIR to ~/.tmp/) or you can hack a funlink() into the kernel.

funlink() is not really possible. The file descriptor refers to an
inode (or generically a vnode) at last. The vnode has no linkage to
the directory entries referring to the file, so funlink() would
have to guess which link you want to remove.

A better thing would be this:

int safe_unlink(const char *fname, struct stat *sb)

like unlink, but only removes the file, if the name still refers
to the same file the stat buffer stems from (i.e. st_dev and st_ino
still match).

Or this:

int safe_unlink(const char *fname, int fd)

unlink fname, but only if fname refers to the same file as fd.

A similar thing could be interesting for directories:

int safe_rmdir(const char *dname, int fd)

(or the same thing with struct stat * like above)

Regards, Felix.



