Barry Margolin <barmar@bbnplanet.com> writes:

>It's not really a security problem, but it is a correctness problem.  If,
>during the window, the old file gets deleted by some other process and
>someone creates a new file with the same name, their new file will be
>deleted even though it's not yet 7 days old.


This is a pretty tiny chance -- if you check the file at the last second
before the unlink, the odds are so small it isn't worth worrying about.
This sort of accidental deletion isn't the same thing as a security race
condition where an attacker can make a system arbitrarily slow to win the
race.

As for the problem of secure deletion, he's the solution I came up with.
If anyone finds any problems with it, I'd like to know.  But I've been
using it for some time.  I took the source to the GNU findutils 4.1, and
added a "-delete" predicate.  It is careful to make sure it only deletes
what it should be deleting by doing a chdir() to the desired directory
and then doing an unlink there.  No chance of a linked directory causing
problems.  I also found and fixed a bug.  Sorry I can't remember exactly
what it was, it was about a year ago I made these changes.

Using this for -delete, you will want to cd into the desired directory
and then do a find from '.' after doing checking the current directory
and making sure you are where you think you should be.  For /tmp this
is not necessary; but if you clean out users' .netscape/cache directories
(which is what I did this for) then you need to be sure!




