In article <6u5kbj$rlv$1@news.clinet.fi>,
Kristoffer Lawson  <setok@oksidi.fishpool.com> wrote:
:2038 is really not a problem. An abstract datatype has been used for
:the number of seconds past epoch. Currently it's usually 32-bit, but
:there's no reason to just switch to 64-bit. All well coded applications
:will work fine (and who cares about badly coded?).

You'd have to replace many binaries simultaneously -- you could
not use your old binaries with the new time structure. At all.
Unless, that is, you mark the new executables as being of a different
variety, and you introduce a layer that switches system call
structures according to whether an old or new executable was being used.
Similar perhaps to the way SGI handled the COFF -> ELF transition:
COFF binaries were actually handed back different structures than
ELF binaries were for the same system calls, to accomedate widening
of UIDs and such.


You are going to have problems with all binary files that store
timestamps as part of a record -- you have to rebuild the database
using the new record width. As someone else pointed out, this includes
all your filesystems.


You are also going to have problems with any binaries that assume that
time differences can be stored in 32 bits. Or even that time
differences can be calculated at all (going to a 64 bit time_t does not
imply that the system can do 64 bit arithmetic.) You might say that
well-coded applications would not have assumed that times could be
operated on arithmetically, but ANSI X.3-159 (aka ANSI C) explicitly
requires (section 4.12.1) that clock_t and time_t are arithmetic types
and POSIX.1 imports time_t from ANSI X.3-159.

Thus, it has never been the case that time_t has actually been an
"abstract datatype": the rule about it having to be an arithmetic type
rules out any manner of structure or union or array, effectively
constraining it to be a long or unsigned long (as 'long long' is an
extension, not a part of ANSI C.) Thus, in standards-conformant
programs, you cannot go beyond 2038 without either changing your epoch
or else changing your ABI so that long is more than 32 bits.



