Chris wrote:
> 
> Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> >
> >
> > As far as tuning is concerned again these options
> > are either dynamic or are configured at boot
> > time. For example one can change say the
> > TCP Keep Alive dynamically.
> >
> 
> I was curious about the kernel tuning - which is the best method - I
> guess Solaris 2 comes as a fixed binary with no rebuild capability ?.
> Would that be more or less efficient in terms of execution speed and
> system resource usage than than the traditional makefile route where you
> only link in the bits you need = smaller executable ?.
> 

Solaris 2 comes as a set of fixed binary(s) in the sense
that 
most of the modules are dynamically loaded at run-time. 

You are probably right to suggest that a re-compiled kernel 
(for re-configuration) purposes could be more efficient 
than the dynamic approach but in my experience in practice 
this efficiency is often not achieved for a number of
reasons.

1. Most customers running SunOS used to run with the GENERIC 
   SunOS kernel which included all the devices that platform 
   might need. Mostly people could not be bothered to
re-compile 
   to remove the unused devices. 

2. When customers have built custom kernels they have in
generally 
   made the change they required for a particular app (SHMEM
etc) 
   but kept the rest of the GENERIC kernel.

3. People made mistakes re-compiling the kernel which
resulted 
   either in a kernel that did not boot or one that crashed.

A fully dynamically loadable kernel gets arround most of 
these issues. The dynamic kernel only loads the modules 
it needs and there is less opportuinity to create an 
environment that won't run.
> Still running 4.1.4 here BTW, for legacy reasons while migrating to
> Alpha. (Can't argue with the price or welly) Have been put off upgrading
> to Solaris 2 by the fact that it does seems slow on low end machines and
> I would not (engineer hat on) be happy with a more recent low end Sparc
> because of the use of ide drives, god only knows what other compromises
> have been made - this shop is all scsi and I wouldn't even put ide
> drives in a pc. The above is not an attack on Sparc BTW - seems to me
> all the w/s vendors are up against the same enemy...
> 
SunOS has a smaller footprint that Solaris and you may 
need more memory on your workstations to support Solaris.

The Ultra5s and Ultra10s use EIDE drives, though you 
can obviously add UltraSCSI or SCSI drives using a 
PCI adaptor. The use of EIDE is probably the only 
compromise that you would be concerned with along 
with 8bit graphics on the entry Ultra5. 

For single user workstations EIDE is not a major 
penalty and most customers I have delt with have 
not noticed the difference. 

If we were talking about servers then I would be 
much less convinced about the merits of using EIDE. 

The Ultra30 and Ultra60 both use SCSI and UltraSCSI.


Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



