Is it not feasible that the libraries use 64bit values bit but with
applications that use 32bit time_ts you would then only see the lower
32 bits. Obviously then you would have wrong dates with all old
applications after 2038 (unless the interpretation of time_t was changed) but 
at least they would run. I'd say the change to 64bit time_t should be
done a decade ahead of time. Plenty of time to recompile any problematic
programs.

: You are going to have problems with all binary files that store
: timestamps as part of a record -- you have to rebuild the database
: using the new record width. As someone else pointed out, this includes
: all your filesystems.

True, but doing this well in advance solves the problem with relatively
little hassle (XFS2, or whatever SGI would want to call it).

: Thus, it has never been the case that time_t has actually been an
: "abstract datatype": the rule about it having to be an arithmetic type
: rules out any manner of structure or union or array, effectively
: constraining it to be a long or unsigned long (as 'long long' is an
: extension, not a part of ANSI C.) Thus, in standards-conformant
: programs, you cannot go beyond 2038 without either changing your epoch
: or else changing your ABI so that long is more than 32 bits.

Well actually the C9X standard will include long long as an arithmetic
type. This is IMO unfortunate as it's an ugly kludge and mainly exists
in the first place for applications which have been written incorrectly
(changing the implementation so that long was 64bits would be cleaner).
Still, it will be there and although nothing guarantees long long will
be 64bits, in most cases it will allow an implementation to define
time_t as a long long. Really I don't see a problem even if long long
didn't exist as after some time pretty much all computers will have
processors supporting 64bit arithmetics so just changing long to be
64bits would solve that problem.

Of course there will be hassle with this particular issue but we can
only hope UNIX vendors have the sense to deal with it well before the
critical date.

