> On my home RedHat 5.0 system there is a small program called 'tmpwatch', by
> Erik Troan <ewt@redhat.com>, that's supposed to handle this. 
> 
Once we had a directory full of Linux patches and the cleaning script
decided to remove the oldest patches because they were not accessed in
<n> days, leaving an unusable set of patches. What seems more
appropriate is to consider every top level file or directory in /tmp and
to remove a directory only if no object inside has been accessed or created
within a number of days (mind extracted archives with very old dates). The
job of removing could then be done by `rm -rf' which is supposed to be
safe.

