Peter da Silva wrote:
> 
> This will protect files owned by "root".
> 
> >If the program decides to delete a file, change the owner of the file to
> >'nobody' (remember we're running as root here), then delete the file as
> >'nobody'.  Then this would work even for files owned by root.
> 
> This would allow you to change the permissions of the file to "nobody".
> 
> The real solution is to remove the directory name race by pinning the directory
> down with a "cd".

The real concern here is some small window to stick a symlink in place
of part of the path.

eg.	/tmp/tst/passwd  and at the exact moment remove tst/passwd and
put a symlink of ln -s /etc /tmp so that the rm /tmp/tst/passwd does
real damage. as the rm on its own will not follow a link nor will the
find.
	The solution is to pin the directory path in /tmp by changing
owner down the path to root:daemon or nobody:nobody then pass it again
that way the evil user will not get to change the directory to a link
they wont have the permission.

I think given that its tmp the degree of subdirs is resonable to assume
that if one like in a subdirectory of /tmp is old enough to remove then
the whole tree goes. Otherwise you could not the permissions/owner
before pinning the permissions and then put then back after the rm.
 I think the discussion is getting a little unrealistic. I think my
disks are more likely to fail than a user actually achived any result :)
esp. on a single processor machine just add up the system calls on the
side of the attacker against the find -exec rm :)


	Cheers
		Mark :)



