In article <6u6fv5$o69$1@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>,
TERENCE MURPHY <tsmurphy@cs.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>VMS is very much a UNIX.

I've used OpenVMS. It's got an OK UNIX emulator on top of it, and I do
appreciate the efforts of the people at Digitalmumblecompaqmumbledmanit
who did the work, but you really can't use the UNIX interface for enough
stuff to call it UNIX. We had a large UNIX application (really large,
many many gigs) and we tried porting it to OpenVMS.

Muttermumble. It'd run on Xenix and SunOS and later Solaris and Digital
UNIX (growing all the time). Going from 16 bit little-endian to 32-bit
big-endian and then to 64-bit little-endian was nothing compared to trying
to treat OpenVMS as UNIX.

We later ported just the user interface to NT. It's just about stable now,
and that took a long time, with the aid of a commercial UNIX-to-NT porting
library.

VMS isn't UNIX. VMS++ (WNT) isn't UNIX. That's not to say that they're not
perfectly good operating systems. Vive diversity!

>>Anyway, the few textbooks I own are explicetly about Unix, or describe
>                                                               ^^^^^^^^
>>an abstract OS, this is just very much like Unix. VMS is obviously not
> ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>>that populair, and actually, not very much in use at universities in
>>Europe (afaik). It not being available for the cheapest of the cheapest
>>hw (intel box), might have something to do with that.

>I very strongly object to the idea that an abstract OS is UNIX.

He didn't say UNIX was an abstract OS, he said that the abstract OS they
described was very much like UNIX. At least that's how I interpreted it.

But calling UNIX an abstract OS isn't so far out in left field.

>The
>only way that is true, is if you consider an abstract OS to be the
>absolute minimum of what is required to run programs (that's what UNIX
>is).

Um, no. The absolute minimum required to run prorams... even with multitasking
and multiuser support... is a LOT smaller than UNIX. At one place I worked
we had a three-user real-time development system in Forth that ran with 4K
of ROM and 12K of RAM, for a total of 16K addressible memory.

>What I'm really concerned about, is all of the college kids leaving
>school thinking UNIX is the definition of "high end" and "stability"
>(in reality, it's quite the opposite) and having no idea about all of
>the great features which exist(ed) on mainframe OS'es.

One of the great things about UNIX was the realization that a lot of those
great features weren't necessary at the OS level. You take a performance hit
in some cases, but you simplify the core immensely. I think that the modern
microkernel model, where the OS proper is just enough to handle scheduling
and partitioning, may not have ever developed without UNIX.

I came from TOPS and RSTS and RSX-11 and GCOS and NOS... and seeing how much
UNIX could do with such a simple, tight, and yes ABSTRACT interface was
liberating.

> Since these are
>the people who will be designing future OS'es, they're just going to
>keep re-designing UNIX (and calling it "NT" or something else) and
>we're never going to progress pass this embarrassment which has
>literally set the computer industry back 30 years...

Or shed thirty years of accretion so it could start over again.

Unfortunately the microkernel concept got shanghaied by operating systems
that are microkernels in name only (Mach, NT), so we're still waiting for
the next level of simplification to really strike home.

