In article <36079B1B.2781@s054.aone.net.au>,
Mark & Suzanne (gcs might work) <SPAMOFFPLS@s054.aone.net.au> wrote:
>	The solution is to pin the directory path in /tmp by changing
>owner down the path to root:daemon or nobody:nobody then pass it again
>that way the evil user will not get to change the directory to a link
>they wont have the permission.

That's not "the" solution, but it's certainly "a" solution. I think it'd be
disruptive to running applications to have permissions change under them,
so this adds yet another new kind of race condition, like the one Barry
Margolin was concerned about. And it's one that's much more likely to trigger
than his condition (where a file is deleted and replaced by another file of
the same name in the window between the stat and the delete) but it's one
that's much more likely to trigger. You can get the same security by using
chdir, at the cost of a fork. And someone just posted some other code that
avoids that fork (it's in the dirutils patches).

Another solution that just occurred to me, that would avoid both race
conditions, would be to rename the file and then check that the renamed
file matched the original stat info... and rename it back if it didn't.

There's still a race if a second program needed to access the file with that
name in that time window. I judge that possibility sufficiently infinitesimal,
especially if you combine it with the chdir trick.

