Matt Knecht <hex@voicenet.com> wrote:
>Kevin W. Thomas <kwthomas@wizard.nssl.noaa.gov> wrote:
>>Matt...
>>
>>/etc/rc2.d/S05RMTMPFILES doesn't do anything useful if /tmp is a mount point.

>Running a script that blindly does an rm -rf /tmp/* is a lot less
>damaging than rebooting the entire system.

About 1990 or so, there was one of the SysV.3's on iNTEL that had
a broken bourne shell.   That,in addition to a poorly written
script, that changed to the directory and then rm -r *'ed the files,
caused total wipeout on more than one machine.

The shell is supposed to fail and exit if something goes wrong.
However this implementation - as I recall it - would continue
to the next line even with the prior "cd" failed.  The 
program then rm -r *'ed the entire root directory.  Such an
interesting way to start off your Monday.  

That was discovered and fixed within a month or so, but too late
help some people.  It was an automatic cleanup script distributed
with the OS.  It's bring back the old TRUST NOTHING/NO-ONE rule.

>I'm of the firm belief that /tmp means temporary.  If people put
>anything there, there are *no* guarentees that it will be there even 3
>seconds from now.  My own scripts don't have quite a draconian view of
>things, though.  They check for file locks before doing anything.  If
>the file is locked, it gets a reprieve, else it gets unlinked.  If
>you're not using file locking, and using /tmp and your file goes away,
>you have nobody to blame but your own bad programming (Or a bad vendor).
Yup - temp is temporary - and I've seen a many applications use
/tmp to store the applications file work area.  A user won't be
extremely happy if their app goes south when the file in /tmp
gets removed.



-- 
Bill Vermillion   bv @ wjv.com 



