>Sticking heads can often be cured by removing the drive - and
>while holding it parallel to the desk/floor - rotating it quickly
>left and then right.   The motor doesn't have enought torque if 
>the problem is 'stiction'.  Stiction is the effect when two highly
>polished surfaces contact each other.  Air pressue keeps them
>together.  That is the principle used in gage blocks used to
>calibrate the carlibration equipment.  You can observe the same
>effect by placing two sheets of very clean flat glass together with
>a slight twisting motion to force all air between them out.

<nod> many times with the drives that shipped with Macs. 
Ive had numerous of DEC's RD53s have the heads stick to the shock cusion pad
on the chassis wall as the rubber in the pad got old and gooey. Open the drive,
give it some WD40 and a dab with some solvent and a qtip got rid of the crap
on the head arm and then some scotch tape over the pad fixed that one.. 
Who does surgery on disks anymore though? 

>
>Then you can't pull them apart because of the air pressure.
>
>Stiction is caused by the 'landing zone' of the disk.  The heads go
>to that track, and then the heads park themselves.  They hit on the
>disk when it is spinning and polish it to a very high degree.
>
>Many drives were made that when the drive spins down current to a
>a solenoid is changed, and the heads are physically locked in
>place, and not resting on the media.
>
>A few years ago one of the major manufacturers was having problems
>with their drive sticking - but not because of stiction.   Their
>problem was they had a lubricant that leaked from the motor in the
>center and slowly migrated outward to the head, OR - thickened to
>point that motor could not overcome inertia.  I have had drives
>exhibit this after being powered on for a year or more between
>interuptions.  Then they wouldn't like to boot if they had been
>turned off for a few hours.
>
A shot of zep-45 on the spindle bearing fixed this problem for me on many
a quantum. Kept 'em spinning happily for years. 

>Just plug them in - and let it sit.  Try booting every 15 minutes -
>and quite often they will warm up to the point where they will
>sping again.

A smack against a desk will do it too. On several occasions when there
wasnt a can of Zep around (WD40 did it in a pinch too, but is so light
that the same problem comes aroudn again, lubricant leaving the
bearing, and Zep really is the stuff for the job) you could get them
old quantums going again by giving them a solid smack against a desk or
a big book and applying power at the same time. Just leave the thing
running til i return with some oil was the general advice to the user. 

Its sort of a shame that these days its cheaper to just buy a new one
than to actually conserve an existing device :( 


>
>The Quantum Atlas series are really good drive.  The Atlas line is
>the old DEC line - and are engineered quite well.  I wouldn't use 
>the lower end SCSI devices and I avoid IDE.
>
>The top end Seagate line - starting with the Wren, and going
>through the Hawk, Baracudda, and Cheetah, all eveloved from the
>great line of CDC drives - when Seagate purchased their drive
>operations.  I wouldn't use others in the line then. 
>
>Not buying a drive because of it's being made by a particular
>manufacturer is short-sighted unless you know of a particular
>problem that occurs over all their drives.

These days though, the old rules are all wrong- so many people have
been bought out and so many lines changed. I think that one rule that
was worht following was that in my experience a Conner was junk from 
day one- and the low end Seagates that are just rebadged conners are 
equally crap. Maxtor ESDI drives have never given me trouble, even under
serious abuse- but that was years ago now and th elast of those here
went offline a year ago (was the boot device in a uVAX-II that is still
in service with SCSI disks :)
I dont trust _any_ IDE disk these days, and most low-end SCSI disks
are dubious too.. Micropolis was always a good buy in my book.. alas
that they couldnt sell enough hig-end stuff in todays commodity market..
(alas that the industry has been reduced to a low-quality-low-margin
commodity market!) 

>
>I've never heard of that except for the el-cheap commodity PC
>drives when there were a lot of manufacturers turing out junk
>for PCs, expecting them to be used 1 or 2 hours/day.

seriously! I once had some el-cheapo IDE drive fail, and upon opening
it for a post-mortem (and a little fun) i was agahst to find that
the head actuator was only _glued_ to the chassis!!! too cheap for
even a screw! 


happy hacking,
gregg



