> Is this a full power down, where a cold boot brings things back,
> or is this a very low power hibernation mode, where the processor
> slows to practically nothing, spins down disks etc., but does not
> actually have to do a cold boot? I could see hibernation, as not
> too hard to understand how to do, but saving and restoring ALL
> user context can be extremely complex. If Sun has that, they
> desreve kudos.
> 
> 

It is a full power down.

I frequently use it when I for some reason or another need to tear a
machine down and rebuild it exactly as it was.

And yes, when I stumbled across this for the first time, before
joining Sun, it was the coolest SW concept I've seen in a long time.

To test it I opened up a bunch of telnet connections to various
machines on a friday afternoon and suspended the system. On monday
morning I power it up and the connections were all in place. I guess
this would not have worked with anything but TCP/IP.

Only ultras can be configured to power up automatically. The older
systems, sun4m:s, need a push on the power button on the keyboard.

