In article <6u2br4$2e5$1@usenet01.srv.cis.pitt.edu>,
Gregg E Economou <geest3+@pitt.edu> wrote:
 
(And I wrote this - however attribution was deleted!)

>>Sticking heads can often be cured by removing the drive - and
>>while holding it parallel to the desk/floor - rotating it quickly
>>left and then right.  

>A smack against a desk will do it too.

The problem with a smack against the desk is that if you do it
vertially you can bounce the heads against the media - sometimes
causing media problem, or worse - head problems.  The rotation
method will avoid that.

>>Not buying a drive because of it's being made by a particular
>>manufacturer is short-sighted unless you know of a particular
>>problem that occurs over all their drives.

>These days though, the old rules are all wrong- so many people have
>been bought out and so many lines changed. I think that one rule that
>was worht following was that in my experience a Conner was junk from 
>day one- and the low end Seagates that are just rebadged conners are 
>equally crap.

Low end drives are built to price-points.   Most of the high-end
drives build for reliability and longevity.  

>Maxtor ESDI drives have never given me trouble, even under
>serious abuse- but that was years ago now and th elast of those here
>went offline a year ago (was the boot device in a uVAX-II that is still
>in service with SCSI disks :)

The last one went off-line here about 9 months ago after running
24x7 for seven years and two months.  The controller died.

>I dont trust _any_ IDE disk these days, and most low-end SCSI
>disks are dubious too.. Micropolis was always a good buy in my
>book.. alas that they couldnt sell enough hig-end stuff in todays
>commodity market.. (alas that the industry has been reduced to a
>low-quality-low-margin commodity market!)

Well I've never liked Micropolis - and that goes way way back to
the times they tried to define new industry standars for higher
density floppies.  That called them Quad capacity, and were the
ONLY manufacturer making floppies at 100TPI.  You had to write and
read ONLY on that type of drive.   They've been spotty on an off,
some years good, some years bad.  I don't miss them.

>>I've never heard of that except for the el-cheap commodity PC
>>drives when there were a lot of manufacturers turing out junk for
>>PCs, expecting them to be used 1 or 2 hours/day.

>seriously! I once had some el-cheapo IDE drive fail, and upon
>opening it for a post-mortem (and a little fun) i was agahst to
>find that the head actuator was only _glued_ to the chassis!!! too
>cheap for even a screw!

There is probably a good reason for that.   Today's glues - if
properly applied - will give a stronger bond - as the contact
is across the entire surface so the surface becomes the bond.  In a
screw that is the single connection point, and thus a point of
failure.

Secondly - if you use a screw - you have to space for the
corresponding socket.  Both use up space to a small extent and
the higher the head the fewer platters.  More importantly by using
a screw device you have increased the head mass.  To keep seek
times to the same as bonded heads then you have to beef up your
head actuation device - or lose performance.

Today's adhesives are wonderful - providing you do it correctly.
Of course people making price-point drives often cut corners - and
something they cut the WRONG corner.


