Thomas Tornblom wrote:
> "Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> writes:
> > Thomas Tornblom wrote:
> > > "Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> writes:

> >       Yes, given enough money and enough different software packages, it
> > can be done.

> Please Don, SDS is bundled.

	Unlike a C compiler. :-(

> > > We actually provide what the customers want. You want a keyboard with
> > > the singularly most unusable key (Caps Lock) in the place of the
> > > singulary most useful key (Control) then fine, order the machine with
> > > a PC-style keyboard. If not, order a Unix-style keyboard.

> >       "Unix-style keyboard"...  please.  You mean SUN-style keyboard.
> > They are the only one who has taken it upon themselves to move
> > standard keys out of their traditional position.  Why don't you just
> > move all the letters around too and tell users how much "better" it
> > is.  Sounds like you SUN folks are taking a page from the Microsoft
> > playbook.

> Ahh, yes, the "Unix-style" is an internal Sun name for it.
> 
> And actually it was the PC-industry that took it upon themself to
> redesign the keyboards. I have been in the trade since before PC:s and
> at that time all non-typewriter keyboards had the control key above
> shift, and backspace was non-destructive.

	As you say; "all non-typewriter keyboards".  If you've "been in the
trade since before PC:s [sic]", you know that most people of similar
vintage learned to touch type on typewriters (while most "computer
guys" consider being able to touch type on a par with a reboot).  My
opinion (and one that SUN obviously doesn't share) is that the IBM
Selectric (and perhaps the VT style) keyboard is the standard by
which all others are judged (by users/typists, not
engineers/programmers).  It is the result of decades of accumulated
knowledge about what works best for people that know how to type. 
On a personal note; one of my sisters types over 125 words a minute
and used to do quite respectfully in the local editions of the
"world's fastest typist" contests.  She was pounding out well over
100 on an IBM Selectric (or similar) for years and her speed has
increased since moving to a computer (although she hates using the
mouse).  Without gathering the empirical evidence to prove it, I can
guarantee that the only use she would have for a SUN keyboard would
be to beat me over the head with it for asking her to use it.  You
might as well sit her down at a keypunch machine and ask her to do a
speed demo.

> > > Before PC:s, backspace always meant back up the cursor one step
> > > non-destructively. Delete backed the cursor up *and* removed the
> > > character. Delete never meant delete forward.

> >       Before PCs, there were typewriters.  You know, those things where
> > the standard placement and function of the keys evolved.  The ones
> > where millions learned to type at quite respectable speeds for a
> > mechanical device.  The ones with "caps lock" above the "shift" key
> > and "backspace" in the upper right hand corner.

> Well, if you did your computing on typewriters, then I guess I can see
> your problem.

	translation: 'real computer guys don't touch-type'.

> I did mine on computers hook to terminals, vt100:s and
> such.

	translation: 'I've learned to use both index fingers'.

	I used a real VT-100 keyboard a couple of times.  It was long ago
and I can't remember exactly but every VT keyboard that I do recall
has the "control" and "caps lock" key placed correctly.  My personal
workstation is an ALPHAstation 600 and I know that it does.  I used
a VT320 (or was it 340?) for years and I know that it did.  I use
the few VT220s we have left now and then and while I can't say with
certainty where the "control" key is placed, I know that I've never
hit the wrong key on one so I would say that it's probably right as
well.

	Don't feel too bad about the SUN keyboard though.  The HP one is
worse.

> > > Interesting as it may be, it isn't very fruitful.

> >       Doesn't your SUN allow you to "ignore" a thread?

> Of course it does. I just wont accept it when people spread
> misinformation. You may deceive someone else into believe something
> that just isn't true.

	Then why complain?  As this thread has already been compared to an
"accident", I would point out that it's hypocritical to say, 'Isn't
that a terrible accident.  Pull up closer so I can get a better
look."

	While we all know that spelling flames are the province of
"clueless newbies", I must make an observation here;  to have the
number of spelling and punctuation errors that you do in a posting
about how 'easy to use' the SUN keyboard is kind of undercuts your
contention (especially since you work for SUN and should be more
familiar with their inferior keyboard than anyone).  It does however
add weight to my opinion that it's not the typist's keyboard of
choice. 

> I am very well aware that you just doesn't like Sun:s, for some reason
> unknown to me.

	They are annoying to use for a myriad of individually trivial
reasons that add up to a poor design.  On the OS side I guess
they're OK although it took them forever to breach the 2GB limit.  I
would prefer that a pre SOLARIS 2.6 machine not hang when doing
something as trivial as an "ls" on a directory that contains a
"large" file.  Even the SunOS machine would just lie and say that it
was 2GB rather than hang.  If you're going to fuck up on large
files, at least fuck up consistently.  Why change "/etc/fstab" to
"vfstab" besides capriciousness?  SUN's "OPENWINDOWS", which still
ships, deserves to go the way of DECwindows.  SUN's "mailtool"
still, after all these years, isn't MIME compliant and handles
attachments in a proprietary way.  "Backspace" and "delete" change
roles depending upon the state of the machine (unless the user
manually patches the bug).  SUN has deigned to move keys around on
their keyboard and told everyone 'it's better that way' in true
Microsoft fashion.  The optical mouse with it's proprietary and
non-interchangable "pad".

	Need I go on...?

> I am also aware that nothing I say will probably change
> that, you have made up your mind, and that is your privilege.

	Thank you.

> I welcome constructive criticism of our systems. They are by no means
> perfect, as is no system in this business. So far I have not seens
> anything resembling this.

	OK... 

	Constructive Criticism #1.  Copy the VT keyboard.  Embrace the VT
keyboard.  It 
				  is a model of perfection for those that type for real.

	Constructive Criticism #2.  Give "OPENWINDOWS" and its attendant
programs like "mailtool"
				  and "calendar manager" a decent and respectful burial. 
				  Their time has come and gone.

	Constructive Criticism #3.  Put out a patch for every version of
SOLARIS prior to 2.6
				  that will allow them to at least do an "ls" on a filesystem
				  that contains a file larger than 2GB without hanging.

	Constructive Criticism #4.  Put out a patch for SunOS that stops it
from reporting that
			          a "large" file system is 2GB in both "df" and "ls". 
Have them
				  report some type of 'overflow' condition by printing out "*"
				  or something for the size.

	Constructive Criticism #5.  Stop handing out "you can fix that",
"your system must not
				  be configured correctly" and "it's better that way" as an
				  answer to user complaints.

	Constructive Criticism #6.  Don't have install programs offer a
choice of where to install
				  software only to barf later on if you don't pick the
"standard"
				  location.

	Constructive Criticism #7.  Figure out how to have "backspace"
always mean BACKSPACE and
				  "delete" always mean DELETE in the normal parlance under all 
				  circumstances rather than trying to out-do our esteemed
President 
				  at being "legally accurate".

	Constructive Criticism #8.  Show some respect for standards rather
than simply bashing others
				  (e.g. Microsoft) for not following them (i.e., why rename
"/etc/fstab"
				  to "/etc/vfstab" for no good reason?).

	Constructive Criticism #9.  Figure out how to have the "command
tool" window scroll correctly.
				  (Hint: new text should automatically scroll it to the bottom
and you
				  should not be able to scroll past the last line of text.) 
[BONUS
				  points are awarded "command tool" for having a large
scrollback buffer.]

.
.
.

	And that's the short list.  Nitpicky?  Perhaps.  That's like saying
that the latency in a particular router isn't "that long".  Add that
router to the other eight or nine hops and it all adds up.

> I will gladly take a discussion off-line. I feel that this slightly
> "infected" argument leads nowhere.

	Perhaps not.  I find it quite educational to read the overarching
"reasons" why people think SUN's odd way of doing things is
"better".  I've never had so many people piss on me and tell me it's
raining before. 

> Let me also be so blunt as to recommend one or more of our Sysadmin
> classes, geared at administering Suns. I feel that the operation of
> your systems may benefit from it.

	Been there, done that didn't get a T-shirt.

			Yours in Christ,
			      Don


