Gregg E Economou wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> >> Now you're being a troll.  A system should NEVER need a reboot.

> >       "NEVER need a reboot"... They all need to be rebooted or they
> >slowly grind to a halt over time.  "/tmp" starts to fill, you get a

> you dont need to reboot to clear tmp!

	You have "need" confused with 'feel like it'.

> if you do, then you obviously should
> read the man page for rm!

	On a SUN reboot takes care of that.  No fuss, no muss.

> >few "defunct" processes hanging around not paying rent, you've

> I think weve all been through the fact that you dont need to reboot to
> kill zombies, either.

	As well as the fact that a reboot will clear them right out without
any problem.

> >collected a few 'Stale NFS file handles' along the way, a tape drive

> You dont need to reboot to clear that up!

	Of course not. It is, of course, less keystrokes and since it
doesn't hurt anything --- why not.

> >or CDROM player has hiccupped and stopped communicating with the

> thats a hardware problem, nothign to do with UNIX. If you have flaky hardware,
> rebooting wont fix it.

	Sure it does.  

	Hardware not found.
	<reboot>
	Oh, yeah there is a CD player attached.

> >workstation, etc...  Embrace the reboot and even the "maintenance
> >reboot" via "cron".  Your system will love you for it and you'll
> >have more of your valuable time free to solve real problems.

> like, 'why does my system require a reboot' ?

	Again you're confusing feel like rebooting with have to reboot.

> >       I'll never understand some people's aversion to something as simple
> >as a reboot.  Shit; if the user has gone home for the day and I need
> >to stop and restart the automounter or something similar, sometimes
> >I'll just "rsh" over the "reboot" command rather than bother with
> >looking up the PID, killing it with a "TERM", etc...  I'm not going
> >for any kind of time up record on my systems.  Longest uninterrupted
> >stretch of uptime is not what interests my users.  Highest
> >percentage of usable uptime during their shift is.

> It's just a very ignorant way to do things.

	An interesting, though ultimately simpleminded, opinion.  Nothing
more.  Unless you have some strange, twisted emotional interest in
seeing how long you can go between reboots, a reboot will serve the
purpose of restarting the automounter as easy as doing it manually. 
Bottom line --- if no one is using the system, a reboot doesn't hurt
a flipping thing.  Does automount get started -- YES.  Objective
achieved.

> Acceptance of ignorant
> methods breeds acceptance of ignorance in general- acceptance of ignorance
> breeds mediocrity and ultimately hurts us all.
> Please dont contibute to this, the folks in redmond and all their smiling
> ignorant customers are contributing to this enough!
> Ah, a reboot is fine.

	On an idle system it hurts nothing.

> Ah, big deal you lost your data.

	On an idle system there is no one logged in with any data to lose.

> Ah, jeez, do we really need to prove this new system's correctness? they
> can just fix it with a reboot if it tanks.

	Reboot restarts everything.  Why take the time out from important
problems to restart a daemon manually.

> Ah, whoopee the big machine just crashed. reboot it.

	Depends on why it "crashed".

> Ah, phooey we lost a few transactions.

	If it "crashed", they're already gone.  You had better reboot it or
else it will sit at the boot prompt all day doing nothing.

> Ah, fudge, a plane just nose-dived into a mini-mall because that transaction
> was lost.
> Oh well, those passengers just need to be rebooted, right?

	What does an airplane have to do with a simple UNIX workstation?

			
			      Don


