Joel Garry wrote:

> >       I love rebooting the machine!  It's like bringing in fresh laundry
> >from the line.  Everything is so clean it's almost like it was new
> >again.

> >       Rule To Live By:  If you're doing everything right but the machine
> >still isn't behaving, reboot.  Problem solved almost every time
> >unless it's hardware.  Time wasted going down blind alleys - 0
> >seconds.

> Someday you are going to work in an environment of 7/24 and this will come
> back to bite you.

	When I work in a shop that's not "7/24 [sic]", I work accordingly.
	When I work in a shop that is, I also work accordingly.

	Like the BORG, I can adapt.

	EX:  Workstation on someone's desk.  That 'someone' logged out and
went home at 1630 on Friday.  They won't be back until 0800 on
Monday.  I have yet to see someone give a good reason why me logging
into that workstation on Saturday, noticing that it's been up for
over 100 days and firing off a "reboot" is a 'bad idea'.  Besides
losing "bragging rights" in the "who's workstations have been up the
longest" contest, all I've got is a machine that's cleaned up all
its trash and is ready to run even longer.  The most "compelling"
reason I've heard so far is "it's not necessary".

	I get the impression that if I said, "I wear sneakers on the
boardwalk to avoid splinters", I'd receive a bunch of condescending
follow-ups asking why my boardwalk had so many splinters and why I
was too stupid to get rid of them.

			      Don


