> Cool concept...
> Is this a full power down, where a cold boot brings things back,
> or is this a very low power hibernation mode, where the processor
> slows to practically nothing, spins down disks etc., but does not
> actually have to do a cold boot? I could see hibernation, as not
> too hard to understand how to do, but saving and restoring ALL
> user context can be extremely complex. If Sun has that, they
> desreve kudos.

It really powers down the machine. All system state are stored on the
disk, which the kernel finds the next time the machine is booted.

A fried of mine has an uptime of several months, even though he powers 
the machine down every night because of noisy fan. :-)

Of course, all drivers need to be able to save their internal state,
so if you have (incomplete) 3'rd party drivers you might not be able
to suspend the system.

