In article <36062A0E.3494F2D4@ave.ac.agit.de>, Roland Ziegler <zi@ave.ac.agit.de> writes:
:Can a VAX 4000-200 be upgraded?

  Yes, it can.  The usual approach is the VAX 4000 model 100 series, 
  which support connections to an external Q-bus enclosure, such as 
  your existing BA215 box.  (The BA215 is the six-slot variant of the 
  more-common BA213 twelve-slot box, so your box was probably upgraded 
  from a MicroVAX 3300 system.)  The VAX 4000 model 100 series then 
  sits on top of the existing BA215.  There are a variety of members
  in the VAX 4000 model 100 series, with the models 106A and 108 being 
  the most recent...  (Circa 32 to 38 VUPs.)

:The 4000-200 has a KA660 CPU module, with on-board DSSI controller,
:Ethernet and memory controller and is mounted in a BA 215 box.

  Circa 5 VUPs.

:I would prefer to keep the system box and just swap the CPU module (and
:maybe the memory boards) to a newer 4000 model.

  The Q-bus Q22/CD doesn't really support the speeds that a processor
  faster than the KA660 Spitfire module will need here -- this is the
  reason why the faster systems have gone to approaches other than a
  board-swap for a faster Q-bus (Q22/CD) processor module...

  What Q-bus widgets do you have in the BA215?

:Some of the few articles in the news group archives on this subject seem
:to point out that the BA 215 Q-bus backplane could be incompatible with
:later VAX 4000 CPU modules.

  Later VAX 4000 series processor modules require the BA440 series
  enclosure, with a seperate bus for connections between the processor
  memory, and related widgets -- the Q-bus became even more of an I/O
  bus.  The BA213, BA215, and BA430 enclosures are supported by the 
  KA660 Spitfire module (used in the VAX 4000 model 200).  The BA440 
  enclosure is not supported.

  The VAX 4000 model 50 (VAXbrick) was available as an upgrade for the
  BA2xx series for a while.  The VAXbrick was an enclosure that contained
  the VAX processor, memory, and I/O.  The enclosure slid into the same
  Q-bus slots that typically contained the processor and memory.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
