On Mon, 21 Sep 1998, Jim Mellander wrote:
[...]
>How about a directory named `cat /etc/shadow` (including the back-quotes).
>When I run cd "`cat /etc/shadow`" on Solaris 2.6 I get:

Putting double quotes (") around a variable substitution prevents further
interpretation of the variable value by the shell.  Thus, if you had
a file:

	/tmp/`cat /etc/shadow`

(including the backtics in the name) and did the following:

	find /tmp -print \
	|	while read fs
		do
			ds="`dirname \"$fs\"`"
			fn="`basename \"$fs\"`"
			cd "$ds" && rm -f "$fn"
		done

Note also that a file of the name:

	/tmp/`cat /etc/shadow`

would actually refer to a file with the name:

	shadow`

located in the directory:

	etc

which is a subdirectory of the directory named:

	`cat 

located in /tmp.  So the above script would be performing the equivalent
of:

	cd '/tmp/`cat /etc' && rm -f 'shadow`'

-- 
