Chris wrote:
> 
> Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> 
> >
> > Actually the headers are very usefull since they make it
> > easy for gcc to run on Solaris or any other 3rd party
> > compiler. If they were supplied for example with the
> > compiler then you would probably have to by Sun's compiler
> > even if you wanted to run gcc, so they are really usefull.
> > > >
> 
> Ok, but it still doesn't answer the question - why don't Sun ship a
> compiler with the OS ? - seems a bit penny pinching. As a comparison,
> have you seen the amount of added value that ships with DU4 ?.
> 
> I assume some people will want to do kernel rebuilds - is there some
> special procedure to allow this without a compiler present, or is all
> the tuning / selection of options done via scripts at boot time ?...
> 


Sun origionally included a very basic K&R C compiler 
with SunOS because you needed it to re-build the 
kernel.  

With Solaris you don't need a compiler to re-build the 
kernel. So Sun stopped shipping a C Compiler, you could 
also argue that Sun wanted to make money out of the 
compiler. Since then Sun has spent large amounts 
of money developing the Compiler/Workshop suite 
of tools to an extent where they have consistently 
won awards from UNIX Review etc for the best UNIX 
compiler suite. Of course much of this is pretty 
subjective but much of the development was funded 
by the revenues Sun has made from the compilers. 

Of course including the headers in the OS means 
that if you want a free but less functional 
compiler/tool suite then you can use gcc. 

Solaris supports dynamically loadable and unloadable 
modules so you don't need to make a new kernel to add 
say a SCSI driver. 

As far as tuning is concerned again these options 
are either dynamic or are configured at boot 
time. For example one can change say the 
TCP Keep Alive dynamically. 


