On Mon, 21 Sep 1998, Jim Mellander wrote:
[...]
> Actually, my example was a little weak, it was just for demo purposes. 
> You could just as well use a single word command surrounded by
> backticks.  bash seems to handle the cd correctly, but other unix shells
> don't, for instance under AIX:
> 
> cd "`my_prog`"
> 
> will run my_prog before attempting the cd

Yeah, but the following two are not equivalent (which I believe is where
your misunderstanding is:

	cd "`my_prog`"

	x='`my_prog`'
	cd "$x"

In the second example, $x will be expanded, but the expansion will not
then be interpreted.  There is *NO CHANCE* that the value of $x could
cause unexpected side effects by executing arbitrary shell commands,
even if the value of $x contains double quotes ("), single quotes ('),
backtics (`), or other shell metacharacters.

-- 
