In article <36067E43.598E55E1@llnl.gov>,
Jim Mellander  <mellander1@llnl.gov> wrote:
>How about a directory named `cat /etc/shadow` (including the
>back-quotes).  When I run cd "`cat /etc/shadow`" on Solaris 2.6 I get:
>
>
><contents of /etc/shadow> not found.

Still not a problem:

Bourne shell:

$ foo='`cat /etc/shadow`'
$ cd $foo
`cat: does not exist
$ cd "$foo"
`cat /etc/shadow`: does not exist

Korn shell:

$ foo='`cat /etc/shadow`'
$ cd $foo
ksh: cd: bad substitution
$ cd "$foo"
ksh: `cat /etc/shadow`:  not found

As you can see, the only processing done on the result of variable
interpolation is parsing of argument delimiters ($IFS), and only when the
substitution isn't inside a quoted string.  Backticks, semicolons, pipes,
and redirection are recognized before interpolation.

There *are* some scripts that can be susceptible to problems like these.
Mainly, they involve use of "eval", "sh -c <command>" or some similar
mechanism to construct a whole new command line (Perl scripts using
system() are often susceptible).  But the script in this thread is not like
that.

