Jim Mellander wrote:
> 
> Chris Ulrich wrote:
> >
> >   Why not just turn the sticky bit off, run the /tmp sweeper as a
> > normal UID, and then set the sticky bit after the sweeper runs?
> > Then the badguy can delete anything in /tmp, but they can't make
> > root delete random files anywhere on the filesystem.
> >
> 
> <snip>
> 
> The problem here is that /tmp is set for a perm of 1777, which means
> that only root & the owner of the file can delete it.  But this
> discussion gave me an idea:
> 
> Why not fork off a child, change the EUID to the owner of the file, and
> delete the file as the owner.  If the perms on system directories are
> set correctly,  even a symlink atack would fail.  This is massively
> wasteful of system resources (1 fork/file), but since the /tmp sweep is
> usually run in the wee hours of the morning, that wouldn't be so bad.
> Some Unices let you switch back & forth between EUID & Real UID, so this
> may not apply.
> 
> This may actually be secure, if the sweeper skips files owned by root.


More musings:

If the program decides to delete a file, change the owner of the file to
'nobody' (remember we're running as root here), then delete the file as
'nobody'.  Then this would work even for files owned by root.



