In article <6u67of$cae@web.nmti.com>,
Peter da Silva <peter@baileynm.com> wrote:
>In article <36069B1E.14553C26@llnl.gov>,
>Jim Mellander  <mellander1@llnl.gov> wrote:
>>You're correct.  Thank you for clearing that up.  However, I have yet to
>>see a program to clean /tmp that is not vulnerable to race conditions. 
>
>Sure you have. Any program that changes directory to the victim's before
>checking it. Once it's changed directory, there's no manipulation with
>any path under /tmp that will budge it.

That solves the race condition on the directory, but not on the file
itself.  For instance, between the time that find identifies a file as
being older than 7 days and when the rm is done, the file could be deleted
and replaced by a new one.  This new one will be deleted even though it's
only a few seconds old, rather than more than a week old.

If your goal is to avoid race conditions that can be used as security
holes, you're probably right.  But if your goal is a fully correct /tmp
cleaner, possibilities like this will always exist unless special kernel
support is provided.

-- 
