In article <3606A427.D0341A64@localhost.localdomain>,
Remco Post  <root@localhost.localdomain> wrote:

>Hmm, Would that make VMS a Unix? I think it has an X server, is
>multi-user and I'd inmagine that versions above 5.3-1 (the most recent
>VMS I've ever seen ) could do NFS and NIS if you'd really want that. 

VMS is very much a UNIX.  Not only X, but also UCX.  C is also the
dominant language on VMS.  ANSI C, that is, so you still have all the
UNIX screw-ups like buffer overruns, and the like.  Also, it's POSIX
(which, admit it or not, means UNIX).  Heck, OpenVMS 7 even has pipes!

>> It's really a shame that the last pure OS has to decided to go UNIX.
>> When MacOS X is released there will be literally no mainstream OS
>> which is not UNIX.  ;-(
>> 
>As mentioned, there still is windows.

Windows, in particular, NT, is still way too UNIX for my tatstes.
Again, C is by far the dominant (and, unlike VMS, the only viable)
language.  POSIX.  You have pipes, redirection, etc.  There are a few
particularly nasty UNIX'isms in Windows, such as the ability to embed
spaces and other bad characters in filenames, and the same general
anarchy in filename quoting and the like...

I have a hard time calling an OS on which "bash" compiles out of the
box, to be not UNIX.

>Well, college textbook authors have no access to the VMS source code, so
>I'd think it's rather hard to show examples from VMS. Anyway UNIX is
>very easy to understand, and a good base to start studying other OS's.

I'm not sure that source code is the issue.  If you're going to
illustrate OS functionality with source code (and normally it's
psudo-code) you can figure out what most if it is; it's not as if it's
hard to divine what the OS is doing.

I think you have the reason in that it's easy to understand; UNIX does
so ridiculously little that you can study it in a semester, where
something like MVS or VMS (the non-UNIX parts ;-) would take a lot
longer.

>Anyway, the few textbooks I own are explicetly about Unix, or describe
                                                               ^^^^^^^^
>an abstract OS, this is just very much like Unix. VMS is obviously not
 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>that populair, and actually, not very much in use at universities in
>Europe (afaik). It not being available for the cheapest of the cheapest
>hw (intel box), might have something to do with that.

I very strongly object to the idea that an abstract OS is UNIX.  The
only way that is true, is if you consider an abstract OS to be the
absolute minimum of what is required to run programs (that's what UNIX
is).  But it is by no means an "abstract" OS.  It's just a cheap
imitation of an OS.  ;-)

What I'm really concerned about, is all of the college kids leaving
school thinking UNIX is the definition of "high end" and "stability"
(in reality, it's quite the opposite) and having no idea about all of
the great features which exist(ed) on mainframe OS'es.  Since these are
the people who will be designing future OS'es, they're just going to
keep re-designing UNIX (and calling it "NT" or something else) and
we're never going to progress pass this embarrassment which has
literally set the computer industry back 30 years...

Regards,

Terry Murphy



