> 
> Jim Mellander <mellander1@llnl.gov> wrote:
> >I would be interested in hearing from others about this topic.  Without
> >some method of ensuring that swapping won't take place between
> >identification of the file to be deleted & the actual system call to
> >delete the file, cleaning /tmp as root is inherantly insecure.
> 

Hmm. I've never felt the need to try this, but couldn't you (as root)
get a process listing (in a loop, until exit condition), calling
kill -STOP on all the processes until only a few 'important' processes
are running (say all root-owned processes), then do the find & rm,
then call kill -CONT on the list of processes you STOPped.

Then no user process could get between the find and the rm. Maybe
call STOP on cron from within the script as well, to prevent the user
spawning new programs in the middle of a long find.

Sounds like waaay overkill to me, but possible...

