> 
> 	The PC user would have to stick with an IP address that is on his
> LAN segment or else he couldn't get through the gateway.  This
> illegal address would either resolve back to nothing or resolve back
> to some other computer on the PC's LAN.  If it resolves back to a
> machine with a DNS entry, the true DNS domain of the PC will be in
> the e-mail header.  On a UNIX machine the "root" user can configure
> "sendmail.cf" to pass the name of the machine and the domain so they
> have a more credible chance of passing for whoever they want.

You didnt answer the question Thomas asked: "How would that differ
from telnetting to port 25?".

It doesn't matter what you do to configure the sendmail.cf file, in
the end, it connects to another machine on port 25 and sends some
information (cleartext, look at RFC 821 for details) to the other
machine. The exact same information can be sent using the standard
"telnet" program bundeled with Windows. There is no was the receieving 
end can figure out wether it's a windows machine or a Unix box sending 
the data.

Can't you just admit that you are wrong on this issue?

