Steve Tate wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool (oldno7@home.com) wrote:
> > Steve Tate wrote:

> > > And you're totally missing the point here.  On a properly set up
> > > environment of Unix workstations, a root user on an individual
> > > workstation can NOT "su -" to another user and read their mail.

> >       Perhaps I'm mistaken but I don't recall anything like Kerberos
> > being an intrinsic part of UNIX.  You're making a lot of pie in the
> > sky assumptions about the UNIX network running Kerberos and the PCs
> > all having third party software installed.  Drop a UNIX CDROM into
> > the workstation's drive and do the install and then drop a Windows
> > CDROM into the PC's drive and do the install.

> I worked in an environment about 8 years ago where it was a pretty
> standard distributed setup, using mostly DECStations running Ultrix,
> with Kerberos-based security and AFS for distributed files.  That was
> pretty standard 8 years ago.  I imagine that all the commercial
> Unix'es have such things now.
> 
> > Now feel free to even
> > put a "+" in the "/etc/hosts.equiv" file on the server.  Finally,
> > tell me how the so-called "root" user on the PC is going to get into
> > the server without the root password (I think we all already know
> > how the UNIX user with root would do it).
> 
> Very, very, very easily, if they have the right software.

	There's the key -- "if they have the right software".  This is not
a fault or a security hole in Windows.  Anyone can get into a server
"if they have the right software".

> Look, in this situation, and the one you've described in which a root
> user reads someone else's email, the security problem you describe is
> at the server's end.  Root on a Unix machine can exploit that, and ANY
> user on a PC can exploit that.

	"If they have the right software" which is not a part of Windows.

> Basically, your workstation is asking the server for some files, and
> the server says "ok, who are you?", and the workstation says "Don
> Kool", and the server says "Duuuuuhhhh.... ok, I guess I'll just take
> your word for it, even though you haven't actually been authenticated
> with me."

	How does the PC "ask the server for some files" with a standard
load of Windows?  How does it get them from the server if the server
isn't runnning PCNFSD or SAMBA?

> So if you're worried about root access on the workstation, it's
> because your SERVER is not set up properly.  And a Windows box can
> exploit that weakness just as easily as root on a Unix machine.

	I'm not worried about it.  Users don't get root.

> >       I'll go out on a limb here and say; They can't.  They can't do what
> > a root user on a UNIX workstation can do with ease.

> They most certainly can.  All they have to use in your situation is
> get one of the many programs out there for allowing a PC to mount NFS
> volumes.

	And all a UNIX user has to do is walk up to the server and insert
an OS CDROM.  If you allow the user to have and to load any software
they please, nothing is secure.

> >       Unless some idiot has left the server wide open by putting a "+" in
> > the "/etc/hosts.equiv" or "/.rhosts".  Even then only the user with
> > root on the UNIX workstation could get in, not the user with
> > so-called "root" on a PC.

> Of course they can -- just run a PC version of "rlogin".

	I must have missed that program on the Windows 95/98 install disk.

> > (BTW; loading third party software
> > doesn't count because it is no different than a user walking up to
> > the server with an OS CDROM.  If you're going to assume that the
> > user can load anything they want on the PC, you've got to assume
> > they can load anything they want on the UNIX side as well.)

> No, they can't do anything on the server.  They can load whatever they
> want to on their Windows-based PC.  However, if the user isn't root,
> you can set up either a PC or a workstation to boot ONLY an approved
> version of the operating system.  That gives you security.  And it
> gives you security that it is impossible to acheive with a Windows
> box.

	How are you going to set up the server to not boot from the OS
CDROM?  You reboot an SGI box and click on "Stop for Maintenance". 
Click on "Install System Software" and it asks you which CDROM to
use.  Load the OS CDROM, click on "OK" and you're in.

	Users with access to software may be dangerous but not users with
just Windows.  All the things you describe require the user to have
extra software above and beyond the Windows operating system.  If my
PC users were allowed to load anything they want without going to
jail, I would just pull the CDROMs and floppy drives out of all the
PCs and be done with it.

