One thing I got out of the talks at the last fall DECUS was that
Wildfire is in fact a NUMA machine, but with the nonuniformity
almost down to zero. It can be programmed as a large SMP, but
SMP doesn't scale too well at those multiplicities. Rather what
VMS will do (and what Sun has partly done) is to split it into
several machines and have them communicate. The VMS Galaxy
is vastly more dynamic than anything Sun has, and VMS has been
for years alone in using a DLM to control device access (though
TruCluster for Dec Unix may now do it also). Were this not so,
Sun would not be so insistent in the SCSI committees on adding
SCSI commands to reserve and release devices persistently; it's
the only way they have to do reliable work. VMS doesn't and never
has needed such kludgery...


