Rev. Don Kool (oldno7@home.com) wrote:

> 	There's the key -- "if they have the right software".  This is not
> a fault or a security hole in Windows.  Anyone can get into a server
> "if they have the right software".

I'm getting pretty tired of this argument -- you really just don't
seem to get the point.  Yes, of course you have to have the right
software.  I think it's pretty irrelevant whether it comes loaded with
the operating system or not.  Windows is inherently insecure because
it gives users full access to the machine.  Unix is not.  Being
paranoid about Unix users by not letting them have root, and yet not
being paranoid about Windows users is just plain silly.

And no, not "anyone" can get into a server "if they have the right
software."  On a properly configured environment, only those
specifically authorized to get into the server can get into the
server.

> 	And all a UNIX user has to do is walk up to the server and insert
> an OS CDROM.  If you allow the user to have and to load any software
> they please, nothing is secure.

You let your users have physical access to the servers?  And yet
you're worried about giving them root access on their own machines?
You really need a more consistent security policy...

> 	How are you going to set up the server to not boot from the OS
> CDROM?  You reboot an SGI box and click on "Stop for Maintenance". 
> Click on "Install System Software" and it asks you which CDROM to
> use.  Load the OS CDROM, click on "OK" and you're in.

Don't know about an SGI box, but on a Sun, DEC, or yes even a PC, you
can set it up so that it ONLY boots off the hard disk (on which you
have properly installed the operating system).  Unless they physically
open up the box and an wipe out the flash memory (killing the BIOS
password) or unless they open up the box and swap hard drives, then
you're totally safe.  And if that's a concern get boxes that lock.

Bottom line is this:  you can make Unix secure.  You can't make
Windows secure.  You're worried about Unix root users and not Windows
users.   That's just silly.

--
