This whole Y2K frenzy has really got to stop somewhere. There is no 
real date conprehension in unix. unix (most all of them, there is always
the oddball exception somewhere) has one concept of time: the number of
seconds since 1 jan 1970, stored in 31 bits. We've got just under 40 years
left on the clock. Sure, there are plenty of user programs that deal with
dates- on many older BSD systems for example the 'date' command only digs
2 digits. But the system itself keeps track of a signed 4-byte integer of
seconds since '70. The user programs are not directly part of UNIX itself-
theyre in the distribution, but they can be replaced wihtout replacing the
system itself. 
There is no intrinsic unix dependency on Y2K. 
