"Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> writes:

> > If "/tmp" fills up, write a cron job that cleans out files older than a
> > certain date.  Rebooting because /tmp fills up is an indication of either
> > a lack of knowledge of UNIX, or just plain laziness!
> 
> echo '0 2 * * 6 find /tmp -atime +7 -exec rm {} \; >
> /var/adm/remlist.txt' >> /var/spool/cron/crontabs/root
> 
> echo "0 2 * * 6 reboot" >> /var/spool/cron/crontabs/root
> 
> 	Notice the difference in the number of keystrokes required?
> 

From "man crontab":

    The following options are supported:

     -e        edit a copy of the current user's  crontab   file,
               or  creates an empty file to edit if crontab  does
               not exist. When editing is complete, the  file  is
               installed  as the user's crontab  file. If a user-
               name is given, the specified user's crontab   file
               is  edited, rather than the current user's crontab
               file; this may only be done by a  super-user.  The
               environment  variable EDITOR determines which edi-
               tor is invoked with the  -e  option.  The  default
               editor is ed(1). Note that all crontab jobs should
               be submitted using crontab ; you  should  not  add
               jobs  by  just  editing  the crontab  file because
               cron  will not be aware of changes made this way.

Read and understand the last sentence.
