  The R80, RM80, and RA80 drives all had similar HDA hardware, but each 
  had different host interface electronics.

  I can't say I've ever heard of anyone using the IDC controller with
  SMD drives -- the only drives I have ever seen used with the IDC were 
  the RL02 and the R80...

  The UDA50 was the typical DEC-provided choice for accessing storage 
  hardware on UNIBUS systems, allowing connections to the RA7x, RA8x,
  and RA9x series disk drives. 

  I would tend to expect that there are at least a few third-party UNIBUS 
  controllers for SCSI and for various other storage widgets...

	--

  I will mention that the VAX-11/730, VAX-11/725, and MicroVAX I series
  systems are likely the slowest VAX systems ever shipped...  Even the
  venerable VAX-11/750 and MicroVAX II systems will handily outperform 
  the VAX-11/730 system.

	--

  I will suggest that you will want to acquire one of the DCL command
  procedures that allows you to create the VAX-11/730 TU58 console cassette 
  with the boot files in the correct order.  Placing the files on the TU58 
  in the default alphanumeric order makes for extremely slow bootstraps. 
  (Having files on the TU58 in the correct order makes for slow bootstraps.)

