Scott Collins wrote:
> 
> >>I would be interested in hearing from others about this topic.  Without
> >>some method of ensuring that swapping won't take place between
> >>identification of the file to be deleted & the actual system call to
> >>delete the file, cleaning /tmp as root is inherantly insecure.
> >
> >It seems an easy solution to this problem would be simply to chmod 700
> >/tmp as your script begins, then chmod 1777 before it exits.
> >
> >Users complaining about not being able to access /tmp for the small amount
> >of time this would go on for would need to be educated about the correct
> >use of /tmp.
> >
> 
> Doing this could crash many different programs that might be using or need to
> use /tmp.  I don't think there is any one best answer.  Depending on the purpose
> of the system and what type of users are using it, you may need one solution
> over another.  If it is a database server with very few users except DBA's and
> SA's, you may well be fine with the "find" solution.  A muli-user or development
> environment may require a totally different approach.
> 
> Bottom line, there is no perfect solution.  Only the best solution for a given
> environment.
> 
> --------------------------------------
> Scott Collins
> UNIX/SAP Systems Manager
> scott.collinsNO_SPAM@mindless.com
> (remove "NO_SPAM" to contact by mail)
> --------------------------------------


I tend to agree with Scott - there are many 24X7 shops where even a
short interruption in the access to /tmp could cause problems.  One idea
I have is to write a custom C program and wrap the relevant sections
(identification of file to delete  to  actual rm the file) with a call
to set the job to realtime priority (AFAIK uninterruptable), then back
to normal priority.   Any comments?



