Thomas Tornblom wrote:
> "Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> writes:

> > > Just
> > > telnet to port 25 and send as many forged mails as you like. Hell, you
> > > don't even have to be root on a Unix box to do that.
> >
> >       While the user on the PC could do that, their hostname (or at least
> > their IP if reverse addressing isn't set up correctly) would still
> > most likely appear in the header.  Of course the user with root on
> > the workstation can again do much worse because they can modify the
> > "sendmail.cf" file to their hearts content and go about seriously
> > spoofing mail.  Thanks for reminding me of another reason that
> > giving users root on a UNIX workstation is much worse than simply
> > having a PC on your network.
> 
> How would that differ from telnetting to port 25? Does the remote
> system use some kind of ESP to determine that it really is a sendmail
> sitting on the other end instead of telnet on a PC?
> 
> The PC-user can also, without anything stopping them, change IP address
> and he/she wouldn't even have to be root.

	The PC user would have to stick with an IP address that is on his
LAN segment or else he couldn't get through the gateway.  This
illegal address would either resolve back to nothing or resolve back
to some other computer on the PC's LAN.  If it resolves back to a
machine with a DNS entry, the true DNS domain of the PC will be in
the e-mail header.  On a UNIX machine the "root" user can configure
"sendmail.cf" to pass the name of the machine and the domain so they
have a more credible chance of passing for whoever they want.

			      Don


