>
>I tend to agree with Scott - there are many 24X7 shops where even a
>short interruption in the access to /tmp could cause problems.  One idea
>I have is to write a custom C program and wrap the relevant sections
>(identification of file to delete  to  actual rm the file) with a call
>to set the job to realtime priority (AFAIK uninterruptable), then back
>to normal priority.   Any comments?

I like this idea.  The portability of realtime extensions could prove
problematic, but I think it is an interesting approach as any type of
scripting or cron job spawned from a shell has potential pitfalls no
matter how much checking is done within a script.  If /tmp needs a lot
of attention, a C program is probably the best bet.

