"Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> wrote:
| [...]
| 	I usually run a "find" and have it remove everything with an
| "atime" over 7 days.  If that doesn't clear enough, I increase the
| size of "/tmp".  (Ooops... that's right... can't do that on a SUN
| while it's running.  Thanks SUN!)

I don't follow you.

You could easily create a swap file and have it added to the "pool" while
the system is running, and if /tmp is mounted onto swap you will have
effectively increased the size of /tmp while the system is running and
you could reclaim the space later [after a reboot :-) ] (unlike, say,
AIX, which once you've increased a logical volume with smit I'm unaware
of any way to reduce its size at some future time).

An "out of the box" Solaris 2.6 install has this entry in /etc/vfstab:

    #device       device         mount    FS    fsck    mount   mount
    #to mount     to fsck        point    type  pass    at boot options
    #
    [...]
    swap          -              /tmp     tmpfs   -      yes    -

Thad



