>
>
>There must be a zillion scripts out there, but here is one that you could kick
>off with a crontab entry.  Run it every day.
>
>find /tmp -type f -atime +7 -exec rm {} \;
>
>That will find all ordinary files in /tmp that are more than 7 days old and
>remove them.
>
>Change the number of days to anything you like, but it is a good idea to leave
>tmp files there for at least a little while (someone may be using them).
>
>

Only problem is that it make is possible for any user to delete any file 
on your system.

mkdir /tmp/foo/bar/
touch /tmp/foo/bar/passwd

In the time between when 'find /tmp -type f -atime +7 -exec rm {} \;' 
finds /tmp/foo/bar/passwd, and 'rm' removes  /tmp/foo/bar/passwd
you do (very fast here:)

mv /tmp/foo/bar/bar /tmp/foo/foo
ln -s /etc /tmp/foo/bar

Then 'rm' comes along and removes your /etc/passwd.

-- 
-janfrode



