>> mv /tmp/foo/bar/bar /tmp/foo/foo
>> ln -s /etc /tmp/foo/bar

Sneaky, though it's a pretty damn hard hole to abuse. You have to:

	create the directory, and the file
	let it age 7 days
	and catch the "find" right at the point you're doing the remove.

It's a very "noisy" hole to make use of... you'd have to create a whole
bunch of files like this, and have a background job sitting there watching
for them to start being deleted, and then quickly do the relinks and hope
you catch find at just the right time. And if you miss you'll have left
all sorts of tracks (at least with my script, since it logs what's deleted).

