On Fri, 18 Sep 1998 12:36:24 GMT, Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:
>Jan-Frode Myklebust wrote:
>> 
>> >find /tmp -type f -atime +7 -exec rm {} \;
>> >
>> 
>> Only problem is that it make is possible for any user to delete any file
>> on your system.
>> 
>> mkdir /tmp/foo/bar/
>> touch /tmp/foo/bar/passwd
>> 
>> In the time between when 'find /tmp -type f -atime +7 -exec rm {} \;'
>> finds /tmp/foo/bar/passwd, and 'rm' removes  /tmp/foo/bar/passwd
>> you do (very fast here:)
>> 
>> mv /tmp/foo/bar/bar /tmp/foo/foo
>> ln -s /etc /tmp/foo/bar
>> 
>> Then 'rm' comes along and removes your /etc/passwd.
>
>	In that case, "find" would remove the symbolic link.  If it were a
>hard link, "find" would lower the link count by one.  In either
>event, the file in "/etc" would remain intact.
>

'find' doesn't remove anything, it finds a real file called
/tmp/foo/bar/passwd and then calls 'rm' to remove it. The security risk is
that in the time between when 'find' finds and 'rm' removes, one can move
away /tmp/foo/bar/bar, and create a link from /tmp/foo/bar to /etc. Then
'rm' will remove your /etc/passwd, or whatever file you wanted rm'd.


-janfrode



