Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> My apologies BTW I misread the IDEAS report, they
> list 41 Servers, 1 of which is a 10 CPU 8400 6/575
> 39 of which are 4100 5/533 each with 4 CPUS and one
> of which is an Alpha 8400 5/625 with 8 CPUs.

	I think you misread more in this report than
that, or at least some assumptions which you've made
lead to some deceptive conclusions.

>This gives you ~440 taking into account loading CPUs DBMS
> and apps servers but that assumes completely linear
> scalability both for apps and for DBMS.

	This also "assumes" that the apps servers were
loaded at the same capacity as the database server,
which your previous posted data does NOT support this
assumption.  It's equally possible that the apps servers
were lightly loaded (after all, ~100 Alpha CPU's could
likely load an 8400 with 10 CPU's).  Without data showing
the load on the apps servers, it's a little disingenious to
state the need for hundreds of Alpha CPU's for apps server
use as a fact.

	Given that, the addition of a pair of Alpha 8400's
(10 CPU) could bring you right back in range of only
using 194 CPUs compared to Sun's 360, and likely 
still have apps server performance to play with.
And with less memory than you allude, which is another
set of assumptions.  Digital could have used 5-15 8400's
for the apps servers and would have "machine/cpu counts"
much closer to Sun's range.  It's likely that the choice
of the 4100's was because they could get a bunch easily.

	Additionally, your "conversion" of CPU's to
machines, then declaring that so many machines doesn't
make sense... well it doesn't make sense because you're
the one that made the assumption.  If you use, instead of the
slower 4100 servers, faster Turbolasers in their place, you
can reduce the "machines" and still keep the same CPU 
capacity (probably with fewer CPU's, too).

	You've made a lot of claims about what it would take
Digital to put together a solution, but many of those claims
are based on facts not in evidence or on ignorance of Digital's
product set and what you can do with it.  You can't legitimately
make claims about needs of hundreds of servers based on the
benchmarks numbers of ONE database server.  Not that I would
expect (or trust) a Sun person to outline the configuration
of a Digital solution, anyway.

