>Bill Vermillion wrote:
>> Werner Kliewer  <Werner_Kliewer@MPIC.mb.ca> wrote:
>> >Rev. Don Kool wrote:
>
>			[...snip...]
>
>> Hard drive failure is the major problem in today's world - and that
>> is getting more rare.
>
>	And what a pain it is.  Earlier in the year we had an HP HP/UX
>system that had been running for three years straight on the third
>floor.  We needed to move it to the second floor so I did a full
>backup of it and then shut it down properly.  I put it on a cart,
>rolled it to the elevator and then rolled it to its new home on the
>second floor (elapsed time ~10 minutes total).  Once I got it hooked
>back up, I powered it up and both disks (Quantum Fireballs) were
>DOA.  Apparently the heads had parked and got stuck there.  Gotta
>love those backups (and pile of spare disks)!

The Fireballs should never be used in a serious machine.  They are
low-end SCSI drives.  The 'serious' drives from Quantum are the
Atlas series.  Those are the DEC designs they acquired when they
bought the drive and DLT operations from DEC.

Depending on the model of the Fireball - I'm hoping these were SCSI
and not IDE - there was one set that was partial to failure.

It would just stop booting.  The HW tech and my self salvaged a
system that had no backups by buying another drive and swapping the
controller cards. It had to do with drive motor controlling
circuitry.   OTOH - I have had 'industrial strength' drives
seeminly go on forever.  One got so old I decided to push it until
it quit.  It never did - the controller failed - and 15Mbit ESDI
controllers were history.  24x7 for 7 years and 2 months.



