jmfbahciv@aol.com wrote:
: 
: Ok, I read some reference to Linux and Redhat Linux.  Are these
: two different OS implementations or is there a version difference
: between the two?

	Is this a serious question?  I will assume so.
	Linux is the kernel and drivers.  Currently it is
	in beta version 2.0.36 or there abouts, and alpha
	version 2.1.13x something.  Alpha (development versions
	have an odd second level number.  The problem from
	my point of view is that between 2.0.18 and the current
	patchlevel (third number) the has been changes that make
	them in compatable with each other.  When that happened
	it should (in my opinon) have gone to 2.2.x, but didn't.

	Now REDHAT, SLACKWARE, DEBIAN, etc are distributions.  A
	Linux distribution is the kernel plus software to make a
	server or workstation operating system.  The selection of
	the added software, the method of configuring the system,
	etc. are what is different between distributions.  Some
	distributions are server oriented (Slackware), some are
	workstation oriented (Redhat, SuSE).

	The big difference is that there is only one kernel development
	thread (headed by Linus), while there are several distribution
	threads.   That is the power Linux has over the free BSDs, IMO,
	you can set up any type of system you want based on the Linux
	kernel.  The BSDs each have a small group who say this is it,
	take it or leave it, but don't change it.  I believe in power
	through deversity, obviously.

	I've run on and on... so I shall stop.

	--graywolf

	
	 



