	And apparently a Meteorologist as well.  It seems you wear three
hats.  

	In some small shops one guy may handle everything from researching
the product line, to picking the solution, to doing the site survey,
to preparing the site, to receiving the boxes at the loading dock,
to transporting them to the site, to doing the physical
installation, to designing the network, to pulling the network
cables, to assigning the IP, to booting the system, to installing
the software, to providing life cycle support and developing
software.  (Sometimes all of the above amounts to a small business
owner picking up a PC at the local office supply, bringing it back
to the store and plugging it into electrical and phone jacks)  That
does not mean that all of these tasks properly fall into the realm
of "system administration".  Many a programmer has been pushed to do
system administration in their 'spare time'.  It doesn't mean that
programming is system administration.  It merely means that they are
doing two jobs and getting paid for one.  As the job increases in
scope and complexity the company may eventually hire (or assign) a
person to just do system and network administration full time.  As
the task becomes larger even the network duties are eventually split
from the pure system administration ones.

	It's like in the restaurant business.  In a small restaurant, you
have "a chef".  In a large one you have an Executive Chef, a Sous
Chef, a Saute Chef, a Broiler Chef, a Banquet Chef, a Pastry Chef, a
Pantry Chef, etc...  The bigger the job, the more it has to be
broken down into managable pieces.

	And, YES, I used to do both programming and system administration
and I have a very good appreciation for how under appreciated the
system admin part of the job is by most managers who put things like
doing backups on a par with emptying the office trashcan --- a dirty
job but somebody's got to do it.

			      Don


