Steve Tate wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool (oldno7@home.com) wrote:

> >       Begging the question.  The question suggested that a user on a
> > Windows box can "do anything" that a root user on the UNIX
> > workstation can do.  A user on a Windows box cannot "su -" to
> > another user and read their (unencrypted) mail.  All a user on a
> > Windows box can do is screw up their own box.

> Oh, and one thing I forgot to say in my last posting.  If you have
> your distributed resources set up sloppily enough where root on an
> individual machine can "su -" and read another user's mail, then there
> are programs under windows that will do EXACTLY the same thing ---
> allow you to access the other user's mailbox.   In other words, you'd
> be just like root on your own machine.  But then again, that was the
> original point we were trying to make!

	"There are programs under windows"...  That assumes that those
extra programs are loaded on the Windows machine.  Not quite fair to
arm the Windows users with any third party software you can imagine
and only let the UNIX side use UNIX.  The original comparison was
between a user on a Windows PC and someone with "root" on a UNIX
workstation.  If "/var/spool/mail" on the server is NFS mounted to
"/var/spool/mail" on the workstation, the root UNIX user can "su -"
and read anyone's mail.  The Windows user can't even mount it
because there is no provision in Windows to run NFS.  Shit, Windows
machines can't even send mail without a server to handle it for
them.  Any UNIX machine can run "sendmail".
			
			      Don


