echo "0 2 * * 6 reboot" >> /var/spool/cron/crontabs/root

	Notice the difference in the number of keystrokes required?

> If you have defunct processes, fix your software.  Also make sure your OS
> patches are up to date.

	Management: "Joe Blow wrote this software just before he left the
company five years ago.  We rely on it for everything.  No one has
any idea how to fix it.  We just know we have to run it no matter
what it does to the systems.  You're just going to have to figure a
way to make it work."

	echo 0 2 * * 6 reboot >> /var/spool/cron/crontabs/root

> Unless you've moved filesystems on your server, "Stale NFS file handles"
> are a consequence of user action.

	Or of a sysadmin on the server you're mounting from.

# umount /why/are/these/mountpoint/paths/so/long/stale_filesystem
# mount
remotehost:/they/are/just/as/long/over/here/stale_filesystem  \
/why/are/these/mountpoint/paths/so/long/stale_filesystem

	or

# reboot

	Again, notice the difference in the number of keystrokes required.

> I've never had a tape drive stop communicating unless it has totally failed.
> In this case, it needs to be removed and sent out for repair.  (We've had
> way too many failures, so this is one of the big reasons for bringing a
> system down and rebooting.)

	As I pointed out.

> >>  If you're going to reboot, you had better at least understand why the
> >> problem occurred, or else you're not going to solve it.

> >       I'll never understand some people's aversion to something as simple
> >as a reboot.  Shit; if the user has gone home for the day and I need
> >to stop and restart the automounter or something similar, sometimes
> >I'll just "rsh" over the "reboot" command rather than bother with
