Hi,

> At first blush, you have a DEC PC AXP150 on your hands. You'll need an
> EISA config utility and an Adaptec 1740 with special firmware to get it
> going
AFAIK you don't REALLY need that disk. It's good if you've got it but it
could work without. The 1740 with speacial firmware is a MUST-HAVE for
my understanding. 

> as well as a special power supply.
That's not needed. My original powersupply died and I used a normal PC
power supply. When I have the board lying in front of me, the CPU at the
right, the 2 left power connectors are like in an PC, so a normal supply,
the "black" wires (mass) in the middle (as normal in PCs) works. The right
connector seems to add just another 3 mass and 3 +5v pins, don't know
what for, possibly DEC thought the machine could overload the normal
wires. Mine hasn't so far :)

You also need normal (Fast Page) PS/2 Memory with REAL parity, shouldn't be
too hard to get and it's quite cheap. I currently have 192MB in mine
(4x16 and 4x32 MB), so at least 256MB should be possible. 

If you're planning to run linux on it (what I'm doing) you also need some
special VGA Card. The trick is that it must initialize on it's own, the SRM
console doesn't do it. The ELSA Winner 1000 Twinbus is a card known to do
this and also is the card recommended if you want to run X on it. As long
as you don't know if your machine is running and you're not certain if
your VGA is OK, don't connect keyboard and mouse but connect the serial port
to a terminal / pc with terminal using a nullmodem an 9600,8n1, and you should
see the machine booting.

P.S: when looking for infos, the machine is known as the "Jensen", it's the
first PC with the Alpha architecture so don't expect tooo much speed, but
it easily outperforms a pentium 200 in some tasks (DES for example), although
it was placed into the market to compete with the P75. I'm currently writing
this article on mine, it really gives a nice workstation and it's always nice
to say that you're working on an Alpha :) The 128 Bit RAM-Bus makes it
faster than the 233 Mhz Multias in many applications, BTW.

(:ul8er, r@y



