#Kevin W. Thomas wrote:
#> IRev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

#> >> acceptable alternative to stability (not that brushing your teeth is
#> >> bad!).

#> >       I get the impression that a lot of you guys have systems that take
#> >two days to boot or something.

#> Huh?  Why reboot if it isn't necessary? 

#	Why not?  It only takes a couple of minutes and it cleans out a lot
#of junk.

Hrms... I'm gonna tell a client 'Don't process any credit card transactions for
fifteen minutes while I reboot the system'.  Yeah, right... Every reboot we
do is either on a regular schedule established with the concurrence of teh
client or involves an emergency change control, with a whole bunch of high
muckity-mucks debating whether to sign off on it, asking about potential
cost impact to the client, asking whether it can be delayed until the next
scheduled reboot.  The red tape can be cut through, my boss knows I won't
ask for one without reason, but it's not something done lightly.

Especially when there's no good reason TO do so, I've got systems running
almost a decade out of date on the OS with 470+ days of uptime (AIX 3.2).
Some of my systems are on reboot schedules, some aren't.  Gets messy 
scheduling a reboot when doing so takes an entire Class A network off the
Internet... People get fussy about their proxy servers and firewalls, for some
reason.  Yes, it can be done, yes there are redundant systems, but paranoia is
a job requirement for system administrators... Every time I shutdown a system,
even more so if I have to power it down, I recognize that there is a non-zero
risk it might not come up cleanly.  Hardware failure someplace being the most
likely cause, but other problems do occasionally pop up, we're still getting
our systems up to the OS revisions I'm comfortable with (as in, I prefer to
be at least CLOSE to the current OS, one at most two revs back).

#> The systems don't panic.  Power
#> is stable due to the lack of thunderstorms this year.  I once had a SS20/61
#> that was up for over a year.

#	So have I on many occassions but I never got a cash award or
#anything for it.  As a matter of fact, as soon as I see a
#workstation that's been up that long I put in an "at" job to have it
#reboot (after the user has gone home for the day) just on general
#principles.  Any workstation that's been up that long has just gotta
#need a good flush.  Even if it doesn't, there's no harm done and an
#ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure.

Must be nice to work in a workstation environment.  Actually, I'd probably
hate it, I support workstations but it's much more interesting to be doing
LVM upgrades on a UE5000, or installing a T-600, or other such stuff.  Might
even be getting another V-box and a couple of Sun UE10000's/Cray C-64's in
the next few months.  Oh, and most of the workstations I support CAN be 
rebooted, but doing so removes that system from the use of our 24x7
support teams, so it's done as seldom as possible.  Sure, I built redudancy
in, we DO have PC's with X software that can be used, with duplicate copies
of critical software loaded on my workstation so they can pull an X display
while I fix their workstation, but why muck with it when I don't have to?

#	Look at it another way... 

#	What is it that finally makes even you reboot-a-phobes finally
#sacrifice your run for the record, knock back a shot of bourbon and
#reboot your machines?  We've already established that I'm the only
#one in the world who has ever seen a "defunct" process and had
#neither the time nor the inclination to track it down so it can't be
#that.  I suppose no one in any of your shops ever accidently sends a
#"kill -9" to "automount" instead of a "kill -TERM" and hangs the
#automounter tight.  Of course you never get a user who calls up to
#complain that the executable he's been running a hundred times a
#day, every day for a year is now suddenly dumping core every time he
#tries to execute it even though the code hasn't been changed for 5
#years.  All of you only run well behaved programs with no memory
#leaks.  You never get "process table full" or "swap space below
#10%".  Memory doesn't get fragmented.  None of your systems ever
#just lose track of a SCSI device for no reason and you never have a
#machine just start running slower and slower until it grinds to a
#halt.

What causes me to reboot systems?  Usually hardware or OS upgrades, although
some of my systems ARE on periodic reboot lists, usually every other week,
but we're phasing a lot of those out.  Other than a few SCO boxes that are
on their last legs and scheduled to be phased out, and a Sequent that will
spontaneously reboot (must think it runs NT or something), most of our 
systems don't NEED rebooting.

#	Even so it must be something.  I'd even venture to guess that
#sometimes the need to reboot comes at a less than optimal time.  Now
#I'm all ears if someone wants to tell me how I'm going to break the
#system by taking the average time between forced reboots, dividing
#it in half and creating a "cron" job to do a "maintenance reboot" of
#the system at that time (in the middle of the night when no one is
#using the system).  Since even you guys have to reboot your systems
#eventually, what have I lost by moving the inevitable reboot to a
#time of my choosing rather than having it occur without notice right
#in the middle of something important?  I'm talking about something
#more substantial that "my system was up longer than yours" bragging
#rights here.  After all, we've already taken metrics and know that
#the system usually stays up twice as long as the time I'm asking it
#to run so I may never see a forced reboot during working hours.

For most of my systems, there is NO time when 'nobody' is using the system.
Some systems have scheduled maintenance windows, but I can usually find at
LEAST one user who wants to do something then, they just understand that
they do so at their own risk.  Usually, if I expect a system to be
somewhat idle, backups are running.  Even multiple DLT 7000 drives have
a difficult time with multiple database instances running.  Lot of data,
not much time to store it.  Customers don't understand 'but sir, your
account statements have been archived off to storage, we have to call someone
to get those restored'.

Purely anecdotal, but other than the thrice-cursed Sequent I mentioned above,
the only system I've had corrupt itself without having suffered a major
hardware failure in the last year was my PC at home.  Windows '95 managed
to corrupt the FAT on my second hard drive.  Had to fully reinstall '95
and my applications, my Linux partitions took SOME damage, but the majority of
the system was retained and running after a very brief run through the install
process.  Didn't even lose my user directories, just had to replace what had
been in /usr.

James
