Lets have a look at a /etc/hosts.equiv: (from an HP 10.20)

-rw-r--r--  1 root   sys    61 Jun  5 09:10 /etc/hosts.equiv

The "idiot"  has to be the superuser in the first place (look at
the permissions) and secondly although you don't explicitly say so 
you imply that adding a "+" to /etc/host.equiv will allow world 
access as root. This is simply not true. Root equivilance is only 
checked via /.rhosts not /etc/hosts.equiv. 

From a Solaris 2.6 man page for hosts.equiv:

"To help maintain system security, the /etc/hosts.equiv  file
is  not  checked  when  access is being attempted for super-
user." 



