In article <uQ3M1.661$pb5.8767@nsw.nnrp.telstra.net>, csabah@news.foo.com (Csaba Harangozo) writes:
|> Hi,
|>      I am VMS person ( bliss ) but now I have a U**x shell account ( shudder )
|>      with my ISP. It is Linux by the way. What I'm doing is that I'm trying
|>      to use this "emacs" thing  for editing, posts, etc  with tpu-edt 
|>      emulation  :-) .  So far so good. But whenever I start emacs it puts
|>      a stupid search prompt at bottom of the screen and I have to use a few
|>      Control G  to get rid of it , temporarily though. My question is ( to
|>      people who has the misfortune to use U**x ) that is it possible to
|>      disable this prompting ? ( preferably in the .emacs startup file ).
|>                     Thanks in advance.

Sounds like some piece of hardware (probably your VMS dialout line) is
misconfigured and is doing in-band Xon/Xoff (DC1/DC3 aka
ctrl-Q/Ctrl-S) flow control.  The dialout port sees lots of traffic
from Emacs' screen-redrawing, sends it a Ctrl-S to shut it up, and
since the remote tty isn't doing xon/xoff, Emacs gets the Ctrl-S and
interprets it as a "i-search".

Turn off the xon/xoff flow control. If necessary, get the modem
dialout port (or network pty, if it's a network link) set to use an
"alternate type-ahead buffer", if VMS still uses those.



