In <bpvpvcws9hd.fsf@no.spam> Elias Martenson <no@no.spam> writes:

>That is why you can get rid of the zombie processes by killing the
>parent process. What Doug said, was that if you still get zombie
>processes, after killing the parent ...

Zombies can also occur when the parent hangs on an I/O call that will
never complete.  For example, trying to write to an NFS-mounted file and
the remote server misbehaves.  Or writing to a tape drive that is
broken.  This can make the parent process unkillable, because it's hung
inside the kernel, and won't see the KILL signal until the current
(hung) system call completes.  And its children will remain zombies
forever.

One could say that the inability to abort a system call hung on an I/O
error is an OS bug, though.  I'm not sure that all UNIXes suffer from
this, but SunOS seems to.
-- 
