c.c.eiftj@79.usenet.us.com (Rahul Dhesi) writes:

>One could say that the inability to abort a system call hung on an I/O
>error is an OS bug, though.  I'm not sure that all UNIXes suffer from
>this, but SunOS seems to.


Typically, "short term" I/O is uninterruptable.  Short term is
translated to mean "I/O the kernel is certain to complete".

This includes disk and tape I/O and some I/O operatiosn performed
in close.

NFS I/O may be included in this, and typically is, unless the
"intr" mount option is specified (which didn't really work until
SunOS 4.1.x and is now the default in Solaris 2.x)

A tape drive that never recovers is a hardware problem
(or perhaps a driver bug).  This is where you'd get into
the "reboot territory", thogh on some system you can just
power cycle teh tape drive and give it a different SCSI ID
if you need to keep the sytem up.

Casper

