In article <87ogsfnk5l.fsf@mihalis.ix.netcom.com>, Chris Morgan <mihalis@ix.netcom.com> writes:
|>carlini@krakar.enet.dec.com (Antonio Carlini) writes:
|>
|>> Comparing a VAX 9000 with an x86 box is pointless - the VAX was physically 
|>> bigger if that helps at all :-)
|>
|>But the word is the 9000 wasn't a screamer, and I am using deliberate
|>understatement. The electricity bills would be onerous as well.

Never having used a VAX 9000 in anger, I can only go by what I have read and
what I have been told. I think the problem was that they were big beasts that
cost a packet and needed some amount of care and attention. The top end
VAX 9000-440 could crank out between 150 and 160 VUPs, which put it in
the same bracket as the VAX 6000-660, which came out shortly afterwards.

Upgrading a VAX 6000-610 to a VAX 6000-660 is simply a matter of slotting
in five additional CPUs (if you have enough slots to spare). Going from
a VAX 9000-410 to a VAX 9000-440 involves some serious spanner wielding,
to say nothing of the new cabs and associated plumbing.

In terms of raw CPU power, a VAX 6000-610 (that's one CPU) could crank out
about 40 SPECmarks or 86 TpsA. The first generation Alpha box, a DCE 2000-300,
could crank out 110 tps. An AlphaServer 2100 4/233 could hit 300 tps.
A DEC 3000-900 workstation (1st generation again) hits about 189 SPECint92
and just over 4 SPEC95. An Intel P233 hits about 7 SPEC95.A PII-450 hits about
17 SPEC95.

So putting all that together and waving hands appropriately, I expect that
a PII-450 would be something like 10-20 times faster (integer wise) than a 
VAX 6000-610. Don't forget though, that you could buy a VAX 6000-610 almost
seven years ago which isn't true of the PII :-)


Antonio

