: > my first PC and I've just gotten the upgrades since then rather than
: > switch to Quicken.)  I know that every time a new version of "Money"
: > comes out, it reads the older file format.

: PowerPoint in office 97 does an uncertain job of reading 
: older format PPT files. Sometimes formating gets messed up 
: and any slides that use builds tend to fall over as well.

: Applixware which I also use never seems to have problems 
: with old versions of the files and the old apps generally 
: read the new files fine. 

Applixware is also forwardly compatible.  We have do different,  highly
customized systems that until recently were at different REV levels,
3.2 and 4.3.  The 3.2 system imported docs from the 4.3 system.  Although
formats had changed, by copying a couple of files from the 4.3 system
to the 3.2 system, 3.2 system users were able to read the 4.3 docs
without problem.

Compare that to MS Office,  where IT recently told users NOT to save
docs in Office 97 format because of interoperability problems.

Another nice thing about the Applix format is that it is plain text
based.  My wife lost a ton of MS files transferring data between
computers on what probably was a single bit error.  The data is
unrecoverable,  but in a similar problem with Applix files I was
able to edit the file,  see the error (it was the onlu binary byte
in the file) repair it an recover the entire file intact.


: Older versions of PPT do not read office 97 files but 
: can do using a downloadable converter. The trouble is 
: most people don't have it installed so you cannot 
: assume a file can be read. Of course they could 
: downlaod the convertor but this is simply making 
: the users install more software on their PC's.


