In article <36005D47.317A8AF5@home.com> "Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> writes:
>Kevin W. Thomas wrote:
>
>> >> Frequent reboots and random crashes are not acceptable -- not matter
>> >> what you do to work around the problem.  It wastes time and lowers
>> >> productivity.  Brushing your teeth during the reboot phase isn't an
>> >> acceptable alternative to stability (not that brushing your teeth is
>> >> bad!).
>
>> Huh?  Why reboot if it isn't necessary? 
>
>Any workstation that's been up that long has just gotta
>need a good flush.  Even if it doesn't, there's no harm done and an
>ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure.
>
>What is it that finally makes even you reboot-a-phobes finally
>sacrifice your run for the record ... ?
>Even so it must be something.

In the long run, it is a quality issue.  If a machine NEEDS to be
rebooted, even after over a year, there is still SOMETHING wron,
and the something EVENTUALLY needs to be fixed.  Something is
broken.  Rebooting is admitting that you are not interested in
getting the problem fixed.

Unix admins have had a long term tradition of demanding that all
problems be fixed.  It has lead to superior quality on our systems.
If we start accepting that reboots are a way to fix problems, those
problems proliferate.

It isn't for a record.  It isn't even a matter of professional
pride (okay, this is a lie).  It IS the primary long-term source of
that quality which is present in Unix.

>Since even you guys have to reboot your systems
>eventually, what have I lost by moving the inevitable reboot to a
>time of my choosing rather than having it occur without notice right
>in the middle of something important?

Point well taken.  I agree in this case.  But I don't agree that
allowing poor quality to creep into the system is a good thing.

