"Better, faster, cheaper" doesn't describe Microsoft Windows NT in
comparison to Linux or BSD.  "Standards"?  Microsoft products generally
don't interoperate with other products as well as products from some
other sources interoperate with each other.  Sometimes, lack of
interoperability even afflicts different versions of Microsoft products
(e.g. Microsoft Word file formats across different versions).

Why, if the government is trying to break what it sees as an illegal
monopoly through the court system, does it pay extra money to use
the alleged illegal monopoly's products, when it could be supporting
competitors through its purchasing power?  Such support for the
competitors would be more likely to have an anti-monopoly effect
(including encouraging people to develop more applications that work
with the competitors) than a court battle that is likely to drag on
for a long time with possibly inconclusive result.

