No.  SVR3.

>  Xenix (a MS Unix, by
>                 the way) is esensually a dead end. 

I can't do ASCII art to save my life, so I won't even try, but the line
should sort of run back from Xenix to just after SVR3. IIRC Xenix
compatibility was introduced to SVR3 by AT&T in AT&T Unix/386 3.2.1 (it
may have been 3.2.2; it's been a long time!).  You'll still find entries
for Xenix in SVR4 header files today (/usr/include/sys/stat.h, for
instance) as some degree of Xenix compatibility found its way from SVR3
to SVR4.  I'm also pretty sure that this Xenix/Microsoft connection is
the source of some of the Microsoft copyright comments seen in some
/usr/include headers and one or two executable scripts on SVR4 boxes
even today.

 And, somewhere in there
>                 needs to be a line from BSD to SVR4 as SVR4 has quite a bit
>                 of BSD influence.

Definitely.  When SVR4 was first announced at the System V Release 4
Developers' Conferences, it was said to be the Unix release which would
(re)unify the Berkeley and AT&T System V "threads" of Unix.  Hence
compatibility libraries like /usr/ucblib, hence the reintroduction of
sockets into SVR4 (SVR3 had no sockets), hence symlinks (no symlinks in
SVR3), hence the UFS filesystem  (System V used the System V filesystem,
which is not the same) and so on.

Cheers

