Hang on I think you missed a few important details about 
the R3 benchmarks.

R3 is 3 tier, client, applications servers and DBMS 
servers. The DBMS central instance server is your bottle 
neck since you can add as many app servers as you like
or need to drive the DBMS server.

Digital used one 10 CPU 8400 for the DBMS and 53 
applications servers (4100s I think). Sun used 
One 64 CPU E10000 for the DBMS and 7 other servers 
to run the applications tier.

In order to equal Sun's performance Digital would 
need to put together one central instance running 
one a server with at least 30 CPU's probably a bit 
more and ~160 applications servers. 


>         From there, how do you get that Digital will need
> 162 servers to meet the benchmark levels you quote Sun doing,
> when Digital was half your numbers with only 10 CPU's?  It
> doesn't take 100+ servers to add ~10 CPU's to a Cluster.
> 

I have no idea what number of CPUs Digital would require 
but its more than the number of machines. If each 
4100 application server had 4 CPUs then Digital would 
need >600 CPUs to approach the Sun throughput and 
loading. 

> 
>         Sun also had to integrate multiple servers (you've stated 8)
> for the benchmark, so what's the big deal about needing 2 8400's
> clustered, anyway?
> 

Sun ran one instance of the DBMS, try running SAP on top 
of Oracle Parallel accross 2 or 3 x 8400s and you will 
run into a bunch of major issues.

1. I am not sure its supported by SAP
2. It may not scale
3. You will need very senior management who enjoy 
   risk taking.
4. By the time you have got it working, tuned it
   partitioned the DBMS, paid Oracle more for OPS 
   you will wish you hadn't


