>	"NEVER need a reboot"... They all need to be rebooted or they
>slowly grind to a halt over time.  "/tmp" starts to fill, you get a
>few "defunct" processes hanging around not paying rent, you've
>collected a few 'Stale NFS file handles' along the way, a tape drive
>or CDROM player has hiccupped and stopped communicating with the
>workstation, etc...  Embrace the reboot and even the "maintenance
>reboot" via "cron".  Your system will love you for it and you'll
>have more of your valuable time free to solve real problems.

I've running the following Sun OS's:

4.0.3, 4.1, 4.1.1, 4.1.2, 4.1.3, 4.1.3_U1, 4.1.4, 5.5.1, and 5.6.

I've never seen one "grind to a halt over time".  Performance just doesn't go
downhill over time.

If "/tmp" fills up, write a cron job that cleans out files older than a
certain date.  Rebooting because /tmp fills up is an indication of either
a lack of knowledge of UNIX, or just plain laziness!

If you have defunct processes, fix your software.  Also make sure your OS
patches are up to date.

Unless you've moved filesystems on your server, "Stale NFS file handles"
are a consequence of user action.

I've never had a tape drive stop communicating unless it has totally failed.
In this case, it needs to be removed and sent out for repair.  (We've had
way too many failures, so this is one of the big reasons for bringing a
system down and rebooting.)

