> 
> Hang on I think you missed a few important details about
> the R3 benchmarks.
> 
> R3 is 3 tier, client, applications servers and DBMS
> servers. The DBMS central instance server is your bottle
> neck since you can add as many app servers as you like
> or need to drive the DBMS server.

	3-tier is a software partitioning model that really
doesn't have anything directly related to the hardware
implementation.
 
> Digital used one 10 CPU 8400 for the DBMS and 53
> applications servers (4100s I think). Sun used
> One 64 CPU E10000 for the DBMS and 7 other servers
> to run the applications tier.

	The information you posted, that I saw, didn't
support this description of the benchmark setup.  Perhaps
an original source for the information should be referenced
again (possible those others that are coming in late.)?

	For all I know, most of the 4100's where there to
LOAD the system at that rate (i.e. generate the requests
that result in the transactions being conducted), which your
numbers don't appear to reflect.
 
> >         Sun also had to integrate multiple servers (you've stated 8)
> > for the benchmark, so what's the big deal about needing 2 8400's
> > clustered, anyway?
> >
> 
> Sun ran one instance of the DBMS, try running SAP on top
> of Oracle Parallel accross 2 or 3 x 8400s and you will
> run into a bunch of major issues.
> 
> 1. I am not sure its supported by SAP

	Actually, the issue on that is support for it
under Oracle, and they do.  Their distributed locking
across server members is based on the OpenVMS Distributed
Lock Management subsystem.

> 2. It may not scale

	Fear, uncertainty, and doubt?  Actually, you
would be surprised at how well clusters scale.  Both in
terms of performance, and simplicity in management since
even 20 servers in a cluster is still managed as if it
was a single server.

> 3. You will need very senior management who enjoy
>    risk taking.
> 4. By the time you have got it working, tuned it
>    partitioned the DBMS, paid Oracle more for OPS
>    you will wish you hadn't

	Again, there is no basis for these claims.  I've
been involved with setting up Oracle databases before in
clusters, and it's no different that setting it up under
a single server.  Most of the tuning is setting up the
caches (which is always an issue), disk load balancing
(again no problem with the StorageWorks array in a cluster
environment), and deciding how much disk caching to put
into the HSZ controllers.

	The cluster advantage, is that you can take down
part of the cluster to upgrade software/hardware without
taking down the entire database/application.  You can
add performance by simply putting a new box next to the
others, and turning it on.  Something which is extremely
difficult to do when your DBMS instance is running on a
single box.

