Rev. Don Kool wrote:

> > Frequent reboots and random crashes are not acceptable -- not matter
> > what you do to work around the problem.  It wastes time and lowers
> > productivity.  Brushing your teeth during the reboot phase isn't an
> > acceptable alternative to stability (not that brushing your teeth is
> > bad!).
> 
>         I get the impression that a lot of you guys have systems that take
> two days to boot or something.

Even if the computer can reboot in less than a minute (SCANDISK over a
few GB can take a while), I don't find it too entertaining to be stopped
in the middle of something, loose the last few minutes of work (assuming
your application automatically saves every few minutes or you stop what
you are doing to save every few minutes), wait for a reboot, restart my
applications, and reload where I left off.  When dealines approach, we
tend to push things a little harder.  This is precisely when Windows
seems to crash.  This is precisely when downtime is not acceptable.

If you are running batch jobs and/or maintaining servers the costs of
downtime are well-known.

