Rev. Don Kool (oldno7@home.com) wrote:
> Steve Tate wrote:
> > Rev. Don Kool (oldno7@home.com) wrote:

> > > Users with "root" on
> > > a networked UNIX workstation can get into all the other users'
> > > business and wreak havoc with possibly hundreds of other users'
> > > accounts.

> > I said it before, and so let me say it again.  If they can do this as
> > root on one workstation, then you don't have things configured
> > properly.  Or more technically, just because you can set your uid to a
> > specific user on a specific machine does NOT mean that you should have
> > access to that user's networked resources -- it should take some type
> > of more secure authentication to do that.  Maybe you should read up on
> > Kerberos and see how a secure Unix network can be run.

> 	Begging the question.  The question suggested that a user on a
> Windows box can "do anything" that a root user on the UNIX
> workstation can do.  A user on a Windows box cannot "su -" to
> another user and read their (unencrypted) mail.  All a user on a
> Windows box can do is screw up their own box.

And you're totally missing the point here.  On a properly set up
environment of Unix workstations, a root user on an individual
workstation can NOT "su -" to another user and read their mail.  Even
root can't access other user's shared networked resources without
knowing the password (of THAT USER, not root) and going through an
authentication step.  Only root on the server machine could do that.
And root on an individual machine doesn't do squat for getting root on
the server.

Root on one machine does NOT imply root on the network, unless you're
not serious about setting up security properly...

So the point still stands, there is nothing you can do as root on a
Unix machine that you can't do as just a standard user on a Windows
machine.

