> >       I get the impression that a lot of you guys have systems that take
> >two days to boot or something.

> Huh?  Why reboot if it isn't necessary? 

	Why not?  It only takes a couple of minutes and it cleans out a lot
of junk.

> The systems don't panic.  Power
> is stable due to the lack of thunderstorms this year.  I once had a SS20/61
> that was up for over a year.

	So have I on many occassions but I never got a cash award or
anything for it.  As a matter of fact, as soon as I see a
workstation that's been up that long I put in an "at" job to have it
reboot (after the user has gone home for the day) just on general
principles.  Any workstation that's been up that long has just gotta
need a good flush.  Even if it doesn't, there's no harm done and an
ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure.

	Look at it another way... 

	What is it that finally makes even you reboot-a-phobes finally
sacrifice your run for the record, knock back a shot of bourbon and
reboot your machines?  We've already established that I'm the only
one in the world who has ever seen a "defunct" process and had
neither the time nor the inclination to track it down so it can't be
that.  I suppose no one in any of your shops ever accidently sends a
"kill -9" to "automount" instead of a "kill -TERM" and hangs the
automounter tight.  Of course you never get a user who calls up to
complain that the executable he's been running a hundred times a
day, every day for a year is now suddenly dumping core every time he
tries to execute it even though the code hasn't been changed for 5
years.  All of you only run well behaved programs with no memory
leaks.  You never get "process table full" or "swap space below
10%".  Memory doesn't get fragmented.  None of your systems ever
just lose track of a SCSI device for no reason and you never have a
machine just start running slower and slower until it grinds to a
halt.

	Even so it must be something.  I'd even venture to guess that
sometimes the need to reboot comes at a less than optimal time.  Now
I'm all ears if someone wants to tell me how I'm going to break the
system by taking the average time between forced reboots, dividing
it in half and creating a "cron" job to do a "maintenance reboot" of
the system at that time (in the middle of the night when no one is
using the system).  Since even you guys have to reboot your systems
eventually, what have I lost by moving the inevitable reboot to a
time of my choosing rather than having it occur without notice right
in the middle of something important?  I'm talking about something
more substantial that "my system was up longer than yours" bragging
rights here.  After all, we've already taken metrics and know that
the system usually stays up twice as long as the time I'm asking it
to run so I may never see a forced reboot during working hours.

			      Don


