Rev. Don Kool (oldno7@home.com) wrote:
> Peter da Silva wrote:
> > Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> > >       There is no "root" on Windows.  It's not a multi-user OS.

> > In terms of people's abilities to abuse your network from a Windows box,
> > everyone on a Windows box is root.

> 	And what are they supposed to do with this "root" on a Windows box
> that they can't do as themselves on the UNIX boxes?  

There are many, many, many things that a Windows user can do that they
couldn't do "as themselves" (i.e., non-root) on a Unix box.  The
Windows user can change, destory, or otherwise mess with any and all
systems settings.  The Windows user can change, destroy, and otherwise
mess with system software.  The Windows user can and does allow
programs, including viruses, full access to the system.  A non-root
Unix user can do none of that, which is one reason why Unix systems are
more stable and easier to administer.

> "Root" on Windows gives a user no special access to the network.

Define what you mean by "special access".  A Windows user has full and
complete access to the network anyway.  A regular user on Unix has
access controlled by the OS, so cannot do any of the harmful things
that any Windows user can, like run network sniffers.

Now if you're talking about special access to other hosts on the
network, then if root on one Unix machine (a user's machine, not the
central server) gives you special access to other hosts on the network
you've set up your network wrong...

Here is a simple straightforward way to say all this:  there is
absolutely nothing you can do as root on a Unix machine that you
couldn't do as just an average user on a Windows machine.

> If you're
> talking about them misconfiguring their network settings or
> something, big deal.  There are easier and more violent ways a
> malcontent can get fired.

So you *DO* see that a Windows user has the ability to cause problems,
just as a root user does on a Unix machine.  And yet you state that
you have a policy of no root access on the Unix machines, but allow
Windows boxes.  Don't you see the inconsistency of that?

