Steve Tate wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool (oldno7@home.com) wrote:
> > Peter da Silva wrote:
> > > Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> > > >       There is no "root" on Windows.  It's not a multi-user OS.

> > > In terms of people's abilities to abuse your network from a Windows box,
> > > everyone on a Windows box is root.

> >       And what are they supposed to do with this "root" on a Windows box
> > that they can't do as themselves on the UNIX boxes?
> 
> There are many, many, many things that a Windows user can do that they
> couldn't do "as themselves" (i.e., non-root) on a Unix box.  The
> Windows user can change, destory, or otherwise mess with any and all
> systems settings.  The Windows user can change, destroy, and otherwise
> mess with system software.  The Windows user can and does allow
> programs, including viruses, full access to the system.  A non-root
> Unix user can do none of that, which is one reason why Unix systems are
> more stable and easier to administer.
> 
> > "Root" on Windows gives a user no special access to the network.
> 
> Define what you mean by "special access".  A Windows user has full and
> complete access to the network anyway.  A regular user on Unix has
> access controlled by the OS, so cannot do any of the harmful things
> that any Windows user can, like run network sniffers.
> 
> Now if you're talking about special access to other hosts on the
> network, then if root on one Unix machine (a user's machine, not the
> central server) gives you special access to other hosts on the network
> you've set up your network wrong...
> 
> Here is a simple straightforward way to say all this:  there is
> absolutely nothing you can do as root on a Unix machine that you
> couldn't do as just an average user on a Windows machine.
> 
> > If you're
> > talking about them misconfiguring their network settings or
> > something, big deal.  There are easier and more violent ways a
> > malcontent can get fired.
> 
> So you *DO* see that a Windows user has the ability to cause problems,
> just as a root user does on a Unix machine.  And yet you state that
> you have a policy of no root access on the Unix machines, but allow
> Windows boxes.  Don't you see the inconsistency of that?

	They can perhaps cause network problems if they change their
network settings.  This is something that our network monitoring
software would pick up right away.  The result would range from the
user no longer having a windows box to termination depending upon
where their actions fell on the "stupidity" vs. "malicious intent"
scale.  There is a certain level of trust that you have to have in
your employees before you even let them on a system in the first
place.  Even on Windows boxes people don't change the network
settings by accident.  They run their applications that are the
reason they need the windows box in the first place and we generally
don't hear squat from them.  Except for when I got extra SIMMs from
the supply system and offered them up to the windows users, I
haven't had a trouble call on a windows box in a year or more.  We
only have about a dozen compared with hundreds of UNIX workstations
and servers and the windows people just plain never call us for
support.  They've got a few little applications that they run on the
things and they just run and run like the little engine that could.

	Can windows users wipe their whole system?  Yes.  Is it a problem?
No.  They all come with a CDROM that reinstalls all the software
back to just like the day it came in the door.  Just like a user's
".cshrc" and ".login" files in their UNIX account, if they screw up
their PC, they get to go back to the default.

	Run "sniffer" software.  Sorry but we don't load that onto the
windows boxes.  If the user has brought such software in from home
then they've broken the rules and could face anything from a
reprimand to dismissal to fines and jail time.  If you're counting
users that bring in software from home as something to be wary of,
then you have to realize that UNIX workstation users could also
bring in a copy of the OS and boot into miniroot.  As long as the
user has physical access to his machine, be it windows or UNIX, they
can conceivably break in and do whatever they want.  You have to
keep an eye out for misuse or abuse but you must also have some
level of trust in your employees.

	You're right that a user with root on UNIX box cannot really do
more harm in the sense that both users can effectively erase the
system which would be about as bad as it could get.  The difference
is that the UNIX boxes are not "personal" workstations.  They're
doing real work and they're configured so that any user can sit down
and (through a combination of NIS and automount) use it just like
the one at their own desk.  Some user's workstations are also the
print servers for their office; a few are NIS slaves.  If they had
root on a UNIX workstation, they could create a dummy password entry
for another user and (if the other user had a ".rhosts" file in
their account) log on as that user all over the network.  Without
leaving the workstation they had root on they could "su" to another
user and read/send/delete mail as that user or read/modify another
user's calendar.  Of course they could also read/modify/delete any
other users' files no matter what the permissions.  Those
possibilites alone are unacceptable.  We, as system administrators,
have had to have legal training from the Inspector General (IG) on
what we can and cannot use our access for.  As you can imagine idly
flipping through a user's e-mail to stave off bordom is considered a
no-no.

	Users of Personal Computers have full access to that computer and
all its files.  They can make a mistake and screw themselves up
royally.  If they have malicious intent, they can cause network
troubles that are easily traced back to them.  Users with "root" on
a networked UNIX workstation can get into all the other users'
business and wreak havoc with possibly hundreds of other users'
accounts.  If they can possibly sneak into a server as root through
some leftover ".rhosts" file or something, they can cause untold
damage by modifying (or deleting) NIS maps, DNS tables, and all
manner of other network services.  There's a big difference.  You
can put a "+" in the "/etc/hosts.equiv" and a PC user still won't be
able to get in as root.  A root user on a workstation most certainly
can.

			
			      Don


