Rev. Don Kool (oldno7@home.com) wrote:

> Users with "root" on
> a networked UNIX workstation can get into all the other users'
> business and wreak havoc with possibly hundreds of other users'
> accounts.

I said it before, and so let me say it again.  If they can do this as
root on one workstation, then you don't have things configured
properly.  Or more technically, just because you can set your uid to a
specific user on a specific machine does NOT mean that you should have
access to that user's networked resources -- it should take some type
of more secure authentication to do that.  Maybe you should read up on
Kerberos and see how a secure Unix network can be run.

> If they can possibly sneak into a server as root through
> some leftover ".rhosts" file or something, they can cause untold
> damage by modifying (or deleting) NIS maps, DNS tables, and all
> manner of other network services.

If you're going to distribute sensitive services like that onto
personal workstations, then you get what you deserve....  of *course*
that's not secure.  But if you're interested in running a secure
environment, you don't do that.

