Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> carefully crafted electrons to say:
> Peter da Silva wrote:
> > Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:
> 
> > >> >       Purchase "free" UNIX?  What's up with that?  :-)
> 
> > >> It's called a support contract.
> 
> > >       It's starting to sound a lot less "free".
> 
> > The point isn't that it's free money-wise, it's that it's free access-wise.
> 
> > It used to be you bought a computer from IBM or DEC or whoever, and you got
> > the O/S source with it. Because you needed that. Then they discoevered they
> > could control you better if you didn't have what you needed, and now we're
> > getting back to the roots... the wheel of change has spun around again.
> > 
> > And we're getting control of our systems back in our own hands.
> > 
> > Why does that bother you?
> 
> 	It doesn't bother me.  As I said, it's for hobbiests and
> tinkerers.  They need the source, we do not.

How large a community do you represent and do they know you repesent
them? :-) I spent eight years as an MVS systems programmer at a large
shop writing OS modifications to a major subsystem in Assembler (and
when I left, they hired a replacement and still have someone in that
job to this day I'm sure). I would not classify this as "tinkering" as
these modifications were was used in production and each modification
had a sound business need to justify its' existence (and the backing
infrastructure).  Obviously, they (and many/most of the large MVS
shops) felt the need for changes IBM was unwilling or slow to make to
the OS, enough to pay for it. When IBM was advocating OCO (Object Code
Only), they made for many unhappy campers.  That sort of need (for OS
source) will always exist for a certain segment of the computing
market.  Anything else means, at best, kludging your way past the
inadequacies of a particular operating system.  Because *you* do not
belong to that segment or see/have a need, don't presume you can speak
for the industry (that *was* a royal "we" above wasn't it?). :-)




