In article <6tkr2o$m4g@web.nmti.com>,
Peter da Silva <peter@baileynm.com> wrote:
>                          PS: how do you specify resolver order in NT?

Well, it's *dead* easy :-).

Just click on `Start'.  Then click on `Settings'.  Now click on `Control
Panel'.  After that, double click on `Network' (unless you have the IE4
desktop enhancements, in which case it might be just a single click
again).

Select the `Protocols' tab.  Click on `TCP/IP Protocol'.  (There
may be more than one, if you have multiple interfaces.)  Click on
`Properties...'.

Select the `DNS' tab.  Now, just use the `Up' and `Down' buttons to
rearrange the existing entries, or the `Add...' button to add a new
one, `Edit...' to change an existing one, or `Remove' to remove one
altogether.

When you're done, click `OK' on the `Microsoft TCP/IP Properties' box,
and click `OK' on the `Network' box to remove them.  To remove the
`Control Panel' box, of course, you must do something different: click
the button with a large X in the top right hand corner.

Fortunately, you're running Windows NT.  Had you been running Windows
95, you would naturally have to reboot after making such a major
configuration change.

Tim.
