> correctly.  That is just a part of the way it works.  Having to
> reboot a Windows machine after doing something as trivial as
> changing the IP address is just how it works (although not in NT
> 5.0, I hear).  It's simply the nature of the beast.

A decent UNIX machine will go over a year without rebooting or
crashing.  A corrupt file system is unlikely to occur in a typical
modern UNIX system with a modern file system.  It can happen, but it is
much much less likely than such occurring in a MS 95/NT system and not
much if any more likely than such occurring with any other operating
system.  Old, crappy UNIXs with crappy file systems excluded...

Frequent reboots and random crashes are not acceptable -- not matter
what you do to work around the problem.  It wastes time and lowers
productivity.  Brushing your teeth during the reboot phase isn't an
acceptable alternative to stability (not that brushing your teeth is
bad!).

-- 
