In article <6tdc4m$nh0@sjx-ixn9.ix.netcom.com>, scdave@hotmail.com (Dave D) writes:
:Does anyone know if there is a downloadable user guide for the Alphastation 
:400 4/233?

  I don't know that the user's guide is (or is not) available on-line.

:I have one that was donated after an apparent failed attempt to 
:upgrade the firmware.  It worked great before, I would like to try and get 
:it going again.

  URLs to look at:

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/utilities/

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/Alpha/firmware/
      readmes/v5.2/doc/alpha200_v68_fw_relnote.txt

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/systems/

  From memory, the sequence is something like this: create a firmware 
  failsafe floppy from the information and files (the mkboot files and
  text documentation) available via the above URLs.  Create a second 
  floppy with the target firmware files -- the firmware you want to 
  load into the FlashRAM.  Power down the box. Open the enclosure, and 
  switch the AlphaStation 400 CPU motherboard J5 jumper from its normal 
  position (pins 2-3 connected) over to the failsafe loader position 
  (pins 1-2 connected).  Insert the failsafe floppy.  Power up -- this 
  will load the firmware from the floppy.  Then swap floppies and reload 
  the desired firmware into the FlashROM per the instructions included at 
  one of the above URLs.  Remove the floppy.  Power down.  Reset J5 to 
  the default position (pins 2-3 connected).  Power up.  Boot.

	--

  Use anti-static procedures whenever working within the system enclosure, 
  as there are components that are static-sensitive, and easily (and even
  potentially subtly) damaged.  If you are not trained and familiar with 
  working with static-sensitive components, or are not trained on working 
  within computer enclosures, please refer the service to an appropriately
  trained hardware service organization.

