You were quoted as comparing the ease of managing 162 machines to 8
machines.  If you had said 162 DEC Unix machines, or 162 non-Sun Unix
machines, etc. you would not have gotten that response.

My own calculation is that it is twice as much work to manage 162
machines as to manage 8, since there is a maximum supported number
of machines in a cluster of 96.  And I do believe that anybody who
has responsibility for 162 machines does want to use configurations
that are supported by the manufacturer.

I do think problem occurances go up according to the number of disk
drives.  If the model you choose has a 3 year MTBF, then with 365
drives you will have one fail every three days.  Shadowing (RAID)
does not remove the need for attention by the manager, it just
hides the incident from the end users.  Software to manage the
shadowing, of course, is just another thing to go wrong.  That
is regardless of vendor - the more complex a configuration is
the more individual pieces can go wrong.

