Goran Larsson wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> >       If you want to think about it that way. "C:" is where it's at in a
> > PC.  Do you complain that "vmunix" has to go in "/" also?

> There doesn't have to be a file called /vmunix. A Solaris 2.X machine
> doesn't even have a file with that name.

	That was simply an example, my son.  Sometimes it is too much of a
chore to type in a bunch of different file names and UNIX variants. 
Many on this newsgroup seem to be 'SUN worshippers' so I used that. 
SOLARIS puts theirs in a subdirectory and calls it "unix", I think. 
IRIX puts it in root and calls it "unix" and I think CRAY UNICOS
does as well.  That's not the point.  The point is that you can't
move the kernel off of the root file system and have it boot.  (If 
you could manage to do it and get it to boot, I doubt you would be
done solving the problems it caused.)

> A Sun, at least, can boot
> from a kernel with any name you like. It can also have several disks
> with different roots.

	So can a PC.  They're called floppies.

> > > If you don't fancy fixing the broken program then you only need
> > > to kill it. Rebooting just to get rid of defunct processes is
> > > overdoing it.

> >       You can't kill defunct processes.  They only go away with a reboot.

> I said you had to kill the broken program. The defunct processes are
> just hanging around until the parent wait()s on them (wich the broken
> programs obviously doesn't do.) If you kill the broken program then
> the defunct processes gets inherited bu init and will then be properly
> wait()ed for. The statement "They only go away with a reboot" is wrong.

	Perhaps.

> >       Rule To Live By:  If you're doing everything right but the machine
> > still isn't behaving, reboot.  Problem solved almost every time
> > unless it's hardware.  Time wasted going down blind alleys - 0
> > seconds.

> That rule is just as silly as measuring time by continously filling
> and empying your bath.

	Not hardly, Goran.  That rule has saved me countless hours on both
PCs and UNIX boxes and it has also been my guide to get more than a
few other SAs out of an hours long downtime.  Example:  One time a
friend of mine was having trouble with his PC.  He was running some
kind of bookeeping software for his business.  Using a DOS shell or
the file manager, there were database files of the type the program
needed clearly visible in the directory.  "File --> Open" on the
program always reported that there were no files to open (and yes it
was pointed to the proper directory).  I couldn't stop by to look at
it until after work and by that time two of them had been beating
their brains in over it for more than four hours.  When I arrived, I
looked at it, verified that they indeed weren't doing anything
stupid and then (following my rule) rebooted the machine.  We fired
up the program, did the "File --> Open" and low and behold there
were the database files.  Had I been there earlier, the rule would
have saved an entire man-day.  The problem never resurfaced in the
four years they continued to use the computer and the software after
that.  I don't want to be overly technical but there are so many
things going on in a computer that sometimes they just get out of
sync.  If you find yourself saying, "Why isn't this working?  I've
done this a thousand times!  When I type 'xxx', the machine should
do 'yyy'.  It always has before.  This should not be a problem!" --
you're in need of a reboot, not another couple hours of scratching
your head.  Should a reboot not fix it... you've had three or four
extra minutes to think about the problem and you've just eliminated
stopping and restarting the automounter, re-exporting the file
systems, stopping and restarting print services, stopping and
restarting NIS, etc... as the fix.  All in one fell swoop.

> >       Oh, one of those.  How sad.  :-(

> What is so sad about taking control of your own life?
> 
> --
>  Goran Larsson            hoh AT approve DOT se
>  I was an atheist,        http://home1 DOT swipnet DOT se/%7Ew-12153/
>  until I found out I was God.

	I will remember you in my prayers and light a candle for you at
service this evening, my son.

			Yours in Christ,
			      Don


