Mark Lehrer wrote:
> 
> "Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> writes:
> 
> > > If you don't fancy fixing the broken program then you only need
> > > to kill it. Rebooting just to get rid of defunct processes is
> > > overdoing it.

> >       You can't kill defunct processes.  They only go away with a reboot.

> I believe they go away when their parent goes away.

	They're supposed to.  If they did what they were supposed to do,
they wouldn't be there in the first place.

> >       I love rebooting the machine!  It's like bringing in fresh laundry
> > from the line.  Everything is so clean it's almost like it was new
> > again.
> >
> >       Rule To Live By:  If you're doing everything right but the machine
> > still isn't behaving, reboot.  Problem solved almost every time
> > unless it's hardware.  Time wasted going down blind alleys - 0
> > seconds.

> Now you're being a troll.  A system should NEVER need a reboot.

	"NEVER need a reboot"... They all need to be rebooted or they
slowly grind to a halt over time.  "/tmp" starts to fill, you get a
few "defunct" processes hanging around not paying rent, you've
collected a few 'Stale NFS file handles' along the way, a tape drive
or CDROM player has hiccupped and stopped communicating with the
workstation, etc...  Embrace the reboot and even the "maintenance
reboot" via "cron".  Your system will love you for it and you'll
have more of your valuable time free to solve real problems.

>  If you're going to reboot, you had better at least understand why the
> problem occurred, or else you're not going to solve it.

	I'll never understand some people's aversion to something as simple
as a reboot.  Shit; if the user has gone home for the day and I need
to stop and restart the automounter or something similar, sometimes
I'll just "rsh" over the "reboot" command rather than bother with
looking up the PID, killing it with a "TERM", etc...  I'm not going
for any kind of time up record on my systems.  Longest uninterrupted
stretch of uptime is not what interests my users.  Highest
percentage of usable uptime during their shift is.

> > > Christ? All religions are created to control the people. "If you don't
> > > live by these rules you will go to hell!"

> >       Oh, one of those.  How sad.  :-(

> Now be reasonable - Christianity has a lot of bad baggage over the
> years.  It's time to start fresh with a new religion and new mythology.

	You mean a "reboot"?

			Yours in Christ,
			      Don


