Peter da Silva wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> >       That's what I meant, the resolver order.  It works differently
> >under each OS (when the OS has made a provision for it).

> And it's never taken mor ethan a couple of "man" commands to find, starting
> with "man gethostbyname". 

	That depends upon the documentation and if the OS even allows you
to even specify the resolver order.  It also depends upon the
background and experience of the SA.  You apparently come from a
computer science background (programming, etc...).  Most of the SAs
we have are being "cross trained" from career fields that are the
victims of "right sizing".  That or they start out as 'advanced
users' (i.e., the guy in the office that everybody comes to first
when they have a problem).  The point I'm making is that in many
shops the SA work is a sideline for one of the programmers or an
advanced user.  There is no SA that only does system administration
full time.  We are not that kind of shop.  All we do is system
administration full time every day.  We don't write code.  We don't
debug code.  We install code.  We manage the networks to a point and
then the network shop takes over (basicaly at the gateway router). 
We install and configure new systems when they come in.  We set up
NIS and DNS domains.  Our jobs are specialized to a higher degree
than most places.  The good part of that is that everyone
(eventually) gets very good at certain things.  The bad part is that
very few people get very proficient at the other guy's thing.  It
just isn't possible unless they move to a position in the other
guy's shop.  Most of our SAs don't know shit from shineola about a
Make file, much less that a command called "gethostbyname" actually
exists or that its "man" page will generally give you the resolver
order.  Hell, most don't know there is such a thing as a resolver
order!

	Because of the stressful nature of the work (especially when you
are specialized into just doing system admin), every time the phone
rings it is somebody who isn't happy about something.  If the system
stays up for a year and they never see you, then you've got a gravy
job in their book as they don't see the hoops you're jumping through
behind the scenes to keep them up.  If their system goes down when
they're trying to work or, heaven forbid, you change a menu or the
version of an application or something, then you're a fuck up who
doesn't know what you're doing.  People burn out fast and new people
are hellishly difficult to recruit.  SAs that can program or who are
as intimately familiar with machine code etc. as you are end up
saying, 'Fuck this!' and moving on to a nice, quiet corner where
they can write code undisturbed for 8 hrs a day.  Between new
interns moving through the training program and old people just
finishing out their career, there are very few really knowledgable
SAs around.  Just read the press and you see how in demand the ones
that actually know something are.  The government can't compete with
private industry to keep the few good people we've managed to train
up to this point.  Earlier this year I was within a hair's breadth
myself of going to a vendor to do OpenVMS support all day, every
day.

> PS: how do you specify resolver order in NT?

	Don't know.  We only had one NT machine (running 3.51) and the
group that it belonged to moved to a different part of the building
and took it with them.  Now we have a separate SA shop that handles
the offices that have been switched to NT.  I'm signed up for the NT
courses now so I'll probably be able to tell you all about it after
the beginning of the year. :-)

> >> And GUI tools can be confusing as well. You're familiar with Netscape 3.
> >> Where do you specify script security?

> >       I haven't used Netscape 3 in quite a while.

> It's under Languages. Where you'd expect to find your "English" or "German"
> locale settings.

> >       And I'm sure you use it all the time because "vi" is for sissys
> >that don't know what they're doing, right?  :-)

> I use the best tool for the job, no matter what. Sometimes that's "ed", even
> now. It's really hard to use "vi" in a shell script.

	It's also hard to use it some single user miniroot situations
especially when "/usr" isn't mounted.

			      Don


