Supak Lailert wrote:
> "Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> writes:

> >> As an admin you should know by heart what files you have to edit
> >> to make things work. Tools are always unavailable when you're really
> >> in need of them.
> 
> >       If I drag you kicking and screaming from your precious SUN and drop
> >you down in front of an SGI, HP, DEC, HARRIS, CONVEX or CRAY, are
> >you going to rattle off to me the name of the file that handles even
> >something as trivial as setting the DNS resolver order off the top
> >of your head for every single system?  If not, I'm sure you would
> >welcome some tool that walked you through it.  I'm sure your users

> I would say that tools can't handle complex tasks very well. And the
> common tasks are fairly similar across platform.

	"Fairly similar" until you put spaces in a file that requires tab
characters or vice versa.  Unless the administrator is quite a bit
more than "fairly" familiar with the layout, they're better off
using the GUI or other tool provided and taking advantage of the
error checking they provide.

> And if I'm supposed to be paid for as a SyaAdm for multi platform
> environment, yes, I'll have to learn them in advance rather than relying
> on tools.

	Actually it works better to have the new admins use the tools
provided while they learn the systems.  Then, when they're more
familiar with the layout, they can choose when and where to leave
the tools behind.

> I agree with you that if all of the sudden I need to
> do "emergency sysadm job" on an unfamiliar platform then I'll have
> a BIG trouble :-)
> 
> >       As far as "tools are always unavailable when you're really in need
> >of them"... I can go 'mano-a-mano' with "ed", can you?  :-)
> 
> Heh Heh the problem is you forgot where the files are because the "tools"
> didn't tell you! :-)

	No, "the problem" is that too many SAs would rather edit a dozen
files and perhaps have to track down some kind of non-obvious typo
like accidently putting unprintable control characters in a file or
not terminating a line correctly than use a simple GUI just because
they feel it makes them look 'smarter' than those 'poor idiots' who
'have' to use a GUI.  In reality being "smarter" also means being
able to admit when a tool exists that actually eases your burden
rather than being so insecure that you always feel the need to
"prove" something by doing it the hard way.  

			      Don


