>In article <6tkcn3$d8v@news1.panix.com>, tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:
>|> You have a 220V 11/750?  That's a definite oddity in the US.  Mine -- and
>|> all the ones I've stolen parts from, and all the ones I ever used, to my
>|> knowledge (there were a few I never poked around inside, come to think of
>|> it) were 110V 60Hz single-phase.
>
>In NZ it was not unheard-of (but not recommended) to run 11/750s with
>4MB, UBA, TU80, and RA8x in a normal office environment from a
>common-or-garden 240v 10A AC wall outlet.  Even with Field Service
>coverage, provided the humidity could be kept within spec.  Or you
>could cheat and put the CPU and the TU80 cabinet on two separate 10A
>circuits, using the same phase.
>
>|> With the Massbus adapter pulled, mine draws well under 20A at 110V.  I
>|> don't have an FP750, however, and I only have 6MB of memory.
>|> 
>|> 25A at 220V is quite unlikely.  The master power supply for the whole machine
>|> has a 20A 220V fuse, IIRC, and the plug which was originally on the cord
>|> coming out of it was the funky plug for a 30A 110V outlet.
>
>Depends on the options. For an 11/750, second UBA cab, DEUNA, UDA50,
>12MB, TU80, and 3-high rack of RA8Xs, 25A at 250V sounds in the right
>ballpark.  Don't even ask about how much the battery-backup option drew.
>Were the configurations really that different in the USA?

Probably not; the ones I used may have been "odd".

However, my 11/750 has a single UBA, a DEUNA, UDA50, 6MB, TU8[mumble, not
there now],  two Emulex DH11-alikes, an RA80, and an RA81.  It's not
all the same exact parts, but it's about how it ran at its original
site up until the power got switched off and I carried it away in my folks'
VW Bus.

We left a TE16 (and thus the need for the MBA, which does suck a fair bit
of juice as I recall) there, since it was too damned heavy to put in the
van; we were afraid we'd do serious damage as it was, with the springs
pretty much flattened out by the weight of the '750 and drives.  I limped
along with a TS03 (600-foot version of the TS11) until I managed to pick
up my current tape drive which may once have been under Terry's care,
as I understand. :-)

My VAX looks an awful lot like (except that it has only 6MB and no FP750)
the ones I used elsewhere; in my experience second Unibus cabinets were
rare.  As I said, once the disks spin up (hint: turn them on *in sequence*!)
it hums along nicely powered by a single 110V 20A circuit.

Originally, everything was plugged together through the auxiliary outlets
on the power supply in the main cabinet, which was on a 110V 30A circuit
and which has a weird 110V 30A plug -- looks like the original plug DEC
put on -- on the cord leading out of it.  So for this type of configuration
20A at 220V is clearly not required, though 15A might be.

Terry's posted that TU7x tape drives did require 220V.  I guess that's why
my VAX originally had the TE16 on it -- there wasn't 220V anywhere near
where the machine was, to my knowledge.

>(PS: wasn't the FP750 just extra (readonly) microcode?)

I'm certain that you know much more about it than I do.  I was just trying
to point out that it was one way in which my '750 had less cards in it
potentially sucking power than the "typical configuration" I was used to
~10 years ago.

-- 
