Well, to get technical, anything can call itself UNIX if it passes the 
'unix compatibility' requirements set by X/open. 
Ultimately, True UNIX would be anythin gup to 32V, and then Eighth Edition
and Plan9, and then nothing more. the next closest thing to The One True
Unix would be 4BSD, and then System V, and then other deriviants. 
But in reality, all of them can be called UNIX as well as solaris, sunOs, 
hpux, OSF/DUnix, AIX, even Irix which is pretty far out there.. and many
others. Not to mention Linux and the *BSD gang!
And , actually, the {Net,Open,Free}BSD people all derived their original
sources from the 4.4BSDLite release from UCBerkeley itself- and that 
derived from a direct lineage from 32V, which was right out of Bell Labs. 

OSF, Linux, and AIX are almost enitrely grown from the ground up, though
based heavily on either SysV, POSIX, or 4BSD, or a mix of them. 
Most others trace a lineage back to primarily BSD or primarily SysV. 


