void wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> >       Which is perfectly OK as long as you're not looking down your nose
> >at someone who gets by just fine with a Windows box as some kind of
> >unenlightened dolt.

> Of course not.  When did I say I did, exactly?

	Not you specifically.  Just the general tone of many UNIX weenies
when anything nice is said about PCs running Windows.  Apparently
I'm in the minority for not taking sides and being able to
appreciate the uses of both.  Then again maybe I'm not and it's just
the more vocal UNIX weenies who react to any mention of Windows like
a jealous lover when the name of an old girlfriend is mentioned.

> >If your windows machines are crashing all the time and losing your work,
> >you're not doing something right.

> That's impossible.  Anyone can use Windows correctly, it's the most
> user-friendly operating system ever devised.
> 
> Anyway, I never lose work to Windows because I never trust my data to it.
> After seeing it in action for a while, I became convinced that it is
> poorly coded and poorly supported.  Nothing I've seen since then has
> changed my mind.
> 
> Somehow I doubt I could have been doing anything wrong, anyway.  I can
> successfully operate computers that aren't "user-friendly" at all, and I
> do alright on Macs too, so if Windows won't work for me it's either broken
> or not providing me with enough information to use it correctly.

	You basically made my original point.  Windows is "easy", UNIX is
"hard".  That's all.  At home when I want to do my little BS chores
and enjoy a little entertainment or diversion, I want "easy".  I
like "easy".  At work when millions of dollars and possibily lives
are at stake, we go with "hard" because "easy" won't cut it.

> >I've been running windows in some version or another at home since '93
> >and I've never lost a byte.
> 
> I'm thrilled for you. 

	Thank you.

> You must be very good at tiptoeing around Windows'
> inadequacies. 

	If that means not mucking with the innards to show it how much
smarter I am that it is, I suppose so.

> How often does your word processor automatically save your
> work?

	Just like with FRAMEMAKER running on UNIX at work; every five
minutes.

> >Tens of millions of satisfied customers have
> >voted with their dollars and more do so every day.  They can't all
> >be wrong and they can't all be losing countless hours of hard work
> >to system crashes on a daily basis.

> You're wrong.  Many of those customers are unsatisfied, but have been
> given the impression that they have no choice.  Many of them lose data,
> time and/or money to system crashes on a daily basis.  If you don't know
> this, you must have been living under a rock for the past five years at
> least.

	Just imagine how it would be if all those housewives, kids and
grannies were trying to load, configure and manage UNIX.  Talk about
lost data and consumer frustration!

> >PCs with Windows work just fine.

> Sometimes.

> >Workstations and servers with UNIX run just fine.
> 
> Usually.

> >Why the "religious war"?

> Why are you wearing a cucumber on your head?  I don't know what you're
> talking about.  My problems with Microsoft software have nothing to do
> with religion and everything to do with quality.  I rely on software every
> day and I don't have the time or patience to deal with bad software any
> more than I absolutely have to.  HTH, HAND.

	The "religious war" is you overstating the problems with PCs
running Windows.  Denigrating one doesn't make the other any
better.  There's no need for a "jihad".

			Hope this helps,
			      Don


