Peter da Silva wrote:
>Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:
> >Peter da Silva wrote:
> >> Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> >> >       That would be fine but...  If they're going to connect to the
> >> >intranet though, we've got to support it.  Users do not have root
> >> >under any circumstances unless it is a standalone machine they're
> >> >using for some sort of project they're working on.  The minute they
> >> >connect to the network, it's our machine, not theirs.  We can't risk
> >> >users inadvertently introducing something to the network that could
> >> >potentially bring it down or cause a security breach.

> >> So you don't have *any* Windows boxes or laptops then?

> >       Yes, we have Windows boxes and a smattering of laptops (although
> >the laptops don't connect to the network).

> But the Windows boxes do, and as you said your user is "root" on that Windows
> box. And there's a lot more canned H/P/V/C software out there for Windows. On
> UNIX you have to have a clue to use the mal-tools.

	There is no "root" on Windows.  It's not a multi-user OS.  It's an
OS for a "personal" computer.  In our agency users are prohibited by
security regulations from bringing magnetic media into the building
and from introducing any media at all into their systems unless it
has arrived at the building shrink wrapped as part of a government
purchase.  Even then there is a requirement to have it all virus
scanned by the sys admin shop.  We also don't have our intranet
connected to the Internet in any way, shape or form.

	The bottom line is that PC users generally get what came loaded
from the factory and that's about it.  If they purchase additional
software, they have to come to us to have it scanned and loaded.  In
our shop we're all about UNIX and the few PCs that we have are used
mostly for running non-critical tools both commercial and in-house
developed that aren't available on the UNIX side.  Of course other
shops are all NT and more and more offices are going that way every
day. :-(


			      Don


