Peter da Silva wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> >       Don't the newer versions read the older formats?

> They read them. They don't *write* them. Which means that as soon as one
> person is using the new tool everyone has to upgrade.
> 
> Designing file formats that are forwards and backwards compatible is trivial.
> There's no technical reason why Microsoft has to have abusively incompatible
> formats... money is the only explanation.

	FRAMEMAKER doesn't write out old formats (or, if it does, it's not
real obvious).  It will only read two versions back also.  Most
applications won't write the older formats because the reason the
format changed in the first place was to accomodate "enhancements". 
There is no place to save those "enhancements" in the older format
so they can't write out that way.  They can read in the older
formats and place them in the newer format because the
"enhancements" simply won't be used.

> > Besides "Money"
> >(and Windows itself) I don't run any Microsoft software.

> If we didn't have to run Microsoft applications we would have no reason
> to run Microsoft Windows at work. Word, Excel, and so on are treated as the
> file interchange format of the '90s, and we HAVE to be compatible with our
> vendors and customers.

	It is my understanding that those programs (at least I'm pretty
sure about "Word") also run on the MAC and the file formats are
interchangeable.  You could always jump from the Windows frying pan
into the MAC fire. :-)

> > (No, I
> >dont make an effort to "not buy it" either.  "Money" came loaded on
> >my first PC and I've just gotten the upgrades since then rather than
> >switch to Quicken.)  I know that every time a new version of "Money"
> >comes out, it reads the older file format.

> I'll bet it doesn't write it. If you needed to interoperate (as we do) then
> you'd be dealing with a whole other problem.

	It can't write it because it would have no where to write all the
"extra stuff" that the format was changed to make room for in the
first place.

> >> That's me. You prefer walking across the building every time some user's
> >> got a configuration problem? Or you prefer talking them through it?

> >       I prefer "rlogin".

> To a *windows* box? Editing the config files and registry directly is the
> only alternative.

	No, editing the config files and the registry directly is, in
general, asking for trouble.  Someone was just going on in this
thread about eschewing the GUI to edit config files manually and
then said in the next sentence that they had recently messed up the
"/etc/pasword" file by not terminating a line.  That doesn't happen
with you use the GUI.  Yes, we all edit the registry by hand at
times.  There's times when it's necessary.  It is just not the
routine way to configure a windows machine.

> >       Some just realize that it's the nature of the beast.  Personally I
> >have to reboot my home PC running Win98 about once every couple
> >weeks.  If I come in in the morning and it's hung, I just hit the
> >"reset" button and go brush my teeth or something.  No great
> >inconvenience.

> Except when you forgot to close your application or it corrupts the file
> system (and yes, it does do that. NT 4.0 prior to SP1 was notorious for
> doing that, and I've had to reinstall games on my son's PC many times
> because it'd trashed some open file when it had been turned off without
> shutting down... to much wailing if he had unsaved state that he'd lost).
> 
> I find this attitude inconceivable. It's like having a car that turns itself
> off at random every couple of weeks.

	Many dyed in the wool UNIX weenies do.  Many PC users wonder what
all the fuss is about.  I find myself somewhat in the middle.

> > Every OS has it's quirks.  I don't fault UNIX for
> >corrupting the file system if not shutdown correctly

> It doesn't do that near as often as Windows 95.

	Please...

> >or not allowing
> >users to use abbreviations of the commands (like OpenVMS).

> Man csh, look up the "alias" command.

	An "alias" is not an abbreviation of the command.  As with SUNs
screwup of the backspace and delete key mapping from earlier in this
thread, the fact that you can fix (or emulate) it doesn't mean that
it doesn't (or in this case, does) exist.  UNIX does not allow you
to abbreviate commands, period.  You can emulate this functionality
by composing a long list of alias that are loaded into memory every
time you log in.  OpenVMS has "symbols" that do the same thing. 
I've seen UNIX users set up ".cshrc" files that made their computers
act just like DOS boxes or just like OpenVMS boxes.  I've also seen
OpenVMS users that set up their "login.com" file to make them
perform like a UNIX box.  I'm guilty like the rest.  All my UNIX
accounts have "lo" aliases to "logout" because that's what I was
always used to from OpenVMS.  As someone mentioned in reference to
using a GUI earlier, you can't always count on that to be there. 
Personally I don't use too many aliases because when I have to
assist a user using their account or "su -" to that user, those
aliases won't be there.  I use the letter keys instead of the arrow
keys to move around in "vi" also for that same reason.  Key mappings
can get screwed up when you are logging all over the place to all
different OSs.

> > I just
> >realize that those things are part of UNIX like rebooting after
> >changing most anything is part of Windows.

> If you think they're part of UNIX, you're not paying attention.

	Either you're not paying attention or your cutting the text before
reading it first.  UNIX does inded crash on occassion and it is
legendary for corrupting the file system if not shut down
correctly.  That is just a part of the way it works.  Having to
reboot a Windows machine after doing something as trivial as
changing the IP address is just how it works (although not in NT
5.0, I hear).  It's simply the nature of the beast.

		
			      Don


