References: <timleeEz6G6t.478@netcom.com> <35FDB4AE.98C255CE@home.com> <6tkgo2$ke2@web.nmti.com> <35FE6952.D2EFFE63@home.com>
Organization: Bailey Network Management
Newsgroups: comp.unix.advocacy,comp.unix.admin,comp.sys.hp.hpux,comp.sys.sgi.admin,comp.sys.sun.admin,comp.sys.dec

In article <35FE6952.D2EFFE63@home.com>, Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:
>Peter da Silva wrote:
>> Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:
>> >       Don't the newer versions read the older formats?

>> They read them. They don't *write* them. Which means that as soon as one
>> person is using the new tool everyone has to upgrade.

>> Designing file formats that are forwards and backwards compatible is trivial.
>> There's no technical reason why Microsoft has to have abusively incompatible
>> formats... money is the only explanation.

>	FRAMEMAKER doesn't write out old formats (or, if it does, it's not
>real obvious).

Ah. You respond to my complaint about the incompetance of one proprietary
software vendor with apparent incompetance on the part of another one.

This is interesting, but it really doesn't help your argument any.

> It will only read two versions back also.  Most
>applications won't write the older formats because the reason the
>format changed in the first place was to accomodate "enhancements". 

Funny. I can still read web pages with Lynx, with no graphics, no scripts,
and the data still gets through to me. And the enhancements on the web make
the minor changes in commercial software over the same period seem trivial.

Some companies recognise this and work to avoid problems. Look at all Apple's
file format documentation, and Electronic Arts' IFF (interchange file format)
with its explicit support for backwards and forwards interoperability. Look
at the file formats that inspired, MFF and PNG. there's a whole family of
formats there that are inherently interoperable.

Look at SGML, and SGML formats, where documenting the format is considered
as important as what's in it.

This isn't rocket science. And even if it was, aren't Microsoft SUPPOSED
to be rocket scientists?


>There is no place to save those "enhancements" in the older format
>so they can't write out that way.

Bull. Totally wrong, if they designed things right in the first place. And they
can use encapsulation, any time they want to, and after that point you'll have
routine interoperability.

You just define the format so it has data sections and extensible tags
describing them, whether they're binary (like in IFF) or text (like in
SGML). The new attributes are written in new tags. The older software
ignores them.

>	It is my understanding that those programs (at least I'm pretty
>sure about "Word") also run on the MAC and the file formats are
>interchangeable.  You could always jump from the Windows frying pan
>into the MAC fire. :-)

The Mac versions aren't always up to date, so you end up sitting around
and waiting for Microsoft to release the new version. And don't tell me
that's not deliberate (not to mention abuse of monopoly... and something
your buddies in Justice should be paying attention to instead of just
whining about browsers).

>> >> That's me. You prefer walking across the building every time some user's
>> >> got a configuration problem? Or you prefer talking them through it?

>> >       I prefer "rlogin".

>> To a *windows* box? Editing the config files and registry directly is the
>> only alternative.

>	No, editing the config files and the registry directly is, in
>general, asking for trouble.  Someone was just going on in this
>thread about eschewing the GUI to edit config files manually and
>then said in the next sentence that they had recently messed up the
>"/etc/pasword" file by not terminating a line.

I think you're mixing threads up. I haven't seen that message. The only
person in this thread talking about editing config files directly is me.

>> > Every OS has it's quirks.  I don't fault UNIX for
>> >corrupting the file system if not shutdown correctly

>> It doesn't do that near as often as Windows 95.

>	Please...

OK, *you* tell my son he's imagining things next time Mech Warrior gets
eaten by file system corruption.

>> >or not allowing
>> >users to use abbreviations of the commands (like OpenVMS).

>> Man csh, look up the "alias" command.

>	An "alias" is not an abbreviation of the command.  As with SUNs
>screwup of the backspace and delete key mapping from earlier in this
>thread, the fact that you can fix (or emulate) it doesn't mean that
>it doesn't (or in this case, does) exist.  UNIX does not allow you
>to abbreviate commands, period.  You can emulate this functionality
>by composing a long list of alias that are loaded into memory every
>time you log in.

Ah. THAT "feature". I'll do without it, thanks. I've been using VMS on
and off since version 2 or 3, and I really hate re-learning commands
because some abbreviation I'd grown used to isn't unique any more.

>	Either you're not paying attention or your cutting the text before
>reading it first.  UNIX does inded crash on occassion and it is
>legendary for corrupting the file system if not shut down
>correctly.

Sure. Back in version 7 days, before the Berkeley fast file system. But
who's been using the Version 7/Xenix file system in the past 10 years?

I've lost data on Windows at least as often as I do on UNIX, and *my*
Windows boxes don't crash near as often as most people's seem to, because
I make sure I don't mess them up with broken screen savers and malware.

It's taken a lot of work, but by sticking with a reliable version of Windows
(3.51) and being very careful with what applications I let into my boxen,
I get multiple months of uninterrupted uptime. But with UNIX I get years,
without taking any special care.

-- 
