Peter da Silva wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> >       You can't kill defunct processes.  They only go away with a reboot.

> If you can't kill them without a reboot, then either you're kidding us with
> all that talk of understanding UNIX, or you have a broken device driver. Which
> you DEFINITELY should find and fix, because it's seriously broken. We had a
> problem like that on Xenix-286 for a while, with the tape drive. It was a
> nightmare, fixing it without source.

	Maybe I'm not understanding it then...  (Hey, I can admit it as
easily as the next guy.).  It's late in the afternoon and all the
users have logged out and gone home for the day.  You "rlogin" to a
system for some reason or another and do a "ps agx" or "ps -ef"
(depending on the OS).  You see processes listed as "defunct" and
nothing is running save the bare minimum to have the machine up
(i.e., no applications, no window manager, no nothing except things
like "ypbind" and such).  Doing a "kill -9" on the "defunct"
processes just returns a "no such process".  Now they may not be
taking up any CPU cycles or anything but they're taking up space in
the process table and they're annoying me so they've got to go. 
Reboot.  Don't get the idea that this is some kind of rampant,
chronic problem or anything.  It just happens now and then and a
simple, painless reboot takes care of it just fine.

