Don,

In article <35FDB114.632694E3@home.com>, Rev. Don Kool wrote:
>  Which is perfectly OK as long as you're not looking down your nose
> at someone who gets by just fine with a Windows box as some kind of
> unenlightened dolt.  If your windows machines are crashing all the
> time and losing your work, you're not doing something right.  I've
> been running windows in some version or another at home since '93
> and I've never lost a byte.  Just like with UNIX at work, I've had
> to restore from tape backup a time or two but such are the
> vicissitudes of life.  Tens of millions of satisfied customers have
> voted with their dollars and more do so every day.  They can't all
> be wrong and they can't all be losing countless hours of hard work
> to system crashes on a daily basis.  PCs with Windows work just
> fine.  Workstations and servers with UNIX run just fine.  Why the
> "religious war"?  Trashing one doesn't make the other any better.

My experience is half like yours.

I have an old WFW3.11 system that never crashes. I had a few minor problems with it, but the upgrade to WFW3.11 
cleaned most of them up. However, I do have a resource monitor running full time, and when anything goes below 40%, 
I reboot. Since it is no longer our primary system, this rarely happens, and mostly we reboot it for power reasons 
once or twice every couple of months. I still have about 40 DC2020 and 2020XL tapes that I used for backup. The only 
data ever lost was files created and deleted between backups.

My newer Win95 machine is a different matter. Since day 1 it has been unstable, and no amount of careful pruning and 
tweaking and application of patches has fixed it. Of course, a lot more is asked of it than the old system, but even 
when using nothing but built-in accessories there seem to be memory leaks and conflicts that eventually require a 
reboot. If I use it for anything more sophisticated, like actual work, I need to reboot more than once a day. If 
anyone plays any sort of game on it, they are required to reboot when done, so the machine will be relatively clean 
for the next family user. Again, it supports a lot more hardware (although most of that used to live on the WFW3.11 
system before and had no trouble there) and there is more going on in the background than on the old machine. The 
point is, I need to reboot several times a day, just to clean up bad application and OS code. Again, I rarely lose 
work, because I back up regularly, originally on a TR-3 drive, now on 4mm.

