> > It doesn't do that near as often as Windows 95.

> 	Please...

???  You really believe that Windows 95/98 doesn't corrupt filesystems
more often than Unix?  What planet do you live on?

> 	An "alias" is not an abbreviation of the command.

An alias is precisely an abbreviation of a command.  Of course, it's
an abbreviation you have to define.  If you don't like that, just use
command completion, which all the modern shells support.  Hell, bash
will even try to correct your spelling if you misspell a directory
name of something similar.  Now personally I hate that (I don't want
the computer second-guessing me) so I turn it off, but it is there.

> 	Either you're not paying attention or your cutting the text before
> reading it first.  UNIX does inded crash on occassion 

Very, very rarely.  This DEC box (running OSF/1) has never, *ever*
crashed due to software in the 4 years that I've had it -- it crashed
one time, when the machine was relatively new, due to a bad memory
module, but that's it.  My uptime is measured in months between
voluntary reboots (or power failures) rather than in days or hours.

> and it is
> legendary for corrupting the file system if not shut down
> correctly.

No, it is not "legendary" for that at all.  In fact, there are quite a
few filesystems these days that will never ever need even an fsck if
the machine is turned off unceremoniously.  No data lost, no file
system check, no nothing.  Just restart and go.  And even less
advanced filesystems that aren't "crash-proof" almost always recover
after an fsck -- I haven't lost a file since the System III/PDP-11
days (that was...  well...  a few years ago to say the least).

> That is just a part of the way it works.

Apparently you haven't used Unix in a while...

And for a comment in a different posting:  you said you don't allow
users "root", but then totally ignore the fact that on a Windows box
ANYONE sitting at the machine is effectively "root".  To be worried
about lack of security when a Unix users is "root" and not to be
worried about the total lack of security provided by Windows is, well,
to put it nicely, inconsistent.

