In article <35FE5D2B.A073677B@home.com>, Rev. Don Kool wrote:
> No worries of trashing the file system like when a UNIX
> box hangs.
>

Not sure which versions of UNIX you are talking about here, but I work 
with AIX, HP/UX and linux, and have yet to have any file system 
corruption ever (that was not automatically repaired on reboot) in the 
6 years of working with them. And yes, we have had hard crashes because 
of electricians turning off the UPS instead of the power behind the 
UPS. Everyone of the 15 AIX, 2 HP/UX and 1 linux system came back up, 
cleaned up after itself and went on with nary a hiccup. Later some of 
the hard drives on some of the AIX systems started exhibiting signs of 
degradation and had to be swapped out, but the file systems on them 
stayed clean and were able to be migrated off to other disks before 
replacement, in real time, with no visible impact to the users. Not 
even degraded response time. Then, late on a Sunday night with no one 
using the system, they were brought down and the hard drives swapped. 
We have even swapped hard drives without bringing the system down, but 
that obviously requires advanced planning and purchase of the right 
kinds of drives and drive systems.

And I have left my laptop running linux when someone else in the family 
decided it was time to turn it off and put it away. It came back up 
later, cleaned itself up and ran fine. No lost data except perhaps the 
last record or two of some log files that were actively being written 
to at the time. But those files were intact and usable in every other 
way.

