In comp.sys.sun.admin Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:
> Timothy J. Lee wrote:
>> "Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> writes:

>> |       UNIX does what it does very well, very expensive and very stable
>> |for its users.  PCs do what they do very cheaply

>> Ummm, Unix is cheaper than Microsoft OSes.  E.g. Linux, BSD, Solaris
>> (non-commercial version for $10 + shipping), SCO (non-commercial version
>> for some cheap price that's less than Microsoft OSes).

> 	Please... the minute you talk about some form of free pseudo-UNIX
> on a PC, we're talking a different language.  PCs are for games and
> windows.  If you want to do UNIX, buy an ALPHA (or HP, or SGI or
> even a SUN).

I have been following these threads for a bit, but have to take some
issue, here.....

First, not all of us can afford an Alpha or HP or SGI or SUN (unless we
have a good edge with the surplus recyclers).  This is especially true
for the home play boxes.  The ``pseudo-UNIX'' softwares provide a very
good, and complete approximation to the real thing (and some ARE the
real thing -- notably the BSD variants).  Pricewise, at free or almost
free, that is a big incentive over GatesOS and related wares.

Second, I have had the fortune (or bane) to have played some with all
of the minor ``pseudo-UNIX'' softwares over the past 10 years that I
could lay hands on (about 10 variants, and many releases of each).
Granted, the earlier releases were struggling at becoming complete
and reliable, BUT, of recent times, they are as complete and reliable
as any of the commercial variants.  Usually, they are more complete,
with the occasional caveat that bug fixes may ensue if you are on the
exact cutting edge at any given time.  But, bug fixes, if needed, are
usually more forthcoming out of the ``pseudo-UNIX'' softwares.  That
is mostly due to a larger, and sometimes more vocal, user base.

Third, although the PC platform has its share of hardware inconsistencies,
Unix on a PC is no different from Unix on a workstation, is no different
from Unix on a mainframe, is no different from Unix on a.... (pdp-11 ...
well, within limits).  Where differences DO seem to occur it is mainly
a function of the vendor's particular hardware/software hacks added onto
Unix, or a function of particular sofware packages that may or may not
be ported to a particular flavor of Unix.  That is where the gotchas seem
to arise, mostly.  That will change with time, until, virtually everything
can be found on every flavor.  If you sit down at a biggie workstation,
and then a lowendian ``pseudo-UNIX'' workstation, you will sense what
I am driving at.  The only differences are speed and size, and the
larger PCish machines can easily hold their own with the high-end
machines.  The differences are diminishing over time.

Fourth, I sense that over the next 5-10 years, that the major players
had better wake up, or they will find themselves in the position of
being downgraded to medium level players, AND, that the ``pseudo-UNIX''
players will find themselves upgraded from lowendian to medium level
players.  Overall, that is good for Unix, but may be hard on the biggie
players.

Whatever you folks do, DON'T, repeat DON'T, discount the ``pseudo-UNIX''
softwares, since they are the Unix platforms of the masses, and the future.

Food for thought....

RDK




