>them.  Additionally, while you can reallocate CPU's between domains
>without rebooting, applications such as Oracle and SAP R/3 have to 
>be shut down and restarted.

In article <6te0oq$slm@alumni.rpi.edu>, jonesm2@alumni.rpi.edu (Michael David Jones) writes:
> You don't have to shut down *anything* to move system boards between
> partitions. It does take several minutes to migrate processes running
> on the CPUs of a system board to be moved and to drain the memory on

I belive the point is, while the OS and hardware don't have to be shut
down, the Oracle and R/3 applications have intimate knowledge of the number
of processors, the size of memory, etc.  If you change those on the fly,
you need to stop the applications and restart it before it will take
advantage of the new resources.  Ditto for deleting resources.

Secondarily, once these applications have noticed the new configuration, it
takes a substantial amount of time to get their memory into it's hot,
stable state.  Given that, it may not save any time to detect a
configuration change on the fly.

Yes, Oracle and R/3 could (and probably will) be re-coded to notice changes
in processors on the fly, at some point, but it doesn't handle it yet.

-- Pete "100% R/3-free" Smoot
   Not speaking for HP.




