>and i found that the proprietary hardware
>	a) is better than PC hardware

Far and away.

>	b) the OS is better adapted to the proprietary hardware

It's always easier to support a set of hardware that you determine
yourself instead of trying to support whatever the Wintel industry throws
out there.  But it keeps prices way up.

>My opinion is: The PC hardware is crap! It deserves to run Windows.
>But if you have a stable PC configuration (by buying expensive
>hardware or by pure luck or by praying for a wonder), then you
>can slurp a Unix (Solaris x86, NetBSD or Linux) onto it and get
>as much comfort as on a proprietary unix machine. 

Agreed.  PC unix users must choose their hardware very carefully.  If you
know you're going to be running unix, you can stick to the recommended
hardware list for whichever flavor you're using, in which case you're
pretty safe.  My FreeBSD machine is rock-solid, because I went with common
hardware from reputable vendors and stayed away from IDE.  It's when you
convert a Windows machine that you can run into problems, with WinModems,
flaky SoundBlaster clones, and bad memory (non-parity of course) that was
never stressed hard enough to fail under Windows. 

What makes this all worth it is stunning price/performance on the low to
middle end for most server applications.  Obviously, for some things
you're going to need better I/O bandwidth or floating point, but for
something simple like web-serving, there's no problem.

[snip PC hardware purchase woes]

You just have to either put it together from parts yourself, or buy from
one of the PC vendors who specialize in unix systems, like Accurate
Automation or VAResearch (is that what they're called?) 

