In article <6t3e4c$bjm$1@pegasus.csx.cam.ac.uk>, der1004@cus.cam.ac.uk (Duncan Reed) writes:
>We currently have 2 alphaserver 2100's which are starting to run out 
>of steam. They each have 2 4/275 cpu's and 1 gig of memory (1/2 gig base
>+ 1/2 gig upgrade)
>
>Reading the digital docs I see that if you add 2 more cpu's you are
>limited to 1 gig memory. I therefore conclude we have 2 free slots.
>I.e. 4 slots with 2 occupied with additional CPU and memory.
>
>Is it possible to have 3 CPU's in a 2100? I've only ever seen 1,2 or 4.
>
>Assuming we had a free slot for more memory, can a 1 gig board be
>fitted or is the most that can be fitted in a slot 1/2 gig.
>
>I see that 5/300 CPU will fit in a 2100 are these the fastest? To do
>this do we need to make any other changes (hardware or software)?
>
>We are currently running OpenVMS 7.1 & Oracle 7.3.2.3.2
>

Duncan,


Yes you can certainly fit 5/300 CPUs which are the fastest for the 2100
systems with VMS. There was going to be a faster CPU ( 5/350 ? ) but this
aparently caused problems and was canned.
You will need to replace all your old CPUs since you cannot mix different
types of CPUs.
The largest memory board for the 2100 is 512MB.

As I recall you should be able to fit 3 CPUs and 2GB of memory - it is only
when you fit the 4th CPU you that you are reduced to 1GB of memory.

David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader
Middlesex University 



