Gregg E Economou <geest3+@pitt.edu> wrote:
>I was just going to say, that stuff will run on single-phase,
>provided, however, its 220V and you have a well-wired feed. You can
>easily get a '750 configuration that can suck down 20 or 25 amps. 
>(at 220 :-) 

You have a 220V 11/750?  That's a definite oddity in the US.  Mine -- and
all the ones I've stolen parts from, and all the ones I ever used, to my
knowledge (there were a few I never poked around inside, come to think of
it) were 110V 60Hz single-phase.

It may have been possible to construct configurations, using all DEC
parts but rather unusual Massbus-attached disks, which required both
110 and 220, or both single-phase and three-phase.  In my experience,
however, the most common configuration, a 11/750 with either a TE16 or
TU[78]x tape drive and RA-series disks, was certainly a 110v, 60Hz beast.

With the Massbus adapter pulled, mine draws well under 20A at 110V.  I
don't have an FP750, however, and I only have 6MB of memory.

25A at 220V is quite unlikely.  The master power supply for the whole machine
has a 20A 220V fuse, IIRC, and the plug which was originally on the cord
coming out of it was the funky plug for a 30A 110V outlet.

