	Sure they've changed.  All the software has gotten bigger. :-)

	I still don't see any UNIX versions of software sitting on the
shelves of the local "Best Buy" though.  You can have the best OS in
the world and it can stay up for years at a time but if there aren't
any applications for it, Harry Homeowner isn't going to automate too
many chores around the house with it.  A couple of years ago I
picked up an $8.00 deck designing program for Windows (sorry, no
LINUX port).  It was a basic kind of CAD thing.  You clicked on the
outline of the deck and pulled it out to the size you wanted,
configured how high it was going to be off the ground, what kind of
railing, horizontal or diagonal layout of the decking, etc...  You
then modified the default prices for lumber with the actual ones
from your local lumberyard and it printed out an itemized list of
all the wood, all the fasteners and all the cement you were going to
need along with the price.  You can lay out a simple deck literally
in five minutes, get an accurate estimate of the cost and print out
a materials list to take to the store.  

	I was just thumbing through a Parson's Technology catalog and sent
away for a genealogy program that I thought might be interesting. 
It comes with four or five CDROMs full of years of Social Security
records, birth records, death records, etc. to help you trace it
out.  You can scan in pictures, documents or whatever and attach
them to each person too.  I've never thought about doing something
like this before but I bought it on a lark because it sounded like
something that would be fun to do before more of the old ones in the
family go on to their reward.  A while back I picked up their
"Family Lawyer" program and knocked out a will, living will, power
of attorney, etc...

	I even downloaded a program off of the Internet that is basically a
bartender book with over 2000 drinks in it (plus you can add your
own).  Kind of fun looking up the grossest drink.  There wasn't a
UNIX version available.

	This type of software that you're probably turning you nose up at
doesn't get written for PC UNIX.  It gets written for PC Windows
because of the huge installed base.  This base means that an
application doesn't have to be something that "everyone has to have"
(e.g. e-mail client, browser, file manager, etc...) in order to be a
viable, money making venture.  Those that program to niche markets
can also make a living.  

	When I look at a PC, I see something that sits on the desk in the
Den, eases Dad's burden of paying the bills or doing taxes, provides
some entertainment for Junior, helps Sis research and write her term
paper and holds Mom's secret recipes.  It is a simple tool of modest
aspirations yet incredibly useful to millions of people all over the
world who don't know squat about computers and don't care to learn.

	When I want something that is bulletproof for critical
applications, I think of a DEC ALPHA running OpenVMS.

	When I want something that is extremely reliable, follows
standards, makes a good development platform and has a lot of good
people developing a lot of good applications for it, I think of UNIX
servers and workstations.

	Then again, that's just where I stand.  This thread is all about
opinions and, for what they're worth, those are mine.

