They read them. They don't *write* them. Which means that as soon as one
person is using the new tool everyone has to upgrade.

Designing file formats that are forwards and backwards compatible is trivial.
There's no technical reason why Microsoft has to have abusively incompatible
formats... money is the only explanation.

> Besides "Money"
>(and Windows itself) I don't run any Microsoft software.

If we didn't have to run Microsoft applications we would have no reason
to run Microsoft Windows at work. Word, Excel, and so on are treated as the
file interchange format of the '90s, and we HAVE to be compatible with our
vendors and customers.

