>have to reboot my home PC running Win98 about once every couple
>weeks.  If I come in in the morning and it's hung, I just hit the
>"reset" button and go brush my teeth or something.  No great
>inconvenience.

Except when you forgot to close your application or it corrupts the file
system (and yes, it does do that. NT 4.0 prior to SP1 was notorious for
doing that, and I've had to reinstall games on my son's PC many times
because it'd trashed some open file when it had been turned off without
shutting down... to much wailing if he had unsaved state that he'd lost).

I find this attitude inconceivable. It's like having a car that turns itself
off at random every couple of weeks.

> Every OS has it's quirks.  I don't fault UNIX for
>corrupting the file system if not shutdown correctly

It doesn't do that near as often as Windows 95.

>or not allowing
>users to use abbreviations of the commands (like OpenVMS).

Man csh, look up the "alias" command.

> I just
>realize that those things are part of UNIX like rebooting after
>changing most anything is part of Windows.

If you think they're part of UNIX, you're not paying attention.

