Leslie Mikesell wrote:
> Rev. Don Kool <oldno7@home.com> wrote:

> >> >       Maintenance reboot.  Gotta clear out those "defunct" processes once
> >> >in a while.

> >> If you're accumulating defunct processes on a UNIX system you broke it
> >> somewhere.

> >       In reality no UNIX system runs for the better part of a year (as
> >mentioned in the section you snipped without annotation) without
> >"accumulating defunct processes".  I load the OS and set up the NFS
> >mounts.  It's the application programmers "job" to break shit.  :-)

> What *are* you talking about?  I've had uptimes over a year on unix
> boxes with nothing unusual in the way of processes. 

	So have I.  Boxes that sit in the corner and act as a print server
or something equally innocuous.  Boxes that people use, abuse, work
on and develop on end up with defunct processes (and other crap)
eventually.  Why is everybody so afraid to reboot a system?  Is
there some kind of high dollar prize that gets awarded to the sys
admin who can go the longest without a reboot or something?   We
have some shops at work that set cron jobs to reboot all their
systems once a week.  A nice clean reboot every now and then keeps
systems humming along.  Defunct processes, applications that
allocate memory and then don't release it when they quit, stale NFS
file handles, bunch of junk in "/tmp", etc...  A nice "maintenance
reboot" now and then cleans all that crap out quite nicely, thank
you.  Set it to go off in the middle of the night and no one has to
have that awful two minute wait for it to reboot.

> The free versions
> get enough useful upgrades that I usually down them every 6 months
> or so to reload (and it takes less time than getting authorization
> to buy a version upgrade for Solaris).

	So you have to reload the OS every six months?  Sounds
"convenient".  Doesn't that require a reboot?

> >> >       With the only problem being that you then have "gcc". :-)  Real
> >> >compilers cost money for a reason.

> >> And this reason is?

> >       Compiled code runs faster, for one.  Perhap I am in the minority in
> >considering this a good thing.

> Consider 'total cost' before you make this kind of comparison.  Compare
> gcc-compiled programs on a machine where you spent the cost of the
> uneeded commercial compiler on a faster CPU or more RAM instead.

	We must be the only ones out there who aren't broke.  


