"Rev. Don Kool" <oldno7@home.com> writes:
|	Standards and support issues.  When the word comes down from on
|high that, after this date, Netscape 4 is our standard browser,
|that's what you configure.  It's good in some ways, bad in others. 
|Almost universally users hate change.  If no one took old
|applications away from them, we would still have some people writing
|documents in WordStar.  Fine if all they're going to do is print it
|out.  Not so good if they're going to e-mail it to somebody who's
|not going to be able to read it.  FRAMEMAKER is our "standard" word
|processor.  You should hear the gripes about that!

Emailing documents in bloated, proprietary formats is not a good
thing.  Much better would be to simply require that all documents
be made available in some of the following (a) print, (b) plain
text, (c) HTML, (d) Postscript, (e) PDF, since reading any of these
formats does not require a program of limited availability.  Instead
of emailing, putting the document on the internal web server, or
emailing the location from where interested people can get the
document, ought to be preferred.

Of course, if some of the programs are expensive, then there could
still be political fights over which one gets bought, but at least one
can allow user choice with the free stuff (e.g. mail clients, web
browsers, some word processors like LaTeX and groff).  Forcing a
particular program on every user just leads to gripes about how the
chosen program does not meet everyone's needs, etc..  Not being beholden
to one single vendor for a type of application also has its advantages
(e.g. if the price gets too expensive, even the users who favor that
application may be convinced to use another if they see others
successfully using another, rather than switching without knowing about
the quality of the alternative).

