Hi!

void wrote:
> unix and "if you want to do unix, buy unix-vendor hardware" until I
> challenge you to learn a little about what you're attacking, and suddenly
> you have no opinion.  Cute.

Ok, then i'll feed in arguments for the "unix-vendor hardware":
I have worked with both proprietary and PC hardware for years
and i found that the proprietary hardware
	a) is better than PC hardware
	b) the OS is better adapted to the proprietary hardware
Hey, i'm NOT willing to argue about that, i state FACTS!
BTW: You pay a lot of bucks for a) and b) to the vendor.

> >> Some PC unixes are derived from the
> >> same branch of the unix family tree that brought you TCP/IP, demand-paged
> >> memory, and (alas) the r* protocols.
> >
> >       Not really.  I dismiss PC UNIX as an area for hobbiests and
> >tinkerers.
> 
> Well, if you're just going to flatly contradict verifiable facts with no
> explanation, I think this discussion is over.

There are not only free Unices for proprietary hardware (Linux, NetBSD),
but professional Unixes for PC hardware (Solaris x86).
And many big Unix vendors have announced a port to the Merced.

My opinion is: The PC hardware is crap! It deserves to run Windows.
But if you have a stable PC configuration (by buying expensive
hardware or by pure luck or by praying for a wonder), then you
can slurp a Unix (Solaris x86, NetBSD or Linux) onto it and get
as much comfort as on a proprietary unix machine. 
But if you want to use such machines in a professional environment,
you'll face new problems. Say you have a working machine. You've
thoroughfully tested it and you're sure, it WILL do what you need.
Now you want to build another 5 of them. You go to your favourite
PC crap store and you find out that:
	a) The motherboard has another BIOS version
	b) The CPU is no longer available, you need a faster one
	b) The graphics card now has a Voodoo chip onboard
	c) The 2GB disk you have used is MORE EXPENSIVE than
	   one of the brand new 4GB.
	d) The 24x CDROM has magically muted to 32x
	e) Keyboard and Mouse are (despite of the IDENTICAL package)
	   more crappy than the 3 month old ones.
You haven't paid a penny more, but you got either better or crappier
hardware than three months ago. But you got DIFFERENT hardware.
And while Windows will run fine on the new machines, Linux won't.

Ciao!

Peter



