Scott Collins wrote:
> 
> If in fact the UE10000 can act as a 64 CPU SMP machine and not as as
> 16 x 4 processor quads, then SUN has a problem.  There is a formula to
> calculate the performance increase gained on SMP systems as it
> relates to SMP efficiency.  Using these widely accepted formulas at
> the rather abysmal (compared to DEC or IBM) SMP efficiency rate of
> 92% the UE10000 stops gaining performance over about 28 CPU's.  This
> is the exact reason why NUMA architectures were invented.
> 

Firstly the E10000 is a UMA system not a NUMA system 
that is to say all CPU's have equal speed access to all 
the systems memory. 

Sun does not have a problem because the E10K has a 16x16 
cross bar which has a bandwidth of >12 GB/s and which 
can sustain >10 GB/s. 

The scalability of the E10000 is good we for instance 
show very good scalability of SAP up to 64 CPUs and SAP 
is an OLTP benchmark which can stress the scalability 
of most systems.

The widely accepted formulas tended to take a pessimistic 
view about the growth in backplane bandwidth. The fact 
is at least on an E10000 backplane bandwidth is not a 
problem even at the 64 CPU mark.

> The bigger issue on the NUMA/Quad architecture UE10000's is the
> effect that near/far memory access latencies have on performance.
> Once again, the type of application and how well it lends itself
> to an architecture should be your deciding factor.  Furthermore,
> as a professional in the SAP field, at this time there are only
> a handfull of SAP R/3 implementations worldwide that NEED this
> much processing power.  Most large SAP implementations end up
> getting split into a few interconnected systems for business
> and availability reasons anyway.

The current crop of NUMA systems tend to be very inefficient 
at commercial workloads. For example Sequent have a result 
of 2801 SAP SD users using 32 CPU's. Taking into account 
their system load they are doing about 12.5 SD users per 
SPECint, HP and Sun would expect to do about 24 SD users 
per SPECint at that fairly low performance level. 

Simular levels of inefficiency also show up in TPC-D 
benchmarks. 

In addition despite announcing NUMA-Q with support for 
up to 256 CPUs over a year ago Sequent have currently 
only advanced to 32 CPUs currently with very few 
customers running more than 16 CPUs in production 
systems. 

One possible reason for this is that average latencies 
at least for Intel NUMA systems are currently way higher 
than something like an E10000. The E10000 has an everage 
latency of ~500ns fully loaded with 64 CPUs. According 
to Sequent a 16 CPU NUMA-Q box has an average latency 
of ~2000ns and this is using a rather optimistic local
to remote memory ratio. 

Finally, while many customer do use one very large 
E10000 running one very large application, many 
use the domain facility in the E10000 to split it 
into a number of dynamic domains each say 8-24 CPUs 
in size.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



