Scott Collins wrote:

> Additionally, while you can reallocate CPU's between domains
> without rebooting, applications such as Oracle and SAP R/3 have to
> be shut down and restarted.  This imposes an INCREDIBLE performance
> penalty on R/3 systems as you must completely rebuild your cache to
> achieve good performance which can take 6 to 8 hours on an active
> system.

Actually from what I understand, you do not have to shut down
any applications when reallocating system boards on the E10000.
In effect, the memory being moved is flushed to disk, and the
applications continue to run, paging the data back in as needed. As
you say, if the cache was in the memory you're moving around, you
will have to wait for it to be repopulated, which can be a significant
performance hit.

The question of how to partitition very large systems for
non-scientific workloads - that is, jobs which do not parallelize
easily - is an interesting area of comparison between various
Unix (and other) OS's. Sun doesn't have a monopoly in this
area of development.

