The big mail order PC house even change motherboards often.  I
remember a while back that a group ordered 10 machines so they
would be idential for testin.  I think there were 3 different
motherboards in use.

In the Penitum arena the only decent motherboards used HX chip set,
which was the only one that was designated as a server chip.

-------- from a posting by someone I know and trust ------

There's also the not so minor problem that many motherbored
chipsets will *NOT* cache anything over 64MB.  I've now had 3ea
clones where I've had to reduce the ram to 64MB in order to get
decent performance.  See:
  http://developer.intel.com/design/pcisets/applnots/memory.htm
for clues.  Basically:
	430LX  192MB
	430VX   64MB
	430Tx   64MB
	430HX  512MB
	440FX    4GB
Also see the chart at the bottom of:
  http://www.motherboards.org/chips.html#FAQ

----- end of included text ----

You'll note that the chip that is perceived to be the most potent -
the current PII - is primarily supported by the LX chip set.  192MB
is getting small in this world.   And as to my above comments on
the HX you'll note that 512MB vx the 64MB is one reason iNTEL
designates it as a server chip.   Most 'over the counter' PC's
are built to a price point, and the lower the better.

	
