George wrote in message ...
>PC's are being outfitted by Linux in mssive quantities. DECs got their
>Linux too. SGI IRIX is a flavor of UNIX. Mac OS X (due in 1999) is UNIX
>too.


Unfortunately, Mac OS X is *NOT* unix.  Don't let the 'X' fool you...  It's
simply Mac OS 10. The unix-based OS that was to be released (and will be
released) on the Mac is "Rhapsody."  However, this operating system is
already DOA because Apple has announced there will be no future updates to
this software.  So many developers (actually almost all) have jumped ship.

>What will be the difference between the various manufacturers' boxes in a
>year or two ? They will be all running the same UNIX operating system... :)


People were saying this 2 years ago, ...saying it 5 years ago, and also
saying it 10 years ago.  It's not going to happen...  At least not for a
long time.  Even if all the personal computers were equipped with Linux
starting today, 99% of the world wouldn't pull away from their Windows or
MacOS.  Most users still depend on the more direct and easy-use offered by
these OS's and it's what they're familiar with and have been trained for.

The best choice for an economical desktop Unix right now is Linux.  In the
next two years, your going to see systems offering the choice of Windows
2000 (AKA: NT 6.0), Linux, and Mac OS X on the PowerPC G3/G4 systems.  These
systems will probably have a decent incarnation of Linux by then and Apple
keeps talking about porting Mac OS X to Intel machines.  The current problem
with Linux is its lack of power for graphics/visualization and multimedia.
Once hardware accelerated OpenGL is available for Linux and greater
multimedia support is added, hardware manufactures will be more eager to
offer their support.  This will in turn attract more developers and Linux
will become a more realistic alternative to NT.

Linux is a good solution now only if you don't need graphics or multimedia.

