storing a PC UNIX that no one ever used.  Moral of the story; except
for developers, applications are the key.

	As far as today's PC UNIXs go, I have no exposure to them except to
hear a lot of kids tout them.  From what I hear and read, its
biggest "advantage" is that it isn't "windoze".  Not exactly
compelling.  More like cutting off your nose to spite your face in
my estimation.  As I said, PC UNIX is not an option at my work.  The
only way I will ever see it is if I end up with an "extra" PC at
home and the time and inclination to play with it.  I am as likely
to sit down and learn how PC UNIXs work as I am to sit down and
figure out PlayStation so that I can write my own game (most of
which are written on SGIs BTW).

> Some PC unixes are derived from the
> same branch of the unix family tree that brought you TCP/IP, demand-paged
> memory, and (alas) the r* protocols.

	Not really.  I dismiss PC UNIX as an area for hobbiests and
tinkerers.  A great way to learn UNIX fundamentals with little $$$,
but nowhere near a viable solution for a Fortune 500 company or a
Government agency.  When these "free" UNIXs go belly up at Y2K, who
do we call?  There ain't no Ghostbusters!  There is a large
difference between, "Hey, let's try this.  It might be neat." and
"If this computer goes down for 5 minutes, we lose a million
dollars.".

	I have nothing against the PC UNIXs.  I merely point out that their
enthusiasts should realize they aren't the real thing.  

			Yours in Christ,
			      Don


