> > > the left. This is just one of those things.

> >       "One of those things" that makes a SUN annoying to use.

> You can't control the apps.

	Hmmm... as I recall, several people on this forum were chiding me
for rebooting a computer that had been up over a hundred days rather
than fixing every app that contributed to "defunct" processes.  Now
the worm has apparently turned.

> That has nothing to do with Sun. The apps
> I refer to run on a number of platforms, but which app works which way
> is up to the vendor, and not tied to any OS or hardware platform. You
> can't blame Sun for the app developers.
> (Talking about third party commercial apps)

	You're right.  I can blame SUN for the fact that when I log into a
system I have to use the delete key and after I start their archaic
OPENWINDOWS window manager, I have to use backspace for the same
function.  How convenient and efficacious for touch typers.  The
fact remains that even if SUN ran applications faster than everyone
in the world and had the best compilers in the world (neither of
which they have by a long shot) the computing experience on one of
their workstations would still be a torturous one.  

