> What will be the difference between the various manufacturers' boxes
> in a year or two ? They will be all running the same UNIX operating
> system...

Not exactly.  Unix is coming to the desktop computer for much the
same reason that scalable font technology did: as expectations of
quality increase, the novices adopt the tools of the professionals. 
Unix is the operating system of computing professionals, and has
been for over twenty years.

But the diffent flavours of Unix are not all equivilent.  While they
share many of the same processor and memory allocation algorythms
(i.e. low-level technologies), their APIs for display of graphics,
font handling, network communication, etc. (i.e. high-level
technologies) are all different.  So a binary compiled for one
flavour of Unix will not usually run under another.

In the Unix world up until now, that has not been a problem.  Most
software was free and you didn't get the binary, you got the source
code, which you then compiled on your machine.  But in the world of
personal computers, software is propritary, source code is secret,
and the end user gets only a compiled binary.  In would be nice if
the Unix-world software distribution model won out, but this seems doubtful.

So while the increasing similiarity of low-level technologies in
operating systems is a great boon to software developers, it will
not make your neighbor's PC running Linux look like your Mac running
OS X, and it will not make software exchange between the two of you
any easier.



