Terry C. Shannon wrote:
> 
> Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> >                   Sun       Digital
> > SD Users          14400     5312
> > Dialog Steps      4725000   1649000
> > CPU's             64        10
> > DBMS load         82%       95%
> > Effective CPUs    52.4       9.5
> > Average response  0.97 secs 1.6 secs
> > time
> >
> > I don't think there is anything in the Sun results to give
> > Digital any cause for celebration.
> All of which beggars one question: what are the relative costs of the
> two configurations? I haven't a clue as to what a fully-loaded UE10K
> costs, but I have to think it's one heck of a lot more than a 10-way
> TurboLaser.
> 

Oh I think you can assume that the capital cost of the E10Ks 
were somewhat larger then the 10 Way TurboLaser. But this 
would miss the point to a certain extent.

1.	Sun only used 8 Systems to get our result, Digital used
	54 servers. So to at least match the Sun config in terms 
	of performance Digital would need 162 Machines vs 8. 
	
	It will obviously cost much more to manage 162 machines 
	than 8.

2.	Most customers also want their systems to be 
	reliable. The 8400 while being a well engineered 
	system cannot get close to the E10K in terms 
	of availability capabilities. The E10K for example 
	has no single points of failure, the 8400 has 
	many SPOF's. The E10K has hot CPU/IO/PSU/FANS/Memory
	etc the 8400 has no hot plugged components except disks.

	So to meet the uptime capabilities of the E10K 
	you may need 2 x 8400 clustered. 

3.	The TurboLaser/E10K comparision is missplaced, 
	cometition for the TL in the Sun range is 
	either the E4500 or the E6500. These cost less 
	than the E10K. Digital don't have anything 
	competative to the E10K in their product range 
	as the SAP result should illustrate fairly 
	clearly.

Finally performance is an absolute, price is not. 
Customers requiring over 5000 SD users or 30,000 
SAPs are unlikely to consider Digital. 

regards 
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



