Digital Officials Cite Pressure From Microsoft-NYT

NEW YORK (Reuters) - Five current and former executives of Digital Equipment
Corp. said their company was forced to drop a planned Internet product last
year under pressure from Microsoft Corp. (MSFT - news) Chairman Bill Gates,
The New York Times reported Thursday.

This offers new evidence supporting the government's allegations that
Microsoft routinely suppresses competition in any new market that might
threaten its monopoly on personal computer operating systems, the report said.

The Digital executives said their company disbanded a product development
group after Gates told Robert Palmer, who was then Digital's chief executive,
that a product the group was developing with Oracle Corp. (ORCL - news) would
threaten an earlier deal it had with Microsoft. That deal was to develop a
version of Microsoft's Windows NT operating system for Digital's new
processor, the Times reported.

A Microsoft spokesman Wednesday said his company placed no improper pressure
on Digital, the report said.



