In <6t4h4d$mo4$1@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca> shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca (Tim Shoppa) writes:

>Are you sure you want to say that?  I'd call them a feature of the
>PDP-11 instruction set before I called them a feature of MACRO-11, but
...
>For example, if in PDP-11 assembler I want to move a string
>pointed to by R2 to an area pointed to by R3, I just say:
>
>LOOP:	MOVB (R2)+,(R3)+
>	BNE LOOP

In other words, the PDP-11 instruction set does NOT contain *any*
instruction for C string manipulation, you have to do that with 
programmed loops, just as you'd do it on any other processor.  Using your
logic, this is a feature of any processor's instruction set.

>If I tried to do this any other way - say by prepending the string length,
>or by appending a FF or a '$' - the code gets longer 

Again, testing against zero takes fewer bytes on many instruction sets.

>(though not necessarily slower...)

If the operation is not copying the string but something needing the
length of the string (e.g. strlen() or strcat()), prepending the string 
length is provably faster.  Try to implement strlen() for Pascal-style
strings in PDP-11 assembler and you'll discover that Pascal-style
strings are a feature of the PDP-11 instruction set :-)  It takes exactly
one instruction, besides the RET (RTS R6).  The same thing for C strings
(optimized for instruction count) would be something like:

	MOV -1,R0
LOOP:   INC R0
	TSTB (R2)+
	BNE LOOP

Dan
