Forth symbol strings are a feature of the PDP-11 instruction set, too:

LOOP:	MOVB	(R1)+,(R2)+
	BLE	LOOP		;may be terminated by high bit set or nul

Forth counted strings are a feature of C:

	type("string", sizeof "string"-1);

And the PDP-11:

	BNE	R3,END
LOOP:	MOVB	(R1)+,(R2)+
	DSZ	R3, LOOP

OK, like, C string constants bias it slightly towards nul-terminated strings,
but not anything like the way strings in higher level languages do. And having
two indications available for the string length (the result of sizeof and the
terminator) means you can go either way easily. From experience in other
languages that have NO alternative to a standard string format, or which
don't give you ANY clues as to the length of a string if you don't provide
your own terminator (can you say PL/M?), this teeny bias is like nothing.

