In article <6t6547$h65$1@probity.mcc.ac.uk>,
	helbig@man.ac.uk (Phillip Helbig) writes:

> As I am not a hardware guy, I would appreciate it if anyone could fill
> in the missing bits in the following table and/or correct anything which
> is wrong.  Some if this stuff I have now, some I'm expecting to get
> soon.  Some I will keep, some I will give away.  I assume that all the 
> computers will run the latest version of VMS.

	Maybe, maybe not. Let's have a look here...

	<snip>

> ==============================================================================
> ROUGH DESCRIPTION:              VAXstation II/GPX with TK50 accessible
>                                 from the front, `heating unit' with many 
>                                 connections at the back

	Fortunately, I own two of these wonderful old beasts. Let's see...
 
> SERIES:                         
> 
> MODEL NUMBER:                   VS21V-03
> 
> SERIAL NUMBER:                  GA00383
> 
> SPEED IN VUPS: 

	As I recall, the CPU in a VAXStation II (same as a
MicroVAX II) could do about 1.1 VUPS (someone check me on
that, please).
                 
> TYPICAL MEMORY: 

	Typical was 4-8. Max is 16. The CPU board has 1MB
onboard RAM which was disabled automatically if the system
had 16 MB of offboard memory installed (two 8-meg modules).
                  
> MAXIMUM INTERNAL DISK SPACE:

	Maximum in what context? The standard disk controller, an
RQDX3, could handle a maximum of two RD54 (170 meg MFM) drives and one
RX50 floppy, or four RDxx series hard drives and no floppies.

	Common drives included the RD52 (about 30 megs), the RD53
(around 85 megs), and the RD54 mentioned above. Since the system used 
a standard DEC Q-22 (Qbus) backplane, it could use any number of ESDI
or SCSI controllers that were MSCP-compatible.

	As far as power supply capacity goes, you're pretty much 
limited to four internal hard drives.
 
> MAXIMUM EXTERNAL DISK SPACE:

	See above. Totally dependent on what interface you use.
With SCSI, you're limited only by the capabilities of the specific
host adapter.
    
> EXTERNAL SCSI DEVICES POSSIBLE:

    Sure, with the right adapter and hardware.
  
> GRAPHICS CAPABILITY:            yes

	Most definitely. The VCB02 graphics subsystem consists
of a single controller board and up to two 4-plane color boards
for a possible maximum of 8-plane color (256 colors total).

> 
> VT TERMINAL AS CONSOLE:

	Yes, with the proper cable.       
> 
> GRAPHICS TERMINAL AS CONSOLE:

	Yes. That's how I work with mine. 
 
> ==============================================================================
> ROUGH DESCRIPTION:              MicroVAX II
> 
> SERIES:                         
> 
> MODEL NUMBER:                   630QY-A3
> 
> SERIAL NUMBER:                  AU80101540
> 
> SPEED IN VUPS:

	Pretty much the same specs as the VAXStation II above,
but minus the graphics capability.

> ==============================================================================
> ROUGH DESCRIPTION:              RD54
> 
> SCSI:

	No. This is strictly an MFM drive. It is
pretty much a rebadged Maxtor XT2190.
                           
> CAPACITY IN MB: 

	170, formatted.
                



