Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation, Palo Alto, CA, USA
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.sysmgt

Martin,

Snapshot Services and Spiralog are completely different products.

You are correct in saying that Snapshot Services uses a container file
in an underlying Files-11 volume, but that is as far as the similarity goes.

Snapshot Services creates an entity known as a storage pool from the space
contributed by the underlying Files-11 volumes that the user has requested
be bound together. The user can then carve up this storage space into
whatever
size chunks of space he/she wishes to form his/her set of virtual disks.
A virtual disk is allocated the amount of storage space requested at
creation
time.

The only time when this is different is when a snapshot is taken of a
particular
virtual disk. When the snapshot of the virtual disk is first created it does
not
consume any space from the storage pool. However, as the virtual disk
changes, the snapshot consumes space from the storage pool. The maximum
amount of space the snapshot can consume is the size of the virtual disk. So
you know the worst case storage space requirements for the snapshot at the
time you create it.

Quite clearly you can construct a storage pool in which a virtual disk is
created that
consumes more than half the available space. If you take a snapshot of that
virtual
disk and then change every block on the virtual disk, all the available
space in the
storage pool will be consumed by the snapshot. However, you can easily
reclaim the space by deleting the snapshot you created.

regards
Kevin Playford
Snapshot Services for OpenVMS Project Lead


