will wrote:

> If anyone has one of these, could you tell me what hard drives (possibly
> where to get them) would fit in place of the floppy drive on an Digital
> Alphastation Multia client 166 MHz. machine, also what type of memory
> does it take? I heard 72 pin SIMM's <70ns buffered + parity, but I also
> heard that the memory doen't have to be buffered. TIA ;-)

The only safe way to upgrade the harddisk internally is by swapping the
current 2.5 inch one with a bigger (capacity..) type 2.5 inch SCSI.. they
are quite rare though.
Maybe you can find one at an Apple dealer (used in apple notebooks).

You could also put in an IDE type 2.5 inch but performance will be
terrible.

I managed to squeeze in an IBM 2 Gb 3.5 inch SCSI just under the floppy
drive, you need to do some 'hacking' on the metal frame though. The 4 pin
connector on the end of the PCI slot board carries 5 and 12 volt but only
at very limited currents. The IBM draws less than 0.2 Amp at 12 volt so
I dared to connect it. (The 12 volt part in the Multia is very very weak)

Cable connecting is the biggest problem when using a 3.5 inch drive, floppy
cable is too short, and you need to make a new cable from drive to
controller to external SCSI-2 connector...

The memory has to be true-parity, aka not created by a logic chip on the
simms.

Gert

