Peter da Silva wrote:
> Scott G. Hall <Scott.Hall@GSC.GTE.Com> wrote:
> >An RJ-11 has four conductors, but six "slots" and so can be plugged into an
> >RJ-12 jack.  An RJ-12 has all six conductors.
> 
> I am *SO* embarassed! You just blew my RJ chops right out of the water.
> 
> (mutter grumble GTE bastards whine snivel)

You should see the "modified" RJ-45E jacks that DigiBoard uses for their
connectors.  These are 10-conductor RJ-45's, where the outer two conductors
are placed on the lip edges that in the other RJ connectors the spec says
that there has to be a minimum space between the outer wall and the outer
conductor.  They have the jacks constructed so that you can plug any of
RJ-45E, RJ-45, RJ-12 or RJ-11 connectors in them and get the corresponding
number of connections.  They lay out their jack wiring like this:

		{   Transmit side   }  {   Receive side   }

                              Tx Gnd    Rx Gnd
				    \  /
			   Tx______  ||  ______Rx
				   \ || /
		        DTR_______  ||||  ________DSR
				  \ |||| /
                    RTS__________  \||||/  ___________CTS
				 \ |||||| /
		RI______________  \||||||/  ______________CD
				\ |||||||| /
				 ||||||||||
				 ^^^^^^^^^^
				|          |
				|  RJ-45E  |
				|          |
				|  RS-232C |

If you plugged in a 4-conductor RJ-11, you get a 3-wire interface to the
terminal (or a RS-422 balanced differential);

if you plugged in a 6-cond. RJ-12, you get minimum hardware handshaking to
allow ttymon or getty to recycle the port if a terminal is turned off;

if you plugged in a 8-cond. RJ-45, you get full hardware handshaking;

and if you plugged in a 10-cond. RJ-45E, you get all of the modem signals
too.  And if you accidentally plugged in a cord connected to a telephone
line, the phone line would be connected to ground, no harm, no foul.

