This is getting absurd.

If nul-terminated string *constants* are a "feature" of the language, then
so are nul-terminated strings.  Denying this is like saying that
ints aren't a part of the language, just int constants.  After all, what
you _do_ with an int variable is outside of the definition of the language.

I know, there's a subtle difference.  int is a data type, but the 
nul-terminated string is not.  

I would claim that the reason there is no nul-terminated string data type
in the language is one of economy.  char * works so well and is so 
flexible that you don't need a data type.  The nul-terminated string may
not enjoy the status of "data type" in C, but I still claim that it
is a "feature" of the language.

The nul-terminated string is intimately linked to the logical expression
semantics (you can do things like "while (*s++) {").  It was thought to
be so integral that the string constant used it.  Also, the entire Standard
Library makes heavy use of it.

I still claim that it's a feature of the language.  You can demonstrate
programs that use other character handling techniques, just like one could
demonstrate alternate integer numeric representations in legal C language
programs without using integral types.

As I said, this is getting absurd.  It's just a semantic question without
an authority to decide it.  I would expect that the Standard is silent on
what constitutes a "feature".  I still think arguing that the nul-terminated 
string is not a "feature", for any reasonable definition of "feature" is
just pointless contentiousness.

