doesn't say that such a method _must_ be provided.  A freestanding
implementation doesn't even have to start the program by calling main().

>What exactly is the difference between hosted and non-hosted environments?

A freestanding implementation, as mentioned above, calls an implementation
defined funtion (not necessarily main) to start the program.  If the program
terminates, the effect is implementation-defined (in embedded systems it may
not be expected that the program ever terminate, of course).

Most significantly, freestanding implementations don't need to have the
standard library -- "any library facilities available to a freestanding
program are implementation-defined".  Similarly, they only have to provide
the headers float.h, limits.h, stdarg.h and stddef.h.

>Even if command line arguments are not supported in non-hosted
>environments, I have the string constants to fall back on for my claim
>that nul-terminated strings are a "feature" of the language.

Right, but of course all the string-handling functions (strlen etc.) are
off-limits.

>So, if you
>attempt to draw the line at hosted/non-hosted => core/extensions you still
>have to allow nul-terminated strings in as part of the core and not just a
>"convention".

This is getting alarmingly close to the notorious "is the library a part of
the language" bust-up we had in comp.lang.c a while ago -- around last
Christmas, IIRC.  It's extremely hard to draw a line which will satisfy most
people, or indeed anyone other than yourself (and believe me, some of us
tried).  The only line which seems to have widespread acceptance is
"standard or non-standard" and that's only because we have a 200-odd page
document to define it.

Cheers,
Richard
--
Richard Stamp, Bournemouth, UK




