>No, nul-terminated string *constants* are. What you do with them after that
>is outside the language definition, 

Not quite.  Quoting from the language definition (the original ANSI version):

    4.1.1 Definitions of terms

    A string is a contiguous sequence of characters terminated by and
    including the first null character.  It is represented by a pointer to
    its initial (lowest addressed) character and its length is the number
    of characters preceding the null character.

>and since sizeof("...") does the right
>thing you can have any character, including a nul, in one.

In a string literal, yes.  In a string, by definition, you can't have
embedded null characters.

>(in fact I recall writing code that involve doubly-nul-terminated string
>constants[2], and in embedded environments I've had occasion to use $ as the
>terminator[3])

CP/M-80 and, later, MS-DOS, used the $ as the string terminator for some
OS system calls.

