>If nul-terminated string *constants* are a "feature" of the language, then
>so are nul-terminated strings.  Denying this is like saying that
>ints aren't a part of the language, just int constants.  After all, what
>you _do_ with an int variable is outside of the definition of the language.

The language defines operations on ints. It defines operations on pointers
to characters. It doesn't define any operations on strings.

This isn't nit-picking[1]. You can define an environment where all string
operations are performed on opaque objects, and string constants are only
used to initialize them by calling a macro "string(foo)", and the compiler is
allowed to optimise "string(foo)" at compile time to a reference to that
opaque object, with no run-time overhead at all. If there were built-in
operations on strings that no longer worked on this opaque object then
that wouldn't be the case.

[1] Well, not entirely. Like, you couldn't redefine strings like this in
    Pascal or Fortran or Modula.

In any case I've obviously let my c-language-pedant skills get rusty... I
messed up on exactly when the winged monkeys are invoked, so I best go meditate
on The Standard again. Reading the Version 6 source code really messes you
up, you know. There's some heavily abusive code in there.

