Jordan Henderson wrote:
> If nul-terminated string *constants* are a "feature" of the language, then
> so are nul-terminated strings.  Denying this is like saying that
> ints aren't a part of the language, just int constants.  After all, what
> you _do_ with an int variable is outside of the definition of the language.

I do not know what the current C standard calls a "string", but in the older
version of C,
"strings" were arrays of single characters. Character constants are not really
an array of characters but rather a pointer to a null terminated string stored
somwehere in the galaxy. The "sizeof" on a character array yields allocated
space, not the string's length. 

char is a data type. string isn't.

You can't redefine the way the C compiler generates string constants, but what
you do with a string array is up to you.

The big question is whether you compiler's implementation allows you to
redefine string handling functions and use your own. If so, then you can
redefine the way a string is stored, and write your own string
functions/macros (such as strlen)  that act on your own way of storing
strings. (As long as you don't use constants).

But should ask: why not name your own copy of sting functions differently
instead of working against the C supplied routines ?



