peter@baileynm.com (Peter da Silva) writes...
}There are disadvantages to pascal-style strings too... for example, they
}have a maximum length of 255 bytes. At the low level C operates, it's hard
}to pick a uniformally perfect format for strings... and at least you can
}work around it this way.
}-- 
}In hoc signo hack, Peter da Silva <peter@baileynm.com>

That depends on the implementation. VAX Pascal allowed a string of any
of the various types (other than "char" which is always 1 byte) to be
up to 65,535 characters. (So says the help from the Pascal installed on
some old VAXstations here, installed in about 1989 as I recall.)

Newer implementaions relax that even more - Delphi, for example, allows
strings to be as large as 4,294,967,295 characters each, since the length is
a 32 bit unsigned integer. (Although Delphi may not fit a strict definition
of Pascal, I suppose, but the newer versions of TurboPascal may have the
same higher limit.)

