In <6t2b74$7j4@web.nmti.com> peter@baileynm.com (Peter da Silva) writes:

>In article <danpop.905217598@news.cern.ch>, Dan Pop <Dan.Pop@cern.ch> wrote:
>>>and you're allowed to initiate
>>>posterior expulsion of avian primates[1] if you see main(int ac, char
>>>**av).
>
>>Nonsense.  You can safely define a function main(int foo, char **bar) in
>>a freestanding environment, it's just that it won't do anything special,
>>unless the documentation of the compiler says otherwise.
>
>Fair enough. If the documentation says that this initiates posterior
>expulsion of avian primates then that's what happens. The C standard doesn't,
>in any case, specify it.

The C standard does not approve such a behaviour.  Defining main(int ac,
char **av) could "initiate posterior expulsion of avian primates" *only*
as a result of undefined behaviour.  But such a definition does NOT
invoke undefined behaviour, so whatever happens has to be compatible
with the definition of the language.

>Other than string literals, though, is there anything in a non-hosted
>environment that deals with strings?

That's up to the implementor.  A non-hosted environment is not required
to implement the whole language specification.

Dan
