>
>No; what I would say that null terminated strings are a feature of your
>MACRO-11 assembly language.

Are you sure you want to say that?  I'd call them a feature of the
PDP-11 instruction set before I called them a feature of MACRO-11, but
as Jordan points out we haven't yet defined "feature".  Personally,
I'd say that a "feature" is something that is consciously designed in
at a given level; if higher-level tools take advantage of that lower-level
feature, I'd call it a "commonly used shortcut".

For example, if in PDP-11 assembler I want to move a string
pointed to by R2 to an area pointed to by R3, I just say:

LOOP:	MOVB (R2)+,(R3)+
	BNE LOOP

If I tried to do this any other way - say by prepending the string length,
or by appending a FF or a '$' - the code gets longer (though not
necessarily slower...)

Not surprisingly, the C code gets longer if you use a different terminator,
too :-)

C's notation *and* commonly used shortcuts are incredibly strongly tied
to the PDP-11 instruction set, addressing modes, and branch conditions.
And these are incredibly strongly tied to minicomputer technology in
the late 60's/early 70's, which is strongly tied to TI's 7400
series logic.  How far back does the "feature" stop and the "commonly
used shortcut" begin?

Tim.



