Peter Watkinson wrote in message <35efcddd.1798500@news.demon.co.uk>...

> I wonder if one of the problems with FX32 is that software vendors
>decide not release native alpha versions of thier software because
>they think -
>"Are we don't need to release Native Alpha NT applications because
>users can use FX32."

No, this has nothing to do with it.  I don't think anyone goes out
and buys software with the intention of running it under FX!32.
It's OK for software that came from your old computer that you
junked when you got an Alpha, or for binary only freeware, but
you get an Alpha for performance and you are not going to pay
more for less performance.  Also it takes some porting effort to
make a big x86 application work under FX!32.  Some things
compilers (even Digital/Compaq's x86 compiler) do cause FX!32 to
throw up it hands, and code containing true bugs may work on x86
processors but the same bugs can cause exceptions when run in an
untested environment (i.e. FX!32).

Software vendors don't give a hoot about anything but the #1
platform and for the most part I think they wish they only had
one platform to support forever and that platform never changed.
Many vendors are even reluctant to support NT-x86.

There is also the problem that you have to sign an NDA with
Intel to find out how to program their chips.  If you aggressively
port to Alpha will Intel be so willing to give you NDA
information in the future?

>However while i appreciate that FX32 is an extremely well thought out
>and produced tool. It does have it's limitations. Obviously if you are
>going to use all of the apllications under FX32 you may as well run a
>pentium instead. However it's the lack of the native less expensive
>but still crucial tools that could be improved by being native. For
>instance a decent newsreader, mail software, picture editor, ftp tool,

What's so bad about Outlook Express, which is what I'm using now?
Microsoft's mail seems OK, too.  The only really weak link seems to
be Dial Up Networking which has big time problems when a connection
is unexpectedly broken (as happens quite often when one lives under
the domination of the terrorist state of US West).  A better FTP
tool would be a good thing, though.

> a few games etc..

There are a few games: Quake 2 (but firing the BFG10K crashes the
program) Crafty+Winboard (but you have to compile these yourself
while working around MSVC++ bugs and run the SPIKE optimizer to
get best performance -- kind of a longwinded procedure).

>Anyone have any comments?


The most shocking software gaps in NT-Alpha are Matlab (their
software doesn't even run under FX!32) and any form of speech-
recognition software (kind of strange when this is one of the
most compute-intensive classes of software an ordinary home user
might use on a daily basis.  Jeez, even soundcard support is
really wimpy: compare the HCL for NT-x86 and NT-Alpha compatibility.)





