Organization: Trident Computer Resources
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

I think this is a known problem with some of the older AlphaStations.  Much
of the writeable values that the firmware uses get zapped.  The date going
to 2046 really does cause any time-limited PAKs to expire in a hurry!  That
was the only thing that clued me in to the problem on a system here.

If you are lucky, just correcting the firmware values back to their original
values will get you going again.  If that isn't sufficient, you might need
to re-run the firmware loader to really get it right again.  Someone might
have a another idea about what to try if that doesn't do it.

This is just another reason to view and record all of your firmware values
on your system when it is healthy so that some day, like this, when it all
goes away, you'll know what to restore it to.  You need to do a SHOW and a
SHOW CONFIG and write it all down.  Actually, you won't need to record it
all if you know what is necessary but unfortunately, you exactly what is.

I never did find out what caused the problem to occur in the first place.
Maybe upgrading to a more recent firmware version might fix it.

If you get to the point that restoring values will get you running, if you
have an LK411 keyboard then you need to do a:

>>> set kbd_hardware_type lk411

to fix the scancode error problem.

I have a SHOW CONFIG from an AlphaStation 200 from July 1995 that shows:

ISA
Slot   Device  Name    Enabled  BaseAddr  IRQ  DMA
0      0       MOUSE   Yes      60        12
       1       KBD     Yes      60        1
       2       COM1    Yes      3f8       4
       3       COM2    Yes      2f8       3
       4       LPT1    Yes      3bc       7
       1       FLOPPY  Yes      3f0       6    2
2
       0       PCXBJ   Yes      530       9    0


