In article <6s6dtu$d34@post.gsfc.nasa.gov>,
Bob Koehler <koehler@bessta.gsfc.nasa.aspm.gov> wrote:
>Sorry, but Mac C compilers are the only ones I've seen which support
>Pascal-format string constants.  ANSI C calls for string constants which
>are null terminated.

But sizeof() gives you the length even if it contains nulls. So you can do
things like this:

char *_string_(char *s, char l)
{
	int i;
	for(i = 0; i<l; i++)
		s[i+1] = s[i];
	s[0] = l;
}

#define PSTRING(s) _string_((s), sizeof(s)-1)

There are disadvantages to pascal-style strings too... for example, they
have a maximum length of 255 bytes. At the low level C operates, it's hard
to pick a uniformally perfect format for strings... and at least you can
work around it this way.

-- 
