I'm perplexed.  What is it exactly from the above are you saying is true 
for a "hosted" implementation, but not in an "embedded" one?  

I infer you are inferring that I was saying that a C compiler had to
write programs that handled command line arguments.  I did not.  I was 
only making statements about how a C implementation _may_ handle
command line arguments.  In an environment where command line 
arguments are typically not provided (device drivers, etc.) the
compiler could write code into the result program that would 
check for the environmental restrictions at run time and provide
command line arguments or not, as appropriate.

Actually, it's more typical that you would have to provide special 
instructions to the compiler/linker/etc. when writing special 
device driver types of programs, but all this detail is outside 
of considerations of what a C compiler can and cannot do in 
ANSI conformant implementations.

>(how else would a device driver satisfy ANSI otherwise)

This is a non-sequitor.  Who was claiming that a C program had to have
command line argument processing to be ANSI conformant?  I was claiming
that if an ANSI conformant C program had command line arguments, they 
would all be nul-terminated strings.

