> docs are out there, but of course they a) reflect the wisdom of the time 
> they were written and b) don't assume your doing things yourself.

A really handy page is the Memory Options Chart found on Prodec's site -
www.prodec.co.uk

Briefly:

VAXstation 3100 M76 - only takes MS44-AA, which are individual 4Mb boards,
up to a maximum of 32Mb.  We have tried swapping these with MS44L-BA
boards from a VS4000/60 (pairs of 4Mb boards) - it worked one way round,
and I cannot remember which.  Otherwise, treat this memory as completely
specific to the model 76s.

VAXstation 4000 60 - only takes MS44L-BA (as above), and MS44-DA options,
which are pairs of 16Mb boards.  A peculiar range of sizes, then, as you
add 8Mb and 32Mb options to the 8Mb on the motherboard, in any combination
up to a maximum of 3 pairs and 104Mb.  This is exactly the same memory as
that fitted to some DECstation 5100s, and more importantly, Microvax 3100s,
models 30/40/80/85.  Quite why the 16Mb and 64Mb options for the MV 3100/90
and /95 and the Vaxstation 4000/90 don't fit, is one of those mysteries -
also MS44 parts.

VAXstation II/GPX - take MS630 boards, unique to this machine and the
Microvax II.  The table says 4Mb and 8Mb boards.

