In article <35EF1532.641DA773@uci.edu>,
Duncan Phillips  <dphillip@uci.edu> wrote:
>We have some old 9-track tapes from a PDP-10 that we need to retrieve
>data from. The tapes have been dumped to cd but I was wondering if
>anyone has a filter to convert ASCII files from PDP-10 format to Unix ?

You didn't specify what OS the 10 was running.  There are utilities on
ftp.dbit.com that can read some of them --

TOPS-10		ftp://ftp.dbit.com/pub/pdp10/tops10/decus/backup.tar
TOPS-20		ftp://ftp.dbit.com/pub/pdp10/tops20/read20/read20.tar
ITS		ftp://ftp.dbit.com/pub/pdp10/its/itstar/

If you don't care about the file structure of the tape and can manually
extract the ASCII text parts, extracting typical PDP-10 ASCII isn't too hard,
each group of five tape frames (i.e. bytes) can be converted to five ASCII
characters as follows:

Input:	aaaaaaaa	(as written by TM03 tape formatter etc.)
	bbbbbbbb
	cccccccc
	dddddddd
	----eeee	(high 4 bits of every 5th byte are dropped)

Yielding 36-bit word:
	aaaaaaaabbbbbbbbccccccccddddddddeeee

Extracting 7-bit bytes starting at the left-hand side:

Output:	0aaaaaaa	(pad with 0 MSB to store as an 8-bit byte)
	0abbbbbb
	0bbccccc
	0cccdddd
	0ddddeee	(N.B. low bit of 36-bit word is dropped)

Just write a quicky program to chew through the CD files 5 bytes at a time
doing the above bit twiddling, and then edit out the garbage from the output
files and you should be done.  Note that PDP-10 software typically stores text
files with lines terminated by CR LF, As God Intended;  if you'll be using
the output on a Weenix system you'll need to strip out CRs that immediately
precede LFs.

John Wilson
D Bit



