On 29 Aug 1998 00:18:21 GMT, mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu wrote in
article <6s7hcd$sgs@gap.cco.caltech.edu>:

> Better would be:
>
>typedef struct
>{
>  unsigned int storage_length;
>  unsigned int length;
>  void *string_ptr; 
>} pascal_string_t;
>
...
>Unfortunately, null terminated strings permeate the libraries, so that you
>would probably have to resort to making all such strings one character
>longer than they would overwise be (storage_length - 1) so that you can pop
>a '\0' onto the end before calling printf (or whatever). 
...

Not necessarily. You could use, for example

printf( "%.*s", string.length, string.string_ptr );

which will only display a maximum of string.length characters. Of
course you then get into the debate of what happens if there is a NULL
in the 'middle' of the string, so you might be better re-writing the
printf() functions with your own to take this behaviour into account.

Greg
