In article <Pine.GSO.3.96.980901144305.19107D-100000@tardis.tardis.ed.ac.uk>, Tim Austwick <tima@tardis.ed.ac.uk> writes:
|> 
|> Which OS can/should I install on these two machines:
|> 
|> 1) DECstation 5000/240, 64megs ram, unknown colour video card, 17" monitor
|> 2) VAXstation 4000 VLC, 16megs ram, monitorless
|> 
|> I want to run X on the DS, as I though the machine should be up to it(?),
|> and the VAX would be more usefull as a unix box (providing ftp/nfs for the
|> network).
|> 
|> So, i think my choices are...
|> 
|> NetBSD, FreeBSD, Ultrix, VMX, Linux.

Choices for the 5000/240 are currently NetBSD or Ultrix.  (There's an
OpenBSD distribution as well, but AFAIK the pmax support is an
out-of-date version of NetBSD).  Linux is apparently coming along, but
then it has been for the last 4 years, and they don't have support for
the IOASIC models like your 3MAXPLUS.

|> .. there may be others

Mach. (but you'd have to build a Unix on top of it from the
4.4bsd-Lite single server); 4.4BSD, if you have a license.
And some academic research kernels.


|> But, are VMS and Ultix free? (this is non-commercial). If not, are they
|> worth paying for? I think BSD on the Vax is a good idea, but i want a fast
|> stable X for DS5000.

I don't know of any hobbyist licence for Ultrix. At this point, I dont
think a 4.3BSD alpha release plus DEC extensions is worth paying for,
unless you really, really need LAT, or DECnet, or TC CI support.


If you want X server support, you need to find out what video card is
on the machine. There is _no_ free X11 support for the PMAG-C, or the
PMAG-D/E/F.  (there is absoultely no info on the hardware accelerators
or the i860 firmware). So if you have one of these cards you'll have
to get Ultrix, or get a replacement card.  Otherwise, NetBSD has
X11R6.3 clients and (for now) an R5 server for other TC video cards.



