David Gray wrote:
> 
> i,
> 
> I would like to be able to go through all files on a disk, that have an eight
> byte numeric extension and delete them.  Iam trying to do this in a loop using
> f$search to find the file, then f$type to check if the extension is an integer.
> 
> My problem is that if I find an extension that is not numeric it needs ignoring
> and the next file checking using f$serach, however f$search will always retrun
> the next file in sequence, ie the one I have just checked.
> 
> This example will probably explain what Iam trying to do better.
> 
> $ FILE_TO_DELETE = UDISK1:<USERS...>*.%%%%%%%%;*
> $
> $START:
> $
> $ SEARCH_FILE = F$SEARCH(FILE_TO_DELETE)
> $ FILE_EXT    = F$ELEMENT(0,";",F$ELEMENT(1,".",SEARCH_FILE))
> $ EXT_TYPE    = F$TYPE(FILE_EXT)
> $
> $ IF EXT_TYPE .EQ. "INTEGER"
> $ THEN
> $   DELETE 'SEARCH_FILE;*
> $ ENDIF
> $
> $ GOTO START
> $
> 
> No problems when file.12345674 is found this is deleted, but also returned
> is file.template which is not deleted so stays on top of the pile, the next
> search finds it again.
> 
> Does anyone have a solution to this problem.
> 
> Regards,
> 
>     David


Change the last lines to:

$   DELETE 'SEARCH_FILE
$ ENDIF
$
$ if search_file .nes. "" then GOTO START

Without a way out of the loop when you are done searching the first
time, you will start another search after deleting all the .123456's. 
You don't want a "next search" - just "next iteration of first search".

The ;* at the end of the DELETE 'SEARCH_FILE yeilds an error.

Bill Henley



