> The key word you seem to have overlooked in the above message is "Rumor".
> 
> To the best of my knowledge, neither "Charlie Matco" nor Terry Shannon
> are now or have ever been employees of Digital or Compaq, nor have
> they ever been hired as official spokespersons for Digital or Compaq.
> Nothing that was printed in their columns was an official committment
> by Digital or Compaq (unless it was a re-print of material that was
> officially issued by Digital or Compaq).

Both Charlie and I completely concur with the above.

> 
> As far as past columns go: sometimes Terry has turned out to be
> correct; and sometimes his predictions have been very far from
> what was planned, or what actually happened.
> 

S**t happens. Or doesn't happen, as the case may be!

> In the past, Digital generally has not made any official committments
> to deliver products until they were fairly certain the committment
> could be met.  I believe that if you go back and check the record
> (using official Digital releases), you will find that Digital has
> a very good record of delivering what it promised, and not announcing
> 'vaporware'.  Keep in mind, however, that changing economic conditions,
> changing officiating body's standards, and occasional cases where
> technology proved more difficult to develop than anyone could forsee,
> have caused delays in delivery.  But on the whole, I believe Digital's
> record is good.

Agreed. Maybe too good for the firm's own good. That said, Digital
during the Palmer Era certainly opened the kimono more than was common
during Ken's stewardship of the company.

Terry Shannon
Who is never seen at the same time in the same place as Charlie Matco





