C'est vrai. However, during the June 3, 1994 Merced press conference,
representatives of Intel and HP projected the availability of the Chip
Formerly Known as P7 in 1997.

At the end of the day, the delays aren't all that big a deal to Intel,
who is making money hand over fist. Developing a brand new architecture
AND the co-requisite compiler and OS technology is a Hurculean
undertaking.

My issue with IA64 (which undoubtedly will be quite successful when it
comes out) is the seemingly-universal belief that the chip will take
over the world. I would (and do) argue that it's way early to write off
any alternative chip technology that offers **significantly** better
price/performance (as well as an adequate apps portfolio) than does
IA64.

I would not write off Alpha, nor, at this juncture, UltraSparc.

Terry Shannon





