Jeffrey J. Potoff wrote:
> 
> Terry C. Shannon wrote:
> >
> <snippity snip>
> 
> >
> > As for the Monet problem, it's all in the card edge connector. Some
> > supplier is gonna get taken to the woodshed (or to Singapore) for a
> > meeting with that old soldier Corporal Punishment.
> 
> Does this mean we'll see _other_ 21264 based systems soon or are all
> of them delayed ?

YES and NO

YES, we will see other 21264-based systems later this Fall. Maybe right
after (US congress) Election Day.

NO, all of them are not delayed. I anticipate low-end and midrange
Compaq-branded AlphaServers will ship on whatever schedule CPQ has
established. 575MHz EV6 chips have been running internally for quite
some time and now are out in field test. I believe this to be the case
with other speed bins as well.


> 
> >
> > The 21264 itself is just fine and dandy. Still, the sooner it shows up
> > in shipping products (low-end and midrange EV6 servers are out and about
> > at field test sites and have been for some time now), the sooner the
> > comments from the peanut gallery will be muffled.
> 
> You got it.  I really, really, want to buy a bunch of these 21264 Alphas
> to get my research done, but they keep getting delayed.  It gets a bit
> frustrating after a while, like having someone dangle a carrot in front
> of a horse...

Or an intern in front of a p... (ummm, never mind!). 

As I said when I lobbed the first grenade in this thread, EV6 is way
late. First time Alpha has missed the shipment mark by a year. That's
the bad news. The good news is that the CMOS7 shrink and the late-1999
debut of EV7 (21364) are in no way impacted by the EV6 problems.

Which means that the time between EV6 and EV7 will be far less than the
time between EV5 and EV6.

More details on the foregoing are in the latest issue of my newsletter,
which already is in the hands of my subscribers.

Terry Shannon
Consultant and Publisher, Shannon Knows Distributed Enterprise Computing





